, we have that $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{EF}$ and $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AD}$ are parallel. Therefore, $\\angle{BCD}$ and $\\angle{CBE}$ are congruent by the alternate interior angles theorem.\n3. From row 2, we have that $\\angle{BCD}$ and $\\angle{CBE}$ are congruent. Therefore, by the definition of congruent angles, $m\\angle{BCD} = m\\angle{CBE}$.\n4. Note that the segment $\\overline{AB}$ divides the angle $\\angle{CBE}$ into consecutive angles $\\angle{ABE}$ and $\\angle{ABC}$. Therefore, by the angle addition postulate, we must have\n$m\\angle{CBE} = m\\angle{ABE} + m\\angle{ABC}$.\n5. From row 1, we have that $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{EF}$ and $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AD}$ are parallel. Therefore, $\\angle{ABE}$ and $\\angle{A}$ are congruent by the alternate interior angles theorem.\n6. From row 5, $\\angle{ABE}$ and $\\angle{A}$ are congruent. Therefore, by the definition of congruent angles, $m\\angle{ABE} = m\\angle{A}$.\n7. From row 6, $m\\angle{ABE} = m\\angle{A}$. Substituting this into the equation in row 4, we have\n$m\\angle{CBE} = m\\angle{A} + m\\angle{ABC}$.\n8. From rows 3 and 7, we have\n$m\\angle{BCD} = m\\angle{CBE}, \\qquad m\\angle{CBE} = m\\angle{A} + m\\angle{ABC}$.\nTherefore, by the transitive property of equality, $m\\angle{BCD} = m\\angle{A} + m\\angle{ABC}$." 13194139533350 17592186045417 true] [17592186073586 1074 "By the converse of the perpendicular bisector theorem, a point equidistant from the endpoints of a segment lies on the perpendicular bisector of the segment. \n\nHere, the points $D$ and $E$ are both equidistant from the endpoints of $\\overline{AB}.$ Therefore, $D$ and $E$ both  lie on the perpendicular bisector of $\\overline{AB}.$ \n\nSince only one line can be drawn through two given points, the line $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{DE}$ is the perpendicular bisector of $\\overline{AB}.$ \n\nIn particular, we have that $AC=BC.$ We can use this equation to solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =BC \\\\ 3x+2 & =5x-2 \\\\ 4 & =2x \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186073608 1074 "Let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. The measure of the minor arc $\\overset{\\frown}{AB}$ is equal to the measure of its central angle,\n$m\\overset{\\frown}{AB} = m\\angle{AOB}=120^\\circ$.\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. The arc $\\overset{\\frown}{ACB}$ represents the major arc of the circle, so it has the measure\n$m\\overset{\\frown}{ACB} = 360^\\circ - m\\angle{AOB} =360^\\circ - 120^\\circ =240^\\circ$.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true." 13194139533350 17592186045417 true] [17592186073609 1074 "When an arc notation contains only two letters, it is always assumed to be a minor arc.\n\nSo here, $\\overset{\\frown}{BC}$ represents the minor arc of the circle with the central angle $\\angle{BOC}$.\n\nTherefore, its measure is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nmBC^(⌢) &= m∠BOC \\\\\n&= m∠AOB + m∠AOC \\\\\n&= 90^{∘} + 45^{∘} \\\\\n&= 135^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533350 17592186045417 true] [17592186073611 1074 "From the diagram,\n\n$$\nm\\angle{EOD} = m\\angle{AOF} = 50^\\circ\n$$\n\nSince the measures of $\\angle{AOF}$, $\\angle{FOE}$ and $\\angle{EOD}$, add up to $180^\\circ$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠AOF + m∠FOE + m∠EOD &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n50^{∘} + m∠FOE + 50^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n100^{∘} + m∠FOE &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠FOE &= 80^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the angles $\\angle{FOE}$ and $\\angle{BOC}$ are vertical, we obtain\n\n$$\nm\\angle{BOC} = m\\angle{FOE} = 80^\\circ\n$$\n\nFinally, since the measure of the arc $\\overset{\\frown}{BC}$ is equal to the measure of the central angle $\\angle{BOC}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nBC^(⌢) &= m∠BOC \\\\\n3x - 10^{∘} &= m∠BOC \\\\\n3x - 10^{∘} &= 80^{∘} \\\\\n3x &= 90^{∘} \\\\\nx &= 30^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533350 17592186045417 true] [17592186073612 1074 "The formula for the area of a circle is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}=\\pi r^2\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the radius. Using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A = 81\\pi\\,\\textrm{ft}^2\n$$\n\nwe can solve for $r$, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle \\mathcal{A} & \\displaystyle  = \\pi  {r}^{2} \\\\ \\displaystyle 81 \\pi  & \\displaystyle  = \\pi  {r}^{2} \\\\ \\displaystyle 81 \\cancel{\\pi} & \\displaystyle  = \\cancel{\\pi} {r}^{2} \\\\ \\displaystyle 81 & \\displaystyle  = {r}^{2} \\\\ \\displaystyle r & \\displaystyle  = 9\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative value since the radius can't be negative.\n\nTherefore, the circumference is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC &= 2πr \\\\\n&= 2π \\cdot 9 \\\\\n&= 18π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the ratio of the length of $\\overset{\\frown}{AB}$ to the circumference $C$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4\\pi}{18\\pi} = \\dfrac{2}{9}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sector is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{9}\n$$\n\nof the whole circle, and we obtain\n\n$$\nm\\angle{AOB} = 360^\\circ \\cdot\\dfrac{2}{9} = 80^\\circ\n$$" 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186073614 1074 "First, we will find the fraction of the circle's area that corresponds to the shaded region. This is the same as the ratio of the arc length of the shaded region to the full circumference of the circle.\n\nFirst, let's find the circumference of the circle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC &= 2πr \\\\\n&= 2π \\cdot 4 \\\\\n&= 8πcm\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the length of the arc $\\overset{\\frown}{AB}$ is $\\pi \\,\\textrm{cm}$, so the ratio of the length of $\\overset{\\frown}{AB}$ to the circumference $C$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{8\\pi} = \\dfrac 18\n$$\n\nThis means the central angle $\\angle{AOB}$ corresponding to the arc $\\overset{\\frown}{AB}$ covers\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{8}\n$$\n\nof a full turn, which means that the area $\\mathcal A_S$ of our sector covers\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{8}\n$$\n\nof the area of the entire circle.\n\nTherefore, we can compute the area of the sector ${AOB}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{S} &= \\frac{1}{8}πr^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{8}π \\cdot 4^{2} \\\\\n&= 2πcm^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533350 17592186045417 true] [17592186073615 1074 "The central angle corresponding to the arc $\\overset{\\frown}{AB}$ is $\\angle{AHB}$. Notice that\n\n$$\nk= \\dfrac{90^\\circ}{360^\\circ} = \\dfrac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ represents the proportion of a full circle corresponding to $\\angle AHB$.\n\nNow, from the problem statement, we can deduce that the length of the inner radius $\\overline{HF}$ is\n\n$$\nHF = 80-60 = 20 \\, \\textrm{yd}\n$$\n\nLet's find the area of the inner sector (the part that corresponds to the infield):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{inner} &= k \\cdot πr^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4} \\cdot π \\cdot 20^{2} \\\\\n&= 100πyd^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we find the area of the outer sector:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A}_{\\textrm{outer}} = \\dfrac 1{4} \\cdot \\pi \\cdot 80^2 = 1\\, 600\\pi \\, \\textrm{yd}^2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total area of the region that makes up the outfield is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{shaded} &= A_{outer} - A_{inner} \\\\\n&= 1600π - 100π \\\\\n&= 1500πyd^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533350 17592186045417 true] [17592186073628 1074 "From the picture,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nmDAB^(⌢) &= mDA^(⌢) + mAB^(⌢) \\\\\n&= 70^{∘} + 80^{∘} \\\\\n&= 150^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\angle{BCD}$ is an inscribed angle that intercepts the arc $\\overset{\\frown}{DAB}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠BCD &= \\frac{1}{2}mDAB^(⌢) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 150^{∘} \\\\\n&= 75^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that $ABCD$ is a quadrilateral inscribed in a circle. So, its opposite angles are supplementary.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠BAD + m∠BCD &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠BAD + 75^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠BAD &= 105^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533350 17592186045417 true] [17592186073630 1074 "Since $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{PA}$ and $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{PB}$ are tangent to the circle, we have that\n\n- $m\\angle{OAP}=m\\angle{OBP}=90^\\circ$, and\n- $\\overline{OA} \\cong \\overline{OB}$ are radii of the circle.\n\n![](images/21/21a2e5a5f962fa6a1807f825713405b5fce804ed999be594c9b98742fdbafb4b.png)\n\nTherefore, $\\triangle{OAP}$ and $\\triangle{OBP}$ are congruent by HL (Hypotenuse-Leg):\n$\\triangle OAP \\cong \\triangle OBP$.\n\nAs a consequence, the tangent segments $\\overline{PB}$ and $\\overline{PA}$ are congruent, and we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nPB &= PA \\\\\n5z + 2 &= 12 \\\\\n5z &= 10 \\\\\nz &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533350 17592186045417 true] [17592186073631 1074 "First, notice that\n\n$$\nCA_1 = CB - BA_1 = 12 - 5 = 7\\,\\textrm{cm}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB}$, $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{BC}$, and $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AC}$ are tangent to the circle, then we get three pairs of congruent tangent segments:\n$AB_1 \\cong AC_1, \\quad BA_1 \\cong BC_1, \\quad CA_1 \\cong CB_1$.\n\n![](images/70/701bfddfb3f7c64c971227efd6ea41dbe176e9f6240d1c0fe095c9eb15a3ddfd.png)\n\nAs a consequence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= AC_{1} + BC_{1} \\\\\n&= 11 + 5 \\\\\n&= 16cm, \\\\\nAC &= AB_{1} + CB_{1} \\\\\n&= 11 + 7 \\\\\n&= 18cm\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the required perimeter is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np &= AB + BC + CA \\\\\n&= 16 + 12 + 18 \\\\\n&= 46cm\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533350 17592186045417 true] [17592186073632 1074 "Since $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{BC}$ and $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{BD}$ are tangent to the circle, then\n\n- $m\\angle{OCB}=m\\angle{ODB}=90^\\circ$, and\n- $\\overline{OC} \\cong \\overline{OD}$ are radii of the circle.\n\nTherefore, $\\triangle{OCB}$ and $\\triangle{ODB}$ are congruent by HL (Hypotenuse-Leg).\n\n![](images/87/875f6e33812c9f6f2a5356ee2c9723f8f3d9fccdc79ab5960cb339e4bd1e753e.png)\n\nAs a consequence, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠DBO &= m∠CBO \\\\\n&= \\frac{m∠DBC}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{60^{∘}}{2} \\\\\n&= 30^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\triangle{ODB}$ is a right $30^\\circ$-$60^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangle, and therefore its hypotenuse is twice the length of its shortest leg:\n\n$$\nOB = 2 \\cdot OD = 2 \\cdot 3 = 6 \\,\\textrm{cm}\n$$" 13194139533350 17592186045417 true] [17592186073633 1074 "Let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. The angle $\\angle ACB$ intercepts the arc $\\overset{\\frown}{AB}$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/4d/4dc2c677de8b1478402014383edd0360a471414f9384c8523494d3541285874e.png)\n\n- Statement II is false. The angle $\\angle{AOC}$ is a central angle of the circle, not an inscribed angle.\n- Statement III is true. Notice that $\\angle{ACB}$ is formed by two chords, and its vertex lies on the circumference. Hence, $\\angle{ACB}$ is an inscribed angle.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533350 17592186045417 true] [17592186073634 1074 "Since we're told that\n\n$$\nm\\overset{\\frown}{CD}= 110^\\circ,\\,\n$$\n\nwe can add this information to the diagram:\n\n![](images/45/452aacbec53807737e83d54045f8ab963d0505ab5ac55dcc341870f0456698fe.png)\n\nAccording to the inscribed angle theorem, the measure of an inscribed angle is half the measure of its intercepted arc. So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠CED &= \\frac{1}{2}mCD^(⌢) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 110^{∘} \\\\\n&= 55^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533350 17592186045417 true] [17592186073654 1074 "Since the inscribed angles $\\angle FDC$ and $\\angle FEC$ intercept the same arc $\\overset{\\frown}{FC}$, their measures are the same.\n\n![](images/da/da658aa1307393d1539708966b98fdb16ad47e76817a6a50602c4e2dc3db233a.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠FDC &= m∠FEC \\\\\n3x + 10^{∘} &= 40^{∘} \\\\\n3x &= 30^{∘} \\\\\nx &= 10^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533350 17592186045417 true] [17592186078143 1074 "Notice that $\\angle BAC$ is a secant-secant angle to the given circle whose vertex lies inside the circle. Thus, its measure is the average of the measures of the intercepted arcs.\n\n$$\nm\\angle BAC = \\dfrac{m\\overset{\\frown}{BC}+m\\overset{\\frown}{DE}}{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given expressions and solving for $x$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n95^{∘} &= ((5x - 5^{∘}) + (4x - 3^{∘}))/(2) \\\\\n95^{∘} &= \\frac{9x - 8^{∘}}{2} \\\\\n190^{∘} &= 9x - 8^{∘} \\\\\n198^{∘} &= 9x \\\\\nx &= 22^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533351 17592186045417 true] [17592186078144 1074 "Notice that $\\angle A$ is a secant-secant angle to the given circle whose vertex lies outside the circle. Thus, its measure is one-half the difference between the measures of the intercepted arcs.\n\n$$\nm\\angle A = \\dfrac{m\\overset{\\frown}{DE}-m\\overset{\\frown}{BC}}{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values and solving for $m\\overset{\\frown}{BC}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n30^{∘} &= (75^{∘} - mBC^(⌢))/(2) \\\\\n60^{∘} &= 75^{∘} - mBC^(⌢) \\\\\nmBC^(⌢) &= 75^{∘} - 60^{∘} \\\\\nmBC^(⌢) &= 15^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533351 17592186045417 true] [17592186078146 1074 "We know that\n\n$$\nm\\overset{\\frown}{AB} + m\\overset{\\frown}{BC} + m\\overset{\\frown}{AC} = 360^\\circ\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given expressions and solving for $x$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n78^{∘} + (x - 20^{∘}) + (3x - 10^{∘}) &= 360^{∘} \\\\\n4x + 48^{∘} &= 360^{∘} \\\\\n4x &= 312^{∘} \\\\\nx &= 78^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\angle{P}$ is a secant-tangent angle to the given circle. Thus, its measure is one-half the difference between the measures of the intercepted arcs.\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠P &= (mAC^(⌢) - mBC^(⌢))/(2) \\\\\n&= ((3x - 10^{∘}) - (x - 20^{∘}))/(2) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2x + 10^{∘}}{2} \\\\\n&= x + 5^{∘} \\\\\n&= 78^{∘} + 5^{∘} \\\\\n&= 83^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533351 17592186045417 true] [17592186078210 1074 "Notice that $\\angle A$ is a secant-secant angle to the given circle whose vertex lies inside the circle. Thus, its measure is the average of the measures of the intercepted arcs.\n\n$$\nm\\angle A = \\dfrac{m\\overset{\\frown}{DE}+m\\overset{\\frown}{BC}}{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values and solving for $m\\overset{\\frown}{DE}$, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n45^{∘} &= (mDE^(⌢) + 65^{∘})/(2) \\\\\n90^{∘} &= mDE^(⌢) + 65^{∘} \\\\\nmDE^(⌢) &= 90^{∘} - 65^{∘} \\\\\n&= 25^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533351 17592186045417 true] [17592186078211 1074 "We know that\n\n$$\nm\\overset{\\frown}{BC} + m\\overset{\\frown}{CD} + m\\overset{\\frown}{BD} = 360^\\circ\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values and solving for $m\\overset{\\frown}{BC}$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nmBC^(⌢) + 160^{∘} + 140^{∘} &= 360^{∘} \\\\\nmBC^(⌢) + 300^{∘} &= 360^{∘} \\\\\nmBC^(⌢) &= 60^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\angle A$ is a secant-tangent angle to the given circle. Thus, its measure is one-half the difference between the measures of the intercepted arcs.\n\n$$\nm\\angle A = \\dfrac{m\\overset{\\frown}{CD}-m\\overset{\\frown}{BC}}{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠A &= \\frac{160^{∘} - 60^{∘}}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{100^{∘}}{2} \\\\\n&= 50^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533351 17592186045417 true] [17592186078244 1074 "We first find the ratio between the central angle and the full turn of $360^\\circ$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{m∠AOB}{360^{∘}} = \\frac{40^{∘}}{360^{∘}} = \\frac{1}{9}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc $\\overset{\\frown}{AB}$ represents\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{9}\n$$\n\nof the circumference. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{9} \\cdot C &= 2π \\\\\nC &= 18π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the circumference, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC &= 2πr \\\\\n18π &= 2πr \\\\\nr &= 9cm\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of the circle is\n\n$$\nr = 9\\,\\mathrm{cm}\n$$" 13194139533351 17592186045417 true] [17592186078245 1074 "First, let's find the circumference of the circle:\n\n$$\nC=2\\pi r = 2 \\pi \\cdot \\dfrac 34 = \\dfrac{3 \\pi}2 \\,\\textrm{in}\n$$\n\nSo, the ratio of the length of $\\overset{\\frown}{AB}$ to the circumference $C$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{m\\overset{\\frown}{AB}}{C} = \\dfrac{\\,\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)\\,}{\\,\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right)\\,} = \\dfrac{2}{9}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the central angle $\\angle{AOB}$ must also cover\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{9}\n$$\n\nof a full turn. So, its measure is\n\n$$\nm\\angle{AOB} = \\dfrac{2}{9} \\cdot 360^\\circ = 80^\\circ\n$$" 13194139533351 17592186045417 true] [17592186078246 1074 "The corresponding central angle of the arc $\\overset{\\frown}{AB}$ is $\\angle{AOB}$, and the portion of a full turn that $\\angle AOB$ covers is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{m\\angle{AOB}}{360^\\circ} = \\dfrac{60^\\circ}{360^\\circ} = \\dfrac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nSo, if $C$ is the circumference of the circle, then the length of the arc $\\overset{\\frown}{AB}$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{C}{6}\n$$\n\nThe perimeter of the sector $AOB$ is given by the length of the arc plus the two radii.\n\nTherefore, if $r$ denotes the radius of the circle, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{C}{6} + 2r &= π + 6 \\\\\n\\frac{2πr}{6} + 2r &= π + 6 \\\\\n\\frac{πr}{3} + 2r &= π + 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply both sides by $3$ to get rid of the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3 \\cdot (\\frac{πr}{3} + 2r) &= 3 \\cdot (π + 6) \\\\\nπr + 6r &= 3π + 18\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor out the linear expressions and cancel out the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle \\pi  r + 6 r & \\displaystyle  = 3 \\pi  + 18 \\\\ \\displaystyle r ( \\pi  + 6 ) & \\displaystyle  = 3 ( \\pi  + 6 ) \\\\ \\displaystyle r \\cancel{( \\pi  + 6 )} & \\displaystyle  = 3 \\cancel{( \\pi  + 6 )} \\\\ \\displaystyle r & \\displaystyle  = 3\\end{array}}\n$$" 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186078272 1074 "Let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB}$ is a secant line since it intersects the circle at two points, $B$ and $C$.\n- Statement II is true. Note that $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AD}$ touches the circle only at point $D$. Moreover, the radius $\\overline{OD}$ is perpendicular to $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AD}$. So, $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AD}$ is a tangent line.\n- Statement III is false. Here, $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{EO}$ is not a tangent since it intersects the circle at two points, $D$ and $E$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139533351 17592186045417 true] [17592186078273 1074 "Since $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{BC}$ is tangent to the circle, we have\n\n$$\nm\\angle OBC=90^\\circ\n$$\n\n![](images/e3/e3353d30fd7bb03a56013092bbfb39deaec5626e9356a34c1fe0412f55e30dd3.png)\n\nTherefore, $\\triangle{OBC}$ is a right triangle, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠O + m∠C &= 90^{∘} \\\\\n65^{∘} + (2x - 5^{∘}) &= 90^{∘} \\\\\n60^{∘} + 2x &= 90^{∘} \\\\\n2x &= 30^{∘} \\\\\nx &= 15^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533351 17592186045417 true] [17592186078274 1074 "Since $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{HP}$ is tangent to the circle at $H$, we have\n\n$$\nm\\angle{OHP=90^\\circ}\n$$\n\nand $\\overline{OH}$ is a radius of the circle.\n\n![](images/ec/ecc62ba7c3f84ffb2155c3b401cb6bf035624bba41b23252805c826f6955bb60.png)\n\nConsequently, $\\triangle{OHP}$ is a right triangle, and we can use the Pythagorean theorem to solve for $OH{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nOH &= \\sqrt{OP^{2} - HP^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{13^{2} - 12^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{169 - 144} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{25} \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of the radius of the circle is $5$." 13194139533351 17592186045417 true] [17592186078354 1074 "We first find the ratio between the central angle and a full $360^\\circ$ turn:\n\n$$\n\\frac{m∠AOB}{360^{∘}} = \\frac{120^{∘}}{360^{∘}} = \\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc $\\overset{\\frown}{AB}$ represents\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nof the circumference $C$. So, its length is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac13\\cdot C\n$$\n\nand using the fact that $C=2\\pi r$ and\n\n$$\nr=3\\,\\textrm{ft}\n$$\n\nwe can calculate the length of $\\overset{\\frown}{AB}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{1}{3} \\cdot C &= \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot 2πr \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot 2π \\cdot 3 \\\\\n&= 2πft\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of the arc $\\overset{\\frown}{AB}$ is $2\\pi \\,\\textrm{ft}$." 13194139533351 17592186045417 true] [17592186078380 1074 "Here, $\\angle{AOB}$ is the central angle that intercepts the minor arc $\\overset{\\frown}{AB}$. So the measure of the major arc (that corresponds to our sector) is\n\n$$\n360^\\circ - m\\angle{AOB} = 360^\\circ - 120^\\circ = 240^\\circ\n$$\n\nNote that the ratio of the measure of the major arc to a full circle turn is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{240^\\circ}{360^\\circ} = \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if the area of the circle is $\\mathcal{A}$, then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{A_{S}}{A} &= \\frac{2}{3} \\\\\n\\frac{20}{A} &= \\frac{2}{3} \\\\\nA &= \\frac{20 \\cdot 3}{2} \\\\\nA &= 30cm^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533351 17592186045417 true] [17592186078725 1074 "For an angle $\\theta $ in a right triangle, the trigonometric ratios are as follows:\n\n- $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{adjacent leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{adjacent leg}}$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the given triangle.\n\n![](images/38/38dcba98867c158c671b35ec033fa1ec55c8cedc67466ebe981de9fbbae314e9.png)\n\nNotice that we are given the length of the leg $\\overset{―}{LM}$ opposite to the given angle $∠N$ and wish to find the length of the leg $\\overset{―}{LN}$ adjacent$∠N.$\n\nThe trigonometric ratio that relates the opposite leg to the adjacent leg is *tangent*. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(m∠N) & =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{adjacent leg}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡({37}^{∘}) & =\\frac{8}{LN} \\\\ LN\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡({37}^{∘}) & =8 \\\\ LN & =\\frac{8}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡({37}^{∘})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator to evaluate the trigonometric ratio, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}LN & =\\frac{8}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡({37}^{∘})} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{(0.753\\,55…)} \\\\  & =10.616\\,35… \\\\  & ≈11,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." 13194139535975 17592186045417 true] [17592186079115 1074 "For an angle $\\theta $ in a right triangle, the trigonometric ratios are as follows:\n\n- $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{adjacent leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{adjacent leg}}$\n\nFirst, note that we are given the length of the hypotenuse $\\overset{―}{AC}$ and wish to find the length of the leg $\\overset{―}{AB}$ opposite the given angle $∠C.$\n\nThe trigonometric ratio that relates the opposite leg to the hypotenuse is *sine*. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(m∠C) & =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡({52}^{∘}) & =\\frac{AB}{15} \\\\ AB & =15\\operatorname{sin}⁡({52}^{∘}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator to evaluate the trigonometric ratio, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =15\\operatorname{sin}⁡({52}^{∘}) \\\\  & =15(0.788\\,01…) \\\\  & =11.820\\,16… \\\\  & ≈11.8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." 13194139535582 17592186045417 true] [17592186083026 1074 "The $z$-score of an observation $x$ is defined by\n\n$$\nz = \\frac{x-\\overline{x}}{\\sigma}.\n$$\n\nThe $z$-score represents how many standard deviations an observation is above or below the mean of the data set.\n\nHere, we're told that the mean is $\\overline{x}=15,$ and we can calculate the standard deviation as\n\n$$\n\\sigma = \\sqrt{\\sigma^2} = \\sqrt{3.25}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $z$-score of the observation $x=17.3$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{x-\\overset{―}{x}}{\\sigma } \\\\  & ≈\\frac{17.3-15}{\\sqrt{3.25}} \\\\  & ≈1.28\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $2$ decimal places." 13194139533391 17592186045417 true] [17592186083027 1074 "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overline{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sigma,$ we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx = \\overline{x} + z \\cdot \\sigma\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=13.2,$ $z=-1.5,$ and $\\sigma=1.2$ into the formula and solving for $\\overline{x}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 13.2 & =\\overset{―}{x}+(-1.5)\\cdot 1.2 \\\\ 13.2 & =\\overset{―}{x}-1.8 \\\\ \\overset{―}{x} & =13.2+1.8 \\\\ \\overset{―}{x} & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045417 true] [17592186083086 1074 "The given repeating decimal can be written as \n\n$$\n0.\\overline{23} = 0.23 + 0.002\\,3 + 0.000\\,023 + \\dots\n$$\n\n$$\n0.\\overline{23} = \\dfrac{23}{100} + \\dfrac{23}{10\\,000} + \\dfrac{23}{1\\,000\\,000}+\\dots\n$$\n\nwhere the first term is $a_1 = \\dfrac{23}{100}$ and the common ratio is $\\dfrac{1}{100}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.\\overset{―}{23} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{23}{100}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }23\\cdot \\frac{1}{100}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }23\\cdot \\frac{1}{100}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{100})}^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }23{(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186083087 1074 "The given repeating decimal can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.1\\overset{―}{4} & =0.144\\,4... \\\\  & =0.1+0.04+0.004+0.000\\,4+… \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}+(\\frac{4}{100}+\\frac{4}{1000}+\\frac{4}{10\\,000}+…).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series in the parentheses is a geometric series with the first term $a_1 = \\dfrac{4}{100}$ and the common ratio $r=\\dfrac{1}{10}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.1\\overset{―}{4} & =\\frac{1}{10}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{100}{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4\\cdot 10}{100}{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{n} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{10}{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186083124 1074 "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overline{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sigma,$ we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx = \\overline{x} + z \\cdot \\sigma\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=184,$ $\\overline{x}=178,$ and $z=1.5$ into the formula and solving for $\\sigma,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 184 & =178+1.5\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 6 & =1.5\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{6}{1.5} \\\\ \\sigma  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the standard deviation of the players heights is $4\\,\\textrm{cm}.$" 13194139533393 17592186045417 true] [17592186083125 1074 "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overline{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sigma,$ we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx = \\overline{x} + z \\cdot \\sigma\n$$\n\nFrom the first observation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}18 & =\\overset{¯}{x}+2.8\\cdot \\sigma .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second observation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =\\overset{¯}{x}+1.6\\cdot \\sigma .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the second equation from the first and solving for $\\sigma,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(18-12) & =(\\overset{¯}{x}-\\overset{¯}{x})+(2.8-1.6)\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 6 & =1.2\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ \\sigma  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this value back into our first equation and solving for $\\bar{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}18 & =\\overset{¯}{x}+2.8\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 18 & =\\overset{¯}{x}+2.8\\cdot 5 \\\\ \\overset{¯}{x} & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045417 true] [17592186083691 1074 "The given repeating decimal can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7.8\\overset{―}{7} & =7.877\\,7... \\\\  & =7.8+0.07+0.007+0.000\\,7+… \\\\  & =7.8+(\\frac{7}{100}+\\frac{7}{1\\,000}+\\frac{7}{10\\,000}+…).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series in the parentheses is a geometric series with first term ${a}_{1}=\\frac{7}{100}$ and common ratio $r=\\frac{1}{10}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7.8\\overset{―}{7} & =7.8+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =7.8+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{7}{100}{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{n-1} \\\\  & =7.8+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{7\\cdot 10}{100}{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{n} \\\\  & =7.8+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{7}{10}{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535822 17592186045417 true] [17592186087538 1074 "*Method 1*\n\nWhen we add a complex number and its conjugate, the result is always twice the real part of the complex number.\n\nFor the given complex number $z=7-3\\textrm{i},$ the real part is $\\operatorname{Re}(z) = 7,$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+\\overset{―}{z} & =2\\text{Re}(z) \\\\  & =2(7) \\\\  & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Method 2*\n\nAlternatively, we can actually carry out the process of adding the complex number and its conjugate.\n\nTo obtain the conjugate of the complex number $z=7-3\\textrm{i},$ we flip the sign of the imaginary part and get $\\overline{z} = 7 + 3 \\textrm{i}.$\n\nAdding, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+\\overset{―}{z} & =(7-3\\text{i})+(7+3\\text{i}) \\\\  & =(7+7)+(-3\\text{i}+3\\text{i}) \\\\  & =14+\\text{i}0 \\\\  & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045417 true] [17592186087539 1074 "*Method 1*\n\nWhen we multiply a complex number and its conjugate, the result is always the sum of squares of the real part and the imaginary part.\n\nFor the given complex number $z=7-4\\textrm{i},$ the real part is $\\textrm{Re}(z)=7,$ and the imaginary part is $\\textrm{Im}(z)=-4,$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z\\overset{―}{z} & =(\\text{Re}(z))^{2}+(\\text{Im}(z))^{2} \\\\  & ={7}^{2}+(-4)^{2} \\\\  & =49+16 \\\\  & =65.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Method 2*\n\nAlternatively, we can actually carry out the process of multiplying the complex number and its conjugate.\n\nTo obtain the conjugate of the complex number $z=7-4\\textrm{i},$ we flip the sign of the imaginary part and get $\\overline{z} = 7 + 4 \\textrm{i}.$\n\nMultiplying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z\\overset{―}{z} & =(7-4\\text{i})(7+4\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={7}^{2}+(7)(4)\\text{i}+(-4)(7)\\text{i}-(4)^{2}{\\text{i}}^{2} \\\\  & =49+28\\text{i}-28\\text{i}-(16)(-1) \\\\  & =49+16 \\\\  & =65.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045417 true] [17592186088033 1074 "To find the complex conjugate, we flip the sign of the imaginary part.\n\nIn the given number $z=2-3\\text{i},$ the imaginary part is $-3\\text{i}.$ Flipping the sign, we get $3\\text{i}.$\n\nTherefore, the complex conjugate is given by $\\overset{―}{z}=2+3\\text{i}.$" 13194139533409 17592186045417 true] [17592186088035 1074 "To find the complex conjugate, we flip the sign of the imaginary part.\n\nIn the given number $z=-2+4\\text{i},$ the imaginary part is $4\\text{i}.$ Flipping the sign, we get $-4\\text{i}.$\n\nTherefore, the complex conjugate is given by $\\overset{―}{z}=-2-4\\text{i}.$" 13194139533409 17592186045417 true] [17592186088036 1074 "To find the complex conjugate, we flip the sign of the imaginary part.\n\nIn the given number $z=-4\\text{i},$ the imaginary part is $-4\\text{i}$ (there is no real part, just an imaginary part). Flipping the sign, we get $4\\text{i}.$\n\nTherefore, the complex conjugate is given by $\\overset{―}{z}=4\\text{i}.$" 13194139533409 17592186045417 true] [17592186088402 1074 "To find the complex conjugate, we flip the sign of the imaginary part.\n\nIn the given number $z=3+5\\text{i},$ the imaginary part is $5\\text{i}.$ Flipping the sign, we get $-5\\text{i}.$\n\nTherefore, the complex conjugate is given by $\\overset{―}{z}=3-5\\text{i}.$" 13194139533433 17592186045417 true] [17592186091945 1074 "Recall that the indicator functions of $\\overline{A},$ $A \\cap B,$ and $A \\cup B$ have the following properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(x) & =1-{1}_{A}(x) \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(x) & =min\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\} \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(x) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true while statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(-1) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(-1),{1}_{B}(-1)\\} \\\\  & =max\\{0,1\\} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $\\mathbf{1}_{A \\,\\cup\\, B}\\,(-1) = 1,$ we conclude that $-1$ belongs to $A \\cup B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139534471 17592186045417 true] [17592186091965 1074 "A *walk* of length $n$ in an undirected graph is a sequence of $n$ edges $e_1,e_2,\\ldots,e_n$ with the associated vertices $v_1,v_2,\\ldots,v_{n+1}$ where the edge $e_i$ is incident on vertices $v_i$ and $v_{i+1}$ for each\n\n$$\ni=1,2,\\ldots,n\n$$\n\nThis can be written as follows:\n$v_1 \\overset{e_1}{-} v_2 \\overset{e_2}{-} v_3 \\overset{e_3}{-} \\cdots \\overset{e_n}{-} v_{n+1}$\nSuppose the graph has no multiple (parallel) edges. In that case, we can drop the $e_i$'s in the above notation since any corresponding pair of incident vertices will uniquely determine any edge.\n\nA *trail* is a walk in which all edges are distinct.\n\nA *path* is a walk in which all vertices (and, as a result, all edges) are distinct.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, it's a walk since we can write it as\n$v_2 \\overset{e_2}{-} v_3 \\overset{e_3}{-} v_4 \\overset{e_9}{-} v_2 \\overset{e_2}{-} v_3 \\overset{e_3}{-} v_4$\nwhere vertices and edges can be repeated.\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, it's a trail since we can write it as\n$v_1 \\overset{e_1}{-} v_2 \\overset{e_9}{-} v_4 \\overset{e_4}{-} v_5 \\overset{e_5}{-} v_6 \\overset{e_{11}}{-} v_8 \\overset{e_8}{-} v_1$\nwhere all edges are distinct.\n- Statement III is false. Notice that it's not a walk, and consequently, it is not a path: no edge directly joins vertices $v_1$ and $v_3$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533354 17592186045417 true] [17592186092602 1074 "Recall that the indicator functions of $\\overset{―}{A},$ $A\\cap B,$ and $A\\cup B$ have the following properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(x) & =1-{1}_{A}(x) \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(x) & =min\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\} \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(x) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false while statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(5) & =min\\{{1}_{A}(5),{1}_{B}(5)\\} \\\\  & =min\\{1,0\\} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(5)=0,$ we conclude that $5$ does not belong to $A\\cap B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139534471 17592186045417 true] [17592186092604 1074 "Recall that the indicator functions of $\\overset{―}{A},$ $A\\cap B,$ and $A\\cup B$ have the following properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{\\overset{―}{A}}\\,(x) & =1-{1}_{A}(x) \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cap \\,B}\\,(x) & =min\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\} \\\\ {1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(x) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(x),{1}_{B}(x)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true while statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(5) & =max\\{{1}_{A}(5),{1}_{B}(5)\\} \\\\  & =max\\{1,0\\} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${1}_{A\\,\\cup \\,B}\\,(5)=1,$ we conclude that $5$ belongs to $A\\cup B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186096444 1074 "We want to find a \"path\" from $T$ to $S$ consisting only of the vectors $\\mathbf a, \\mathbf b,$ or $\\mathbf c.$\n\nAccording to the triangle law of addition,\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{\\overrightarrow{TS}}} = {\\color{blue}{\\overrightarrow{TP}}} + {\\color{blue}{\\overrightarrow{PQ}}} + {\\color{blue}{\\overrightarrow{QS}}}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/53/53940607b5cbf0220008e6e31ec60f2fd7fd2e4f70c5a8e95e7fcfd64064b095.png)\n\nNow, from the original diagram, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\overrightarrow{TP}} = -\\mathbf{b}, \\qquad {\\color{blue}\\overrightarrow{PQ}} = \\mathbf{a}, \\qquad {\\color{blue}\\overrightarrow{QS}} = \\mathbf{c}.\n$$\n\nLet's add these to our diagram.\n\n![](images/81/810cce10eb330b1767a101d9107a9534c437daa123f13056a486a9fc96dbab89.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{TS} & =\\overset{\\to }{TP}+\\overset{\\to }{PQ}+\\overset{\\to }{QS} \\\\  & =-b+a+c \\\\  & =a-b+c.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045417 true] [17592186096451 1074 "![](images/76/76b9867a7c1ef484922241ebe44272f0104e406c31310bf36227a13850f50d8b.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup ONM$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $ON=2|\\mathbf{n}|$;\n\n- $\\overrightarrow{ON}$ and $\\mathbf{n}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overrightarrow{ON}=2\\mathbf{n}$.\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $NM=2|\\mathbf{k}|$\n\n- $\\overrightarrow{NM}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overrightarrow{NM}=2\\mathbf{k}$.\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\overset{\\to }{ON}+\\overset{\\to }{NM} \\\\  & =2n+2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $\\mathbf{m}=\\lambda\\mathbf{n}+\\mu\\mathbf{k}=2\\mathbf{n}+2\\mathbf{k}$. \n\nSince $\\mathbf{n} \\nparallel \\mathbf{k},$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{n}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda=2$ and $\\mu=2$" 13194139533390 17592186045417 true] [17592186096452 1074 "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{OX} = \\overrightarrow{OA}+\\overrightarrow{AX}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overrightarrow{OA} = \\mathbf{a},$ so we just need to work out $\\overrightarrow{AX}.$\n\nFirst, from $\\triangle OAB,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{AO}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-a+b \\\\  & =b-a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $AX:XB=3:1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{AX}=\\dfrac{3}{4}\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\dfrac{3}{4}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OX} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AX} \\\\  & =a+\\frac{3}{4}(b-a) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}a+\\frac{3}{4}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045417 true] [17592186096486 1074 "- Equality I is true. Indeed, $\\overrightarrow{CB}+\\overrightarrow{BA}=\\overrightarrow{CA}.$\n\n- Equality II is false. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{CB}-\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{CB}+\\overset{\\to }{BA} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{CA} \\\\  & \\ne \\overset{\\to }{AC}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nsince $\\overrightarrow{CA}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AC}$ have opposite directions.\n\n- Equality III is false. Indeed, $\\overrightarrow{DC}\\neq\\overrightarrow{BA}$ since they have opposite directions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533391 17592186045417 true] [17592186096518 1074 "The linear correlation coefficient $\\rho$ of the data sets $x=\\big\\{ x_1,x_2, \\ldots, x_n \\big\\}$ and $y=\\big\\{ y_1,y_2, \\ldots, y_n \\big\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\rho(x,y) =  \\dfrac{\\textrm{Cov}(x,y)}{\\sigma_x \\cdot \\sigma_y} = \n\\dfrac{S_{xy}}{\\sqrt{S_{xx} \\cdot S_{yy}}}.\n$$\n\nIn addition, we have the following rule of thumb:\n\n- if $|\\rho| \\gt 0.7,$ the correlation is *strong*\n\n- if $0.3\\leq |\\rho| \\leq 0.7,$ the correlation is *weak*\n\n- if $|\\rho| \\lt 0.3,$ then the correlation is *negligible*\n\nLet's now examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. To calculate $\\rho,$ we first compute the sum in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =(26-24)(100-107)+(30-24)(118-107) \\\\  & +(22-24)(124-107)+(18-24)(86-107) \\\\  & =(2)(-7)+(6)(11)+(-2)(17)+(-6)(-21) \\\\  & =-14+66-34+126 \\\\  & =144\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSecond, we compute the sums in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =(26-24)^{2}+(30-24)^{2}+(22-24)^{2}+(18-24)^{2} \\\\  & =4+36+4+36 \\\\  & =80 \\\\ {S}_{yy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y})^{2} \\\\  & =(100-107)^{2}+(118-107)^{2}+(124-107)^{2}+(86-107)^{2} \\\\  & =49+121+289+441 \\\\  & =900\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{144}{\\sqrt{80}\\cdot \\sqrt{900}} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{144}{8.944\\,272\\cdot 30} \\\\  & ≈0.54,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Since $0.3\\leq |\\rho| \\leq 0.7$ the correlation is weak. Also, since $\\rho$ is positive, the correlation is positive.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186096522 1074 "The (population) linear correlation coefficient of the data sets $x=\\big\\{ x_1,x_2, \\ldots, x_n \\big\\}$ and $y=\\big\\{ y_1,y_2, \\ldots, y_n \\big\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\rho(x,y) =  \\dfrac{\\textrm{Cov}(x,y)}{\\sigma_x \\cdot \\sigma_y}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\textrm{Cov}(x,y)$ is the covariance, and $\\sigma_x$ and $\\sigma_y$ are the standard deviations of the data sets.\n\nFirst, we need to compute the covariance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(6-2)(5-4)+(3-2)(7-4)+(-1-2)(-2-4)+(0-2)(6-4)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(4)(1)+(1)(3)+(-3)(-6)+(-2)(2)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[4+3+18-4] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 21 \\\\  & =5.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the formula. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{5.25}{2.739\\cdot 3.536} \\\\  & ≈0.54\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186096523 1074 "The residual equals the difference between the actual value of the dependent variable and the value estimated using the regression model:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Residual} = \\text{Actual} - \\textrm{Estimated}\n$$\n\nThe highlighted datapoint has coordinates $(80,60)$. So, our actual value is $y=60$.\n\nOn the other hand, the point on the regression curve that corresponds to $x=80$ has the $y$-coordinate $50$. So, our estimated value is\n\n$$\n\\widehat{y}=50\n$$\n\n![](images/49/49014ffe0943d7ce18084c359bc09ab0877cbc93a7d5df4beff83b0dadce47b7.png)\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nResidual &= Actual - Estimated \\\\\n&= y - y^(^) \\\\\n&= 60 - 50 \\\\\n&= 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residual is $10$ animal species." 13194139533355 17592186045417 true] [17592186096593 1074 "The linear correlation coefficient $\\rho$ of the data sets $x=\\big\\{ x_1,x_2, \\ldots, x_n \\big\\}$ and $y=\\big\\{ y_1,y_2, \\ldots, y_n \\big\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\rho(x,y) =  \\dfrac{\\textrm{Cov}(x,y)}{\\sigma_x \\cdot \\sigma_y} = \\dfrac{S_{xy}}{\\sqrt{S_{xx} \\cdot S_{yy}}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the sum in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =(13-11)(-1-2)+(10-11)(3-2) \\\\  & +(9-11)(2-2)+(12-11)(4-2) \\\\  & =(2)(-3)+(-1)(1)+(-2)(0)+(1)(2) \\\\  & =-6-1+0+2 \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSecond, we compute the sums in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =(13-11)^{2}+(10-11)^{2}+(9-11)^{2}+(12-11)^{2} \\\\  & =4+1+4+1 \\\\  & =10 \\\\ {S}_{yy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y})^{2} \\\\  & =(-1-2)^{2}+(3-2)^{2}+(2-2)^{2}+(4-2)^{2} \\\\  & =9+1+0+4 \\\\  & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{(-5)}{\\sqrt{10}\\cdot \\sqrt{14}} \\\\  & ≈-\\frac{5}{3.162\\,278\\cdot 3.741\\,657} \\\\  & ≈-0.42\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186096594 1074 "The equation of the regression line is given by\n\n$$\ny = mx+b,\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{{S}_{xx}},\\,b=\\overset{―}{y}-m\\overset{―}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $m{:}$\n\n- First, we compute the sum in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =(1-4)(6-3)+(2-4)(4-3) \\\\  & +(4-4)(2-3)+(9-4)(0-3) \\\\  & =(-3)(3)+(-2)(1)+(0)(-1)+(5)(-3) \\\\  & =-9-2-0-15 \\\\  & =-26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, we compute the sum in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =(1-4)^{2}+(2-4)^{2}+(4-4)^{2}+(9-4)^{2} \\\\  & =9+4+0+25 \\\\  & =38\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting into the formula for $m,$ we obtain \n\n$$\nm = \\dfrac{-26}{38} = -\\dfrac{13}{19} \\approx -0.7.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute into the formula for $b{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =\\overset{―}{y}-m\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =3-(-\\frac{13}{19})\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =\\frac{109}{19} \\\\  & ≈5.7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the regression line is\n\n$$\ny = -0.7x + 5.7.\n$$" 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186096595 1074 "The equation of the regression line is given by\n\n$$\ny = mx+b,\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{{S}_{xx}},\\,b=\\overset{―}{y}-m\\overset{―}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $m{:}$\n\n- First, we compute the sum in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =(0-2)(35-24)+(1-2)(26-24) \\\\  & +(3-2)(19-24)+(4-2)(16-24) \\\\  & =(-2)(11)+(-1)(2)+(1)(-5)+(2)(-8) \\\\  & =-22-2-5-16 \\\\  & =-45\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, we compute the sum in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =(0-2)^{2}+(1-2)^{2}+(3-2)^{2}+(4-2)^{2} \\\\  & =4+1+1+4 \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, substituting into the formula for $m,$ we obtain \n\n$$\nm =-\\dfrac{45}{10} = -4.5.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute into the formula for $b{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =\\overset{―}{y}-m\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =24-(-\\frac{9}{2})\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =33\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the regression line is\n\n$$\ny = -4.5x+33\n$$\n\nFinally, we can estimate the required value by substituting $y=10$ into the equation of the trend line and solving for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-4.5x+33 \\\\ 10 & =-4.5x+33 \\\\ -23 & =-4.5x \\\\ x & ≈5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to this model, it takes approximately $5$ years for the car's value to drop to $\\[math]10\\,000.$\n\n**Note:** Since we're extrapolating (the point where $y=10$ is not within the original data range), our result *may* be unreliable." 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186096603 1074 "We want to find a \"path\" from $T$ to $Q$ consisting only of the vectors $\\mathbf a, \\mathbf b, \\mathbf c,$ and $\\mathbf d.$\n\nAccording to the triangle law of addition, \n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{\\overrightarrow{TQ}}} = {\\color{blue}{\\overrightarrow{TS}}} + {\\color{blue}{\\overrightarrow{SQ}}},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/49/4902397acd28e2e5f85063ba55c576d3768360225a705f31a66bd460405dd5a6.png)\n\n \n\nNow, from the original diagram, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\overrightarrow{TS}} = \\mathbf{d}, \\qquad {\\color{blue}{\\overrightarrow{SQ}}} = -\\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nLet's add these to our diagram.\n\n![](images/08/08086f7e09568f908aa4919a1156d52e465216221be8908e72a1fd90ec8843dd.png)\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{TQ} & =\\overset{\\to }{TS}+\\overset{\\to }{SQ} \\\\  & =d+(-b) \\\\  & =d-b.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045417 true] [17592186097027 1074 "We want to find a \"path\" from $Q$ to $T$ consisting only of the vectors $a,b,$ and $c.$\n\nAccording to the triangle law of addition,\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{QT}=\\overset{\\to }{QS}+\\overset{\\to }{SP}+\\overset{\\to }{PT}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/f7/f728becaa8903fc0aa17ea9d15dd7199447dd70eff5b4a3d2be5982f1eeca8a5.png)\n\nNow, from the original diagram, we have that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{QS}=-b,\\,\\overset{\\to }{SP}=-a,\\,\\overset{\\to }{PT}=c.\n$$\n\nLet's add these to our diagram.\n\n![](images/05/055bb2c63c42defaa9f624f20b8ad92dd80f3d34b0d689def859b34785318749.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{QT} & =\\overset{\\to }{QS}+\\overset{\\to }{SP}+\\overset{\\to }{PT} \\\\  & =-b+(-a)+c \\\\  & =c-a-b.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535619 17592186045417 true] [17592186097046 1074 "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AT}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=a,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{AT}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OAB,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{AO}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-a+b \\\\  & =b-a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $AT:TB=1:1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{AT}=\\frac{1}{2}\\overset{\\to }{AB}=\\frac{1}{2}(b-a).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OT} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AT} \\\\  & =a+\\frac{1}{2}(b-a) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}a+\\frac{1}{2}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533450 17592186045417 true] [17592186097078 1074 "The equation of the regression line is given by\n\n$$\ny=mx+b,\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{{S}_{xx}},\\,b=\\overset{―}{y}-m\\overset{―}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's compute $m:$\n\n- First, we compute the sum in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =(3-6)(95-109)+(5-6)(106-109) \\\\  & +(7-6)(110-109)+(9-6)(125-109) \\\\  & =(-3)(-14)+(-1)(-3)+(1)(1)+(3)(16) \\\\  & =42+3+1+48 \\\\  & =94\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, we compute the sum in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =(3-6)^{2}+(5-6)^{2}+(7-6)^{2}+(9-6)^{2} \\\\  & =9+1+1+9 \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting into the formula for $m,$ we obtain \n\n$$\nm=\\frac{94}{20}=\\frac{47}{10}=4.7.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute into the formula for $b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =\\overset{―}{y}-m\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =109-4.7\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =109-28.2 \\\\  & =80.8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the regression line is\n\n$$\ny=4.7x+80.8.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can predict the height of a six-year-old child in this family by substituting $x=6$ into our regression line:\n\n$$\ny=4.7(6)+80.8=109\n$$\n\nTherefore, the model predicts that a six-year-old child will be $109.0\\,\\text{cm}$ tall." 13194139535987 17592186045417 true] [17592186097164 1074 "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=a+\\lambda b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $a.$ So, we begin with the vector $a,$ and then find the remaining vector needed to reach the point $C.$\n\n![](images/67/6782f2fa59056afd9022edbaf69c44a991bf12a7ac6a190bea3cc08fc7a4996c.png)\n\nNote that in $△OAC,$ we have:\n\n- $AC=2.5|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $b$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=2.5b.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =a+2.5b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=a+\\lambda b=a+2.5b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $b,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =2.5\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045417 true] [17592186097165 1074 "![](images/60/601e049067de307ef2321ae9b516d1c7306d837ca0758c080074446a8c54389d.png)\n\nIn $△OAC$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OA=2|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OA}$ and $a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=2a.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $AC=2|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $b$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=2b.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =2a+2b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+\\mu b=2a+2b.$ \n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =2$" 13194139533410 17592186045417 true] [17592186097169 1074 "First, we sketch the line that is passing through $O$ and parallel to $\\text{a}.$\n\n![](images/12/12579c5ee42e21b0a312f628bda36992fc343ace38dfbc2758a2e3d6aed49124.png)\n\nNow, we choose a point $A$ on the line, in the direction of $\\text{a},$ such that $OA$ equals the length of $\\text{a}.$\n\n![](images/cd/cdda8763ddc663d090099a30731d212a076b24063c7fdfeef9c0f7d9505709e1.png)\n\nSo, $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=\\text{a}.$" 13194139535509 17592186045417 true] [17592186097181 1074 "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+3b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $3b.$ So, we first draw the vector $3b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/f6/f67af4512a7ab61f862932a60dafc25ef62