ormation into a diagram, as shown below.\n\n![](images/2a/2ac8b2b1d619213a0d2680409ee4af6bd5b8be31ba2dfc1cf586bb705984941d.png)\n\nLet $m_1$ and $m_2$ be the slopes of the lines $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{JK}$ and $l$, respectively.\n\nFirst, we compute the slope of $\\overline{JK}$ using the coordinates of the points $J(10,3)$ and $K(4,6)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm_{1} &= \\frac{y_{2} - y_{1}}{x_{2} - x_{1}} = \\frac{6 - 3}{4 - 10} = \\frac{3}{-6} =-\\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the perpendicular bisector is the negative reciprocal of the slope of the diameter. Therefore, the slope of $l$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm_{2} &= -\\frac{1}{m_{1}} = - (1)/((-\\frac{1}{2})) = 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe center of the circle is the midpoint of the diameter. So, we can use the midpoint formula to find the center as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP &= (\\frac{10 + 4}{2}, \\frac{3 + 6}{2}) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{14}{2}, \\frac{9}{2}) \\\\\n&= (7, \\frac{9}{2})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line $l$ passes through the center\n\n$$\nP\\left(7, \\dfrac{9}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nwith a slope of $m_2=2$. So, we can use the point-slope formula to write the equation of the line as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_{1} &= m_{2}(x - x_{1}) \\\\\ny - \\frac{9}{2} &= 2(x - 7) \\\\\ny &= 2x - 14 + \\frac{9}{2} \\\\\ny &= 2x - \\frac{19}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533356 17592186045417 true] [17592186100633 1074 "The angle $\\angle ABC$ is subtended by the diameter $\\overline{AC}$, so its measure is $90^\\circ$. So we have $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB} \\perp \\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CB}$.\n\n![](images/e0/e0016ff9053c8e38d6ca354574486b18b132e21756f712f9d56279591d594b34.png)\n\nUsing the coordinates $A(-1,0)$ and $B(2,3)$, let's find the slope of the line $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB}$:\n\n$$\nm_{AB} = \\dfrac{y_2 - y_1}{x_2 - x_1} = \\dfrac{3 - 0}{2 - (-1)} = 1\n$$\n\nSince $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CB}$ is perpendicular to $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB}$, its slope is the negative reciprocal:\n\n$$\nm_{CB} = -\\dfrac{1}{m_{BC}} = -1\n$$\n\nSince $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CB}$ passes through the point $B(2,3)$ with slope\n\n$$\nm_{CB} = -1\n$$\n\nits equation in slope-point form must be\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_{1} &= m_{CB}(x - x_{1}) \\\\\ny - 3 &= -1 \\cdot (x - 2) \\\\\ny - 3 &= - (x - 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the point $C(k,0)$ also lies on $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CB}$, so its coordinates $(k,0)$ must satisfy the equation above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - 3 &= - (x - 2) \\\\\n0 - 3 &= - (k - 2) \\\\\n- 3 &= -k + 2 \\\\\nk &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the answer is $k=5$." 13194139533356 17592186045417 true] [17592186100725 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Differentiating $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(\\frac{1}{x})}^{′} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ and $y' = -\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533391 17592186045417 true] [17592186100801 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y = e^x + 1,$ we get $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = e^x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ {e}^{x} & \\overset{?}{=}{e}^{x}+1 \\\\ 0 & \\ne 1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *false*. Therefore, the function $y = e^x+1$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y = -e^x,$ we get $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = -e^x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ -{e}^{x} & =-{e}^{x}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y = -e^x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y = 2e^x,$ we get $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = 2e^x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ 2{e}^{x} & =2{e}^{x}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y = 2e^x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533394 17592186045417 true] [17592186101155 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)={x}^{4},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{4})^{′} \\\\  & =4{x}^{3} \\\\  & \\ne 5{x}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y={x}^{4}$ and ${y}^{′}=4{x}^{3}$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4y}{x} \\\\ (4{x}^{3}) & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4({x}^{4})}{x} \\\\ 4{x}^{3} & =4{x}^{3}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y={x}^{4}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533405 17592186045417 true] [17592186101157 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Differentiating $f(x)={e}^{2x},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={({e}^{2x})}^{′} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y={e}^{2x}$ and ${y}^{′}=2{e}^{2x}$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}2y \\\\ 2{e}^{2x} & =2{e}^{2x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y={e}^{2x}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533405 17592186045417 true] [17592186101158 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=-2{e}^{-2x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4{e}^{-2x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2y \\\\ 4{e}^{-2x} & \\overset{?}{=}-2(-2{e}^{-2x}) \\\\ 4{e}^{-2x} & =4{e}^{-2x}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=-2{e}^{-2x}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=-2{e}^{x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-2{e}^{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2y \\\\ -2{e}^{x} & \\overset{?}{=}-2(-2{e}^{x}) \\\\ -2{e}^{x} & \\overset{?}{=}4{e}^{x} \\\\ 0 & \\ne 6{e}^{x}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=-2{e}^{x}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=2{e}^{-x}+1,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-2{e}^{-x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2y \\\\ -2{e}^{-x} & \\overset{?}{=}-2(2{e}^{-x}+1) \\\\ -2{e}^{-x} & \\overset{?}{=}-4{e}^{-x}-2 \\\\ 2{e}^{-x} & \\ne -2.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=2{e}^{-x}+1$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533405 17592186045417 true] [17592186101538 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=2x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2y}{x}-2 \\\\ 2 & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2\\cdot 2x}{x}-2 \\\\ 2 & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4x}{x}-2 \\\\ 2 & \\overset{?}{=}4-2 \\\\ 2 & =2.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=2x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y={x}^{2},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2y}{x}-2 \\\\ 2x & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2\\cdot {x}^{2}}{x}-2 \\\\ 2x & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{x}-2 \\\\ 2x & \\overset{?}{=}2x-2 \\\\ 0 & \\ne -2.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *false*. Therefore, the function $y={x}^{2}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y={x}^{3},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2y}{x}-2 \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2\\cdot {x}^{3}}{x}-2 \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{x}-2 \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & \\overset{?}{=}2{x}^{2}-2 \\\\ {x}^{2} & \\ne -2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y={x}^{3}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533432 17592186045417 true] [17592186105455 1074 "First, notice that the tangent $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{FG}$ passes through the endpoint $H$ of the chord $\\overline{KH}$. Thus, by the tangent-chord theorem,\n\n$$\nm\\angle{KHF} = \\dfrac{1}{2}m\\overset{\\frown}{KH}\n$$\n\nSolving for $m\\overset{\\frown}{KH}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nmKH^(⌢) &= 2m∠KHF \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 70^{∘} \\\\\n&= 140^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045417 true] [17592186105456 1074 "Since the sum of the interior angles of the triangle $\\triangle ABC$ is $180^{\\circ}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠B &= 180^{∘} - m∠C - m∠CAB \\\\\n&= 180^{∘} - 49^{∘} - 75^{∘} \\\\\n&= 56^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the tangent $l$ passes through the endpoint $A$ of the chord $\\overline{AC}$. Thus, by the tangent-chord theorem,\n\n$$\n\\alpha = \\dfrac{1}{2}m\\overset{\\frown}{AC}\n$$\n\nNow, according to the inscribed angle theorem, the measure of an inscribed angle $\\angle B$ is half the measure of its central arc $\\overset{\\frown}{AC}$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\alpha = \\dfrac{1}{2}m\\overset{\\frown}{AC} = m\\angle{B} = 56^{\\circ}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045417 true] [17592186105464 1074 "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $P$ and parallel to $\\mathbf{v}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\mathbf{p} + t \\mathbf{v},\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$ and $\\mathbf{p}$ is the position vector of $P.$ \n\nWe can find a direction vector by computing the displacement vector between the given points $P(1,-1,2)$ and $Q(3,2,7),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =⟨3,2,7⟩-⟨1,-1,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,3,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector equation of the line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535467 17592186045443 true] [17592186105465 1074 "First, we find the vector equation of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =⟨1,2,0⟩+t⟨-4,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\nSince $A$ lies on the line, we know that there must be a real number $t$ such that\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{OA} = \\langle 1, 2, 0 \\rangle + t \\langle -4, 1, 1 \\rangle\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PA} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(⟨1,2,0⟩+t⟨-4,1,1⟩)-⟨1,2,0⟩ \\\\  & =t\\,⟨-4,\\,1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4t,\\,t,\\,t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $PA=6\\sqrt{2},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}PA & =6\\sqrt{2} \\\\ |\\overset{\\to }{PA}| & =6\\sqrt{2} \\\\ |⟨-4t,\\,t,\\,t⟩| & =6\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{(-4t)^{2}+{t}^{2}+{t}^{2}} & =6\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{18{t}^{2}} & =6\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\pm 3t\\sqrt{2} & =6\\sqrt{2} \\\\ t & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the values $t=\\pm 2$ into the equation for the line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{1}}=⟨1,2,0⟩+2⟨-4,1,1⟩=⟨-7,4,2⟩, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{2}}=⟨1,2,0⟩-2⟨-4,1,1⟩=⟨9,0,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points are $A_1(-7, 4, 2)$ and $A_2(9, 0, -2).$" 13194139533388 17592186045417 true] [17592186105466 1074 "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(-2\\,i+5\\,j-k)-(-4\\,i+j+2\\,k) \\\\  & =2\\,i+4\\,j-3\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\mathbf{p} + t \\mathbf{v} \n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\mathbf{r} = (-4 \\, \\mathbf{i} + \\mathbf{j} + 2\\,\\mathbf{k}) + t (2 \\, \\mathbf{i} + 4 \\, \\mathbf{j} - 3 \\, \\mathbf{k})\n$$\n\nand the parametrization of the line segment $\\overline{PQ}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =(-4\\,i+j+2\\,k)+t(2\\,i+4\\,j-3\\,k) \\\\  & =(2t-4)\\,i+(4t+1)\\,j+(2-3t)\\,k,\\,t\\in [0,1].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045417 true] [17592186105495 1074 "![](images/af/af459fd393070011074661807dd094d98ecb32f256816c5fa517b11e7d696c1f.png)\n\nThe correct proof is shown below.\n\n|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | $ABCD$ is a parallelogram | Given |\n| 2 | $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AD} \\parallel \\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{BC}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Definition of a parallelogram}}$ |\n| 3 | $\\angle C$ and $\\angle D$ are consecutive interior angles | Definition of consecutive interior angles |\n| 4 | $\\angle C$ and $\\angle D$ are supplementary | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Consecutive interior angles theorem}}$ |\n| 5 | $m\\angle{C} + m\\angle{D}= 180^\\circ$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Definition of supplementary angles}}$ |\n\nLet's examine each row in turn.\n\n- Consider row 2. By the definition of a parallelogram, its opposite sides are parallel. This means that $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AD} \\parallel \\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{BC}$.\n- Consider row 4. From row 3, we know that $\\angle C$ and $\\angle D$ are consecutive interior angles. Therefore, by the consecutive interior angles theorem,\n$\\angle C$ and $\\angle D$ are supplementary.\n- Consider row 5. From row 4, we have that $\\angle C$ and $\\angle D$ are supplementary angles. Therefore, by the definition of supplementary angles, we have\n$m\\angle{C} + m\\angle{D}= 180^\\circ$." 13194139533357 17592186045417 true] [17592186105498 1074 "First, since $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CB} \\parallel \\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{PO}$, then\n$\\angle{CAP} \\cong \\angle{C}$,\nas they are alternate interior angles. Thus,\n\n$$\nm\\angle{CAP} = 50^{\\circ}\n$$\n\nNotice that tangent $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{PO}$ passes through the end-point $A$ of the chord $\\overline{AB}$. Thus, by the tangent-chord theorem,\n\n$$\n\\angle{BAO} = \\dfrac{1}{2}m\\overset{\\frown}{AB}\n$$\n\nNow, according to the inscribed angle theorem, the measure of an inscribed angle $\\angle C$ is half the measure of its central arc $\\overset{\\frown}{AB}$. Therefore,\n\n$$\nm\\angle{BAO} = \\dfrac{1}{2}m\\overset{\\frown}{AB} = \\angle{C} = 50^{\\circ}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠BAO + m∠BAC + m∠CAP &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n50^{∘} + x + 50^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nx &= 180^{∘} - 50^{∘} - 50^{∘} \\\\\n&= 80^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045417 true] [17592186105502 1074 "First, notice that the tangent $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{UV}$ passes through the endpoint $V$ of the chord $\\overline{VW}$. Thus, by the tangent-chord theorem,\n\n$$\nm\\angle{UVW} = \\dfrac{1}{2}m\\overset{\\frown}{VRW}\n$$\n\nSolving for $m\\overset{\\frown}{VRW}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nmVRW^(⌢) &= 2m∠UVW \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot 120^{∘} \\\\\n&= 240^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nmVW^(⌢) &= 360^{∘} - mVRW^(⌢) \\\\\n&= 360^{∘} - 240^{∘} \\\\\n&= 120^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533357 17592186045417 true] [17592186105513 1074 "Recall that a directed cycle in a directed graph is a walk with no repeated vertices, except the first and last vertex, which are the same.\n\nA directed acyclic graph (DAG) is a directed graph that contains no directed cycles.\n\nBy examining our graph, we can see it contains only one cycle:\n$v_2 \\overset{e_3}{\\to} v_5 \\to v_7 \\to v_4 \\to v_2$\n\nHence, to obtain a DAG, we should break this cycle by removing one of its edges.\nOf the given labeled edges, only the edge $e_3$ is a part of the cycle.\nTherefore, we remove $e_3$." 13194139533357 17592186045417 true] [17592186105520 1074 "Since the line passes through the points $S$ and $T,$ we can represent this line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{ST}\n$$\n\nWe use the \"$\\longleftrightarrow$\" symbol to denote that the line extends forever in **both** directions." 13194139534633 17592186045417 true] [17592186105586 1074 "We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OA}=a=i+j+2k, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{OB}=b=2i-2j+3k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ as the difference of position vectors, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{OB}-\\overset{\\to }{OA} \\\\  & =b-a \\\\  & =(2i-2j+3k)-(i+j+2k) \\\\  & =i-3j+k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+9+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045417 true] [17592186105589 1074 "Note that the position vectors of the points $A$, $B$, $C$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a=⟨2,-1,-5⟩,\\,b=⟨-1,0,-4⟩,\\,c=⟨5,1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle $\\triangle ABC$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $\\angle BAC = \\theta.$\n\n![](images/28/285b8343208079181ac6036cf9ca16abc974d59b9262878ed40c0da9af5683d0.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AC}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-4⟩-⟨2,-1,-5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,1,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =⟨5,1,1⟩-⟨2,-1,-5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,2,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC}}{|\\overset{\\to }{AB}||\\overset{\\to }{AC}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3\\cdot 3+1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 6}{\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{6}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{\\sqrt{11}\\sqrt{49}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{7\\sqrt{11}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arccos \\left( -\\dfrac{1}{7\\sqrt{11}} \\right) \\approx 92.5^\\circ.\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045417 true] [17592186105591 1074 "First, we find the vectors $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AC}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(-2i+k)-(i-2k) \\\\  & =-3i+3k \\\\  & =⟨-3,0,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(i+j-k)-(i-2k) \\\\  & =j+k \\\\  & =⟨0,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 1)i-(-3\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 0)j+(-3\\cdot 1-0\\cdot 0)k \\\\  & =-3i+3j-3k \\\\  & =⟨-3,3,-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $\\bigtriangleup\\!ABC$ is\n\n$$\nA_{\\bigtriangleup} = \\dfrac{1}{2}A = \\dfrac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045417 true] [17592186105592 1074 "Note that the length of the altitude (height) can be related to the area of the triangle as follows:\n\n$$\nA_{\\bigtriangleup} = \\dfrac{b \\cdot h}{2},\n$$\n\n \nwhere $b$ is a base and $h$ is the corresponding altitude of the triangle.\n\nFor the $\\bigtriangleup\\!ABC,$ we have $h=CH,$ and $b=AB.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nA_{\\bigtriangleup} = \\dfrac{1}{2} CH \\cdot AB.\n$$\n\nSo, to find $CH,$ we first find $A_{\\bigtriangleup}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AC}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(i+j+2k)-(-i+j) \\\\  & =2i+2k \\\\  & =⟨2,0,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(i-j+k)-(-i+j) \\\\  & =2i-2j+k \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(0\\cdot 1-2\\cdot (-2))i-(2\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 2)j+(2\\cdot (-2)-0\\cdot 2)k \\\\  & =4i+2j-4k \\\\  & =⟨4,2,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $\\bigtriangleup\\!ABC$ is\n\n$$\nA_{\\bigtriangleup} = \\dfrac{1}{2}A = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot 6 = 3.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula $A_{\\bigtriangleup} = \\dfrac{1}{2} CH \\cdot AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3 & =CH\\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533390 17592186045417 true]