verset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{2}=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2} & =CH\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045417 true] [17592186106135 1074 "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(2i+3j+k)-(i+2j-k) \\\\  & =i+j+2k \\\\  & =⟨1,1,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(4i+j+2k)-(i+2j-k) \\\\  & =3i-j+3k \\\\  & =⟨3,-1,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =d-a \\\\  & =(i+4j+2k)-(i+2j-k) \\\\  & =2j+3k \\\\  & =⟨0,2,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|-1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|+2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot 3-2\\cdot 3)-(3\\cdot 3-0\\cdot 3)+2(3\\cdot 2-0\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =-9-9+12 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelpiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|-6|=6.\n$$" 13194139535314 17592186045417 true] [17592186106192 1074 "The given figure is a polygon with $8$ sides: $\\overset{―}{AB},\\,\\overset{―}{BC},\\,\\overset{―}{CD},\\,\\overset{―}{DE},\\,\\overset{―}{EF},\\,\\overset{―}{FG},\\,\\overset{―}{GH}$ and $\\overset{―}{AH}.$\n\nBecause it has $8$ sides, it is an octagon." 13194139534885 17592186045417 true] [17592186106197 1074 "First, we find the vector equation of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =⟨3,1,-1⟩+t⟨2,1,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSince $A$ lies on the line, we know that there must be a real number $t$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OA} & =⟨3,1,-1⟩+t⟨2,1,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PA} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(⟨3,1,-1⟩+t⟨2,1,3⟩)-⟨3,1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =t\\,⟨2,1,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,\\,t,\\,3t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $PA=\\sqrt{14},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AP & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ |\\overset{\\to }{PA}| & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ |⟨2t,\\,t,\\,3t⟩| & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ \\sqrt{(2t)^{2}+{t}^{2}+(3t)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ \\sqrt{14{t}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ \\pm t\\sqrt{14} & =\\sqrt{14} \\\\ t & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the values $t=\\pm 1$ into the equation for the line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{1}}=⟨3,1,-1⟩+1⟨2,1,3⟩=⟨5,2,2⟩, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{2}}=⟨3,1,-1⟩-1⟨2,1,3⟩=⟨1,0,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points are ${A}_{1}(5,2,2)$ and ${A}_{2}(1,0,-4).$" 13194139533411 17592186045417 true] [17592186106198 1074 "$E$ is the endpoint of the sides $\\overset{―}{DE}$ and $\\overset{―}{EF}.$ Therefore, the vertices $D$ and $F$ are adjacent to $E.$" 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186106283 1074 "First, we find the vector equation of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =⟨-2,5,-3⟩+t⟨1,-1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSince $A$ lies on the line, we know that there must be a real number $t$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OA} & =⟨-2,5,-3⟩+t⟨1,-1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PA} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(⟨-2,5,-3⟩+t⟨1,-1,2⟩)-⟨-2,5,-3⟩ \\\\  & =t\\,⟨1,-1,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t,\\,-t,\\,2t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $PA=\\sqrt{24},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AP & =\\sqrt{24} \\\\ |\\overset{\\to }{PA}| & =\\sqrt{24} \\\\ |⟨t,\\,-t,\\,2t⟩| & =\\sqrt{24} \\\\ \\sqrt{{t}^{2}+(-t)^{2}+(2t)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{24} \\\\ \\sqrt{6{t}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{24} \\\\ \\pm t\\sqrt{6} & =2\\sqrt{6} \\\\ t & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the values $t=\\pm 2$ into the equation for the line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{1}}=⟨-2,5,-3⟩+2⟨1,-1,2⟩=⟨0,3,1⟩, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{O{A}_{2}}=⟨-2,5,-3⟩-2⟨1,-1,2⟩=⟨-4,7,-7⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points are ${A}_{1}(0,3,1)$ and ${A}_{2}(-4,7,-7).$" 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186106293 1074 "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $A$ and parallel to $v$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=a+tv,\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $a$ is the position vector of $A.$ \n\nWe can find a direction vector by computing the displacement vector between the given points $A(4,5,3)$ and $B(-1,2,0)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{AB} \\\\  & =⟨-1,2,0⟩-⟨4,5,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-5,-3,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector equation of the line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535467 17592186045443 true] [17592186106294 1074 "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $P$ and parallel to $v$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=p+tv,\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $p$ is the position vector of $P.$ \n\nWe can find a direction vector by computing the displacement vector between the given points $P(0,-2,-4)$ and $Q(5,0,1)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =⟨5,0,1⟩-⟨0,-2,-4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5,2,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector equation of the line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535467 17592186045443 true] [17592186106387 1074 "Two sides are consecutive if they have a vertex in common. Therefore, $\\overset{―}{CD}$ and $\\overset{―}{EF}$ are consecutive sides to $\\overset{―}{DE}.$" 13194139535830 17592186045417 true] [17592186106388 1074 "Let's consider $△CDE.$ We can see that $\\overset{―}{CD}$ is the height corresponding to the base $\\overset{―}{DE}$ and that $DE=6\\text{ cm}.$ \n\nSince the area of $△CDE$ is $18{\\text{ cm}}^{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2} \\\\ 18 & =\\frac{6\\cdot CD}{2} \\\\ 3CD & =18 \\\\ CD & =6\\text{ cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that $\\overset{―}{CD}$ is also the height of $△ABC$ corresponding to the base $\\overset{―}{AB}.$\n\n![](images/87/87c774cbdce6c4858605e2b8c6186abc6dde73b2b636966b4049ded4f82af684.png)\n\nTherefore, we find the area of $△ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{AB\\cdot CD}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 6}{2} \\\\  & =12{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533427 17592186045417 true] [17592186106389 1074 "Diagonals join non-adjacent vertices.\n\nTherefore, the diagonals of the polygon are $\\overset{―}{AC},\\,\\overset{―}{AD},\\,\\overset{―}{BD},\\,\\overset{―}{BE}$ and $\\overset{―}{CE}.$ Of all these, the diagonal $\\overset{―}{CE}$ was not drawn on the polygon.\n\n![](images/15/15154f866047ab378947f39273c8cc5aaf30c62b6eac21b305e04b06a3f2eb61.png)" 13194139534885 17592186045417 true] [17592186106422 1074 "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. As we can see, both endpoints $A$ and $B$ lie on the circle and $\\overset{―}{AB}$ passes through the center $O.$ Therefore, $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter.\n\n- Statement II is false since both endpoints of $B$ and $C$ lie on the circle. So, $\\overset{―}{BC}$ is a chord but not a radius.\n\n- Statement III is false since the endpoint $O$ does not lie on the circle. So, $\\overset{―}{OA}$ is a radius but not a chord.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true." 13194139535332 17592186045417 true] [17592186110392 1074 "Notice that the vertex of $\\angle RST$ is point $S,$ not point $T.$ So $\\angle RST$ is *not* a straight angle.\n\nAs a result, the two rays $\\overset{\\longrightarrow}{SR}$ and $\\overset{\\longrightarrow}{ST}$ of our angle coincide. So, we can think of $\\angle RST$ as:\n\n- A *null* angle, assuming it doesn't turn at all.\n\n![](images/a8/a8916a724d4463bf2047c919b73770160d3124838b61af0dcdc2b9e04322381a.png)\n\n- A *full* angle, assuming it makes one full turn.\n\n![](images/9c/9cd768b4361e95a18ffa52d6315fb5ea6cd0cf6784bc4c9e12b1bae1f6566755.png)\n\nSo, the answer is \"Null\" or \"Full.\"" 13194139534657 17592186045417 true] [17592186110393 1074 "First, notice that $\\angle PQA$ and $\\angle PQB$ are supplementary angles. So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠PQB + m∠PQA &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠PQB + 61^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠PQB &= 119^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe lines $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CD}$ are cut by the transversal $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{PS}$.\n\nThe angles $\\angle PQB$ and $\\angle QRD$ are corresponding angles. To have parallel lines, the corresponding angles must be congruent.\n\nTo have $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB} \\parallel \\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CD}$, we need $\\angle PQB\\cong\\angle QRD$. So, these two angles must have the same measure:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠PQB &= m∠QRD \\\\\n119^{∘} &= 11x - 2^{∘} \\\\\n121^{∘} &= 11x \\\\\n11^{∘} &= x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx = 11^\\circ\n$$" 13194139533358 17592186045417 true] [17592186110394 1074 "The lines $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CD}$ are cut by the transversal $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{EF}$.\n\nThe angles $\\angle BJI$ and $\\angle JID$ are consecutive interior angles. To have parallel lines, the consecutive interior angles must be supplementary.\n\nTo have $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB} \\parallel \\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CD}$, we need\n\n$$\nm\\angle BJI + m\\angle JID = 180^\\circ\n$$\n\nSubstituting into this equation, we can solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠BJI + m∠JID &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nx + 50^{∘} + 5x + 10^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n6x + 60^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n6x &= 120^{∘} \\\\\nx &= 20^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533358 17592186045417 true] [17592186110396 1074 "The lines $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CD}$ are cut by the transversal $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{PS}$.\n\nThe angles $\\angle ARS$ and $\\angle PQD$ are alternate exterior angles. To have parallel lines, the alternate exterior angles must be congruent.\n\nTo have $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB} \\parallel \\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CD}$, we need\n\n$$\nm\\angle ARS = m\\angle PQD\n$$\n\n.\n\nSubstituting into this equation, we can solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠ARS &= m∠PQD \\\\\n2x + 20^{∘} &= 110^{∘} \\\\\n2x &= 90^{∘} \\\\\nx &= 45^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533358 17592186045417 true] [17592186110399 1074 "![](images/3b/3b0fddd3229947725b0f4f07e58ce9bfac3a9e7e348c25757162f5bc8e03d699.png)\n\nLet $m \\angle FAC=x$ and $m \\angle CAG=y.$ Then,\n\n$$\nm \\angle GAF=m\\angle FAC + m \\angle CAG=x+y.\n$$\n\nSince $\\overset{\\longrightarrow}{AF}$ is the bisector of $\\angle BAC$, we have\n\n$$\nm \\angle BAC=2x.\n$$\n\nSince $\\overset{\\longrightarrow}{AG}$ is the bisector of $\\angle CAD$, we have\n\n$$\nm \\angle CAD=2y.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $\\angle BAD$ is a straight angle, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠BAC+m∠CAD & =m∠BAD \\\\ 2x+2y & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ x+y & ={90}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $m \\angle GAF=x+y=90^\\circ.$" 13194139535458 17592186045417 true] [17592186110927 1074 "Here, the letter $x$ represents the measure of the unknown angle $∠BAC.$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The angles $∠BAC$ and $∠CAD$ are adjacent because they share the side $\\overset{\\to }{AC}.$\n\n- So, the measure of the *large* angle $(∠BAD)$ must be the *sum* of the measures of the two *smaller* angles $(∠BAC$ and $∠CAD).$\n\n- This means that the measure of $∠BAC$ must be the *difference* between the large angle $(∠BAD)$ and the smaller known angle $(∠CAD).$\n\nTherefore, the correct equation to determine $x$ is the following:\n\n$$\nx={90}^{∘}-{40}^{∘}\n$$" 13194139534679 17592186045417 true] [17592186110954 1074 "![](images/67/67952e3c3d8b030327cbae686da23d466d509de9171242f5ccc4e502f10813d7.png)\n\nFirst, notice that $\\overset{^}{COD}$ and $\\overset{^}{DOE}$ are adjacent because they share the same side $\\overset{\\to }{OD}.$\n\nWe can add the measures of two adjacent angles to find the measure of the combined angle.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m\\overset{^}{COE} & =m\\overset{^}{COD}+m\\overset{^}{DOE} \\\\  & ={63}^{∘}+{47}^{∘} \\\\  & ={110}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534679 17592186045417 true] [17592186111262 1074 "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The trapezoid has a pair of parallel sides, $\\overset{―}{AB}∥\\overset{―}{CD}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. We can see that $∠ABC$ is a right angle, that is, $m∠ABC={90}^{∘}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The trapezoid has one pair of parallel sides, $\\overset{―}{AB}∥\\overset{―}{CD}.$ We can see that $\\overset{―}{AD}$ and $\\overset{―}{BC}$ are not parallel.\n\nIn conclusion, statements I and II are true." 13194139534982 17592186045417 true] [17592186111276 1074 "![](images/cd/cd66b6b8c4e1a7a1deb68de79d842a21760df66d615721e933105361ea3f8af5.png)\n\nFirst, notice that $∠AOB$ and $∠BOC$ are adjacent because they share the same side $\\overset{\\to }{OB}.$\n\nWe can add the measures of two adjacent angles to find the measure of the combined angle.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠AOC & =m∠AOB+m∠BOC \\\\  & ={32}^{∘}+{46}^{∘} \\\\  & ={78}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534746 17592186045417 true] [17592186114985 1074 "The height (or altitude) corresponding to $\\overline{BC}$ is a line segment that \n\n- runs from the opposite vertex $A$ to the line $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{BC},$ and\n\n- is perpendicular to $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{BC}.$\n\nThe correct diagram is shown below: \n\n![](images/1a/1a704d0dd679fa71874b7485173ae83d35892dba6018e170f83aa9e119bcc315.png)" 13194139533389 17592186045417 true] [17592186114986 1074 "The height (or altitude) corresponding to $\\overline{BC}$ is a line segment that \n\n- runs from the opposite vertex $A$ to the line $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{BC},$ and\n\n- is perpendicular to $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{BC}.$\n\nTo draw the height, we proceed as follows:\n\n- Extend the line segment $\\overline{BC}.$\n\n![](images/01/010d2c4de6c3df93112295eb2351247c2162a0d84a1f6e122fdca71dca72c168.png)\n\n- Draw the perpendicular from $A$ to $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{BC}.$\n\n![](images/39/392d7d23590557ccce29b25cecabb276ee875f86c172ce15c845c13668939933.png)" 13194139533389 17592186045417 true] [17592186115042 1074 "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x) \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =3x \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-3x & =0 \\\\ x(2x-3) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=0$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2(0)^{2}) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3(0)) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(0) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is not valid because $\\ln \\left(0\\right)$ is undefined. Therefore, $x=0$ is not a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $x=\\dfrac{3}{2}$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3(\\frac{3}{2})) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{2}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{2}).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=\\dfrac{3}{2}$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the only solution is $x =\\dfrac{3}{2}.$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186115043 1074 "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(x-1) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x+1) \\\\ x-1 & =2x+1 \\\\ x & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=-2$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(x-1) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x+1) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡(-2-1) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}⁡(2(-2)+1) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡(-3) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}⁡(-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\log (-3)$ is not a real number.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the equation has no real solutions." 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186115068 1074 "The central angle is\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nradians and the radius is\n\n$$\nr = 10\\,\\textrm{cm}\n$$\n\nWe find the length of $\\overset{\\frown}{AB}$ using the arc length formula:\n\n$$\ns = r \\cdot θ = 10 \\cdot \\frac{π}{4} = \\frac{5π}{2}cm\n$$" 13194139533359 17592186045417 true] [17592186115128 1074 "If we have a data set that consists of the points $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n,$ then the mean is denoted by $\\overline{x}$ and is given by the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^n x_i\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=12$ and $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^{12} x_i = 96,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot 96=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045417 true] [17592186115129 1074 "If we have a data set that consists of the points $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n,$ then the mean is denoted by $\\overline{x}$ and is given by the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^n x_i\n$$\n\nHere, there are $n=5$ data points. Adding up all the points in the data set, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{x}_{i} & =9+10+8+11+12 \\\\  & =50.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 50 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045417 true] [17592186115130 1074 "If we have a data set that consists of the points $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n,$ then the mean is denoted by $\\overline{x}$ and is given by the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^n x_i\n$$\n\nHere, there are $n=6$ data points. Adding up all the points in the data set, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{6}{x}_{i} & =5+3+a+a+a+7 \\\\  & =15+3a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mean is given by the expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot (15+3a) \\\\  & =\\frac{15+3a}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we're told that $\\overline{x} = 9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9 & =\\frac{15+3a}{6} \\\\ 54 & =15+3a \\\\ 39 & =3a \\\\ a & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045417 true] [17592186115134 1074 "If we have a data set that consists of the points $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n,$ then the variance can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\sigma^2 = \\overline{x^2} - (\\overline{x})^2,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overline{x}$ is the mean of the data set and $\\overline{x^2}$ is the mean of the squares of the points in the data set.\n\nFirst, let's compute $\\overline{x}.$ There are $n=7$ points in our data set, so the mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot (4+5+4+7+8+11+3) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot 42 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\overline{x^2}.$ Computing the mean of the squares of the points in the data set, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot ({4}^{2}+{5}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{7}^{2}+{8}^{2}+{11}^{2}+{3}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot (16+25+16+49+64+121+9) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot 300 \\\\  & =\\frac{300}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{300}{7}-{(6)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{300}{7}-36 \\\\  & =\\frac{300-252}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{48}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045417 true] [17592186115135 1074 "The standard deviation of a data set $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is the square root of the variance:\n\n$$\n\\sigma = \\sqrt{ \\sigma^2 } = \\sqrt{ \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2 } = \\sqrt{ \\,\\,\\, \\vphantom{\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n} \\overline{x^2} - \\left( \\overline{x} \\right)^2 }\n$$\n\nFirst, let's compute $\\overline{x}.$ There are $n=6$ points in our data set, so the mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot (7+5+0+2+1+3) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 18 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\overline{x^2}.$ Computing the mean of the squares of the points in the data set, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot ({7}^{2}+{5}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot (49+25+0+4+1+9) \\\\  & =\\frac{88}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{44}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{44}{3}-{(3)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{44}{3}-9 \\\\  & =\\frac{44-27}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{17}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the standard deviation is\n\n$$\n\\sigma = \\sqrt{ \\sigma^2 } = \\sqrt{ \\dfrac{17}{3} }.\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045417 true] [17592186115136 1074 "If we have a data set that consists of the points $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n,$ then the variance of the data set is given by \n\n$$\n\\sigma^2 = \\overline{x^2} - (\\overline{x})^2,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overline{x}$ is the mean of the data set and $\\overline{x^2}$ is the mean of the squares of the points in the data set.\n\nFirst, let's compute $\\overline{x}.$ There are $n=8$ points in our data set, and we know that $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^{8} x_i = 40,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot 40 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\overline{x^2}.$ We know that $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^{8} x_i^2 = 320,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot 320 \\\\  & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =40-{(5)}^{2} \\\\  & =40-25 \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533393 17592186045417 true] [17592186115564 1074 "In this case, the area of the right triangle is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}AC\\cdot BC.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(m∠B)=\\frac{3}{2}.$ Notice that relative to $∠B,$ we have expressions for the lengths of the opposite side $\\overset{―}{AC}$ and adjacent side $\\overset{―}{BC}.$ Hence, we can use the tangent ratio to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(m∠B) & =\\frac{AC}{BC} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & =\\frac{x+3}{3x-5} \\\\ 3(3x-5) & =2(x+3) \\\\ 9x-15 & =2x+6 \\\\ 7x & =21 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the side lengths are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =3x-5=3(3)-5=4, \\\\ AC & =x+3=3+3=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of $△ABC$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}AC\\cdot BC \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533411 17592186045417 true] [17592186115569 1074 "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the variance can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}$ is the mean of the data set and $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}$ is the mean of the squares of the points in the data set.\n\nFirst, let's compute $\\overset{―}{x}.$ There are $n=10$ points in our data set, and we know that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{10}{x}_{i}=-110,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{10}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot (-110) \\\\  & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}.$ We know that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{10}{x}_{i}^{2}=1\\,350,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{10}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\cdot 1350 \\\\  & =135.\