{\\sqrt{3}}$ corresponds to the $x$-coordinate of a point that does not lie on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ Therefore, we can ignore it. Thus, ${c}_{1}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}.$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}$ to find ${c}_{2}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{a}}{{y}_{b}-{y}_{a}} & =\\frac{x-{x}_{a}}{{x}_{b}-{x}_{a}} \\\\ \\frac{y-1}{2-1} & =\\frac{x-0}{2-0} \\\\ y-1 & =\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{2}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & =\\frac{{c}_{1}}{2}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking into account that ${c}_{1}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}},$ we obtain that ${c}_{2}=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{2}+1=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}+1.$ So, the point $C$ that satisfies the mean value theorem is $(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}+1).$" 13194139533449 17592186045417 true] [17592186124761 1074 "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)={x}^{3}-yz$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨1,1,1⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1,1)-f(0,0,0) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨1,1,1⟩-⟨0,0,0⟩) \\\\ 0-0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,1,1⟩ \\\\ 0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨3{x}^{2},-z,-y⟩ \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨3{c}_{1}^{2},-{c}_{3},-{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,1,1⟩ \\\\ 0 & =⟨3{c}_{1}^{2},-{c}_{3},-{c}_{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,1,1⟩ \\\\ 0 & =3{c}_{1}^{2}-{c}_{3}-{c}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset