 =∇f⟨c⟩\\cdot ⟨1,1⟩ \\\\ 6 & =⟨-3{c}_{1}^{2},3{c}_{2}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,1⟩ \\\\ 6 & =-3{c}_{1}^{2}+3{c}_{2}^{2} \\\\ 2 & =-{c}_{1}^{2}+{c}_{2}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{a}}{{y}_{b}-{y}_{a}} & =\\frac{x-{x}_{a}}{{x}_{b}-{x}_{a}} \\\\ \\frac{y-1}{2-1} & =\\frac{x+1}{0+1} \\\\ y-1 & =x+1 \\\\ y & =x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & ={c}_{2}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the equation $-{c}_{1}^{2}+{c}_{2}^{2}=2$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({c}_{2}-2)^{2}+{c}_{2}^{2} & =2 \\\\ 4{c}_{2}-4 & =2 \\\\ {c}_{2} & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find ${c}_{1}={c}_{2}-2=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, the point $C$ that satisfies the mean value theorem is $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2}).$" 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186125260 1074 "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(b)-f(a) & =∇f(c)·(b-a) \\\\ 6-1 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c)⟩\\cdot (⟨2,3⟩-⟨1,2⟩) \\\\ 5 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c)⟩\\cdot ⟨1,1⟩ \\\\ 5 & ={f}_{x}(c)+{f}_{y}(c).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533501 17592186045417 true] [17592186125261 1074 "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(b)-f(a) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (b-a) \\\\ 3-2 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c),{f}_{z}(c)⟩\\cdot (⟨1,4,1⟩-⟨3,2,5⟩) \\\\ 1 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c),{f}_{z}(c)⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,2,-4⟩ \\\\ 1 & =-2{f}_{x}(c)+2{f}_{y}(c)-4{f}_{z}(c) \\\\ -1 & =2{f}_{x}(c)-2{f}_{y}(c)+4{f}_{z}(c).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complete sentence is as follows:\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that $2{f}_{x}(c)-2{f}_{y}(c)+4{f}_{z}(c)=-1.$ *" 13194139533480 17592186045417 true] [17592186125262 1074 "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)=4xz-{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,1,1⟩$ and $b=⟨1,3,2⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,3,2)-f(0,1,1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨1,3,2⟩-⟨0,1,1⟩) \\\\ 3-0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,2,1⟩ \\\\ 3 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨4z,-2y,4x+2z⟩ \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨4{c}_{3},-2{c}_{2},4{c}_{1}+2{c}_{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,2,1⟩ \\\\ 3 & =⟨4{c}_{3},-2{c}_{2},4{c}_{1}+2{c}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,2,1⟩ \\\\ 3 & =4{c}_{3}-4{c}_{2}+4{c}_{1}+2{c}_{3} \\\\ 3 & =4{c}_{1}-4{c}_{2}+6{c}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{1} & =\\frac{y-1}{2}=\\frac{z-1}{1} \\\\ x & =\\frac{y-1}{2}=z-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,y=2t+1,\\,z=t+1,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=t,\\,{c}_{2}=2t+1,\\,{c}_{3}=t+1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation $3=4{c}_{1}-4{c}_{2}+6{c}_{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4t-4(2t+1)+6(t+1) & =3 \\