ed that $N≫n,$ we have that\n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}≈B(n,p)\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that have the characteristic, and \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=np,\\,\\text{Var}[X]=np(1-p).\n$$\n\nThe sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=\\overset{―}{X}=\\frac{X}{n}.\n$$\n\nUsing the properties of expectation and variance, it is straightforward to show that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=p,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}]=\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $p=15%=0.15,$ and $n=60.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =15%=0.15, \\\\ \\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =\\frac{0.15(1-0.15)}{60} \\\\  & ≈0.0021\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places." 13194139536001 17592186045417 true] [17592186162610 1074 "We have the linear Diophantine equation\n\n$$\nax+by=c\n$$\n\nwhere $a=6,$ $b=8,$ and $c=10.$ We're given that the equation has solutions.\n\nNotice that our equation can't be solved for either of the two variables using integer coefficients. So, let's find the linear representation of $\\text{gcd}(6,8)$ using the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}8 & = & 6\\cdot 1 & + & 2 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 6 & = & 2\\cdot 3 & + & 0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the remainders (the rightmost terms) in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n2=8-6\\cdot 1.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that $d=\\text{gcd}(a,b)=2$ and\n\n$$\n6\\cdot (-1)+8=2.\n$$\n\nMultiplying this equation by $5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\cdot (-1)+8 & =2 \\\\ 6\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 5+8\\cdot 5 & =10 \\\\ 6\\cdot (-5)+8\\cdot 5 & =10,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that $(x,y)=(-5,5)$ is a solution to our Diophantine equation.\n\nNow, the general solution is\n\n$$\n(-5+\\frac{b}{d}t,\\,5-\\frac{a}{d}t)=(-5+4t,\\,5-3t),\n$$\n\nwhere $t=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…\\,.$\n\nThe greatest possible negative value of $x=-5+4t$ is attained when $t=1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{∼}{x}=-5+4\\cdot 1=-1.\n$$" 13194139534209 17592186045417 true] [17592186166485 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n,$ and $\\mathbf F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $\\mathbf n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S.\n$$\n\nTo compute the above dot product, we must normalize the vector $\\widetilde{\\mathbf n}.$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{\\overset{~}{n}}{‖\\overset{~}{n}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨z,\\,-y,\\,-x⟩}{\\sqrt{(z)^{2}+(-y)^{2}+(-x)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨z,\\,-y,\\,-x⟩}{\\sqrt{{z}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨z,\\,-y,\\,-x⟩}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x,\\,2z,\\,z-y⟩\\cdot \\frac{⟨z,\\,-y,\\,-x⟩}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{⟨x,\\,2z,\\,z-y⟩\\cdot ⟨z,\\,-y,\\,-x⟩}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{xz-2yz-x(z-y)}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{xy-2yz}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\frac{xy-2yz}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}S.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533388 17592186045417 true] [17592186166487 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n,$ and $\\mathbf F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $\\mathbf n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that our surface $z = 2x+2y$ can be written using the dot product as \n\n$$\n\\langle