p(B_j)$, respectively, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np^(^)(A_{i}) &= \\frac{number of observations with levelA_{i}}{total number of observations} \\\\\np^(^)(B_{j}) &= \\frac{number of observations with levelB_{j}}{total number of observations}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in our case we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np^(^)(Adults) &= \\frac{20 + 28}{80} = \\frac{3}{5} \\\\\np^(^)(Children) &= \\frac{24 + 8}{80} = \\frac{2}{5} \\\\\np^(^)(Beach) &= \\frac{20 + 24}{80} = \\frac{11}{20} \\\\\np^(^)(Mountains) &= \\frac{28 + 8}{80} = \\frac{9}{20}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's estimate the probability of each combination of values under the assumption of independence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(Adults∩Beach) &= p^(^)(Adults) \\cdot p^(^)(Beach) = \\frac{33}{100} \\\\\nP(Adults∩Mountains) &= p^(^)(Adults) \\cdot p^(^)(Mountains) = \\frac{27}{100} \\\\\nP(Children∩Beach) &= p^(^)(Children) \\cdot p^(^)(Beach) = \\frac{11}{50} \\\\\nP(Children∩Mountains) &= p^(^)(Children) \\cdot p^(^)(Mountains) = \\frac{9}{50}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected frequencies for each combination of values under the assumption of independence can be estimated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE_{11} &= N \\cdot P(Adults∩Beach) = 80 \\cdot \\frac{33}{100} = 26.4 \\\\\nE_{12} &= N \\cdot P(Adults∩Mountains) = 80 \\cdot \\frac{27}{100} = 21.6 \\\\\nE_{21} &= N \\cdot P(Children∩Beach) = 80 \\cdot \\frac{11}{50} = 17.6 \\\\\nE_{22} &= N \\cdot P(Children∩Mountains) = 80 \\cdot \\frac{9}{50} = 14.4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA table summarizing the observed and corresponding expected frequencies is given below:\n\n|  | Beach | Mountains |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| Adults | $O_{11}=20$<br>$E_{11} =\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}26.4}$ | $O_{12}=28$<br>$E_{12} =\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}21.6}$ |\n| Children | $O_{21}=24$<br>$E_{21} =\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}17.6}$ | $O_{22}=8$<br>$E_{22} =\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}14.4}$ |" 13194139533371 175921