\\text{Estimated}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe highlighted datapoint has coordinates $(7,-2).$ So, our actual value is $y=-2.$\n\nOn the other hand, the point on the regression line that corresponds to $x=7$ has the $y$-coordinate $-4.$ So, our estimated value of is $\\overset{^}{y}=-4.$\n\n![](images/92/92a26b8ab019879dec3208878f6a0604c0616776b09e3f8fa916fcab72df3a73.png)\n\nTherefore, the residual is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Residual} & =\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated} \\\\  & =y-\\overset{^}{y} \\\\  & =-2-(-4) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535666 17592186045417 true] [17592186176479 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=2\\text{i}$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2-2\\text{i} & 4 \\\\ -2 & -2-2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(2-2\\text{i}){x}_{1}+4{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+(-2-2\\text{i}){x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-2\\text{i}I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=-2,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1-\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nOn the other hand, since ${\\lambda }_{1}=2\\text{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the  complex conjugate of ${\\lambda }_{1}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{2}=\\overset{―}{{\\lambda }_{1}}=-2\\text{i}.\n$$\n\nMoreover, the corresponding eigenvector of ${\\lambda }_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1+\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}=\\frac{(-2)(-2)}{(1-\\text{i})(1+\\text{i})}=2.\n$$" 13194139535661 17592186045443 true] [17592186176480 1074 "First, we need to find the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we must solve the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-6-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ 5 & 2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-6-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-(-4)(5) & =0 \\\\ -12+6\\lambda -2\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+20 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +8 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm \\sqrt{{4}^{2}-4(1)(8)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm 4\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =-2\\pm 2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =-2\\pm 2\\text{i}.$\n\nNow, let's consider the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2+2\\text{i}.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-2+2\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2+2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4-2\\text{i} & -4 \\\\ 5 & 4-2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(-4-2\\text{i}){x}_{1}-4{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}+(4-2\\text{i}){x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-2+2\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=(-1-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ (-1-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=2,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2-\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, to compute the second eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2-2\\text{i}$, we simply compute the complex conjugate of ${\\overset{―}{v}}_{1},$ and get\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2+\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}} & =\\frac{(2)(2)}{(-2-\\text{i})(-2+\\text{i})}=\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535661 17592186045443 true] [17592186179425 1074 "The town's population increases by $1.5\\%$ per year; that is, by a factor of\n\n$$\n(1+0.015)=1.015\n$$\n\nevery year.\n\nLet $t$ denote the number of years and $P(t)$ the population of the town. We can construct a table for the relationship between $t$ and $P(t)$, as follows:\n\n| $t$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $\\ldots$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $P(t)$ | $15 \\, 000\\cdot(1.015)$ | $15 \\, 000\\cdot(1.015)^2$ | $15 \\, 000\\cdot(1.015)^3$ | $15 \\, 000\\cdot(1.015)^4$ | $\\ldots$ |\n\nTo determine if the relationship is linear or exponential, we need to determine how $P(t)$ changes as $t$ increases at each step:\n\n- For a linear relationship, there should be a common *difference* between successive values of $P(t)$.\n- For an exponential relationship, there should be a common *ratio* between successive values of $P(t)$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. From our table, we see that $P(3) = 15\\,000\\cdot(1.015)^3$.\n- Statement II is false. Let's check for a linear pattern by finding the difference between successive values of $P(t){:}$\n$\\begin{bmatrix}15000 \\cdot (1