rror. Since we are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.101)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{18,0.025}=2.101.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=2.101\\cdot \\sqrt{8\\cdot (\\frac{1}{20}+\\frac{{2}^{2}}{24})}≈2.8\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=3.5\\pm 2.8\n$$" 13194139536958 17592186576411 true] [17592186193185 1074 "Notice that $∠BAF$  and $∠AFC$ are alternate interior angles formed when the transversal $\\overset{↔}{AF}$ cuts the parallel lines $\\overset{↔}{AB}$ and $\\overset{↔}{CF}.$ Hence, \n\n$$\nm∠AFC=m∠BAF={71}^{∘}.\n$$\n\n \n\n![](images/bf/bfb1ce1e2aba3efe901251ad40f84fda062e8324c4dd7a1f2888081724679842.png)\n\nAlso, notice that $∠FBE$  and $∠BFC$ are alternate interior angles formed when the transversal $\\overset{↔}{BF}$ cuts the parallel lines $\\overset{↔}{AB}$ and $\\overset{↔}{CF}.$ Hence, \n\n$$\nm∠BFC=m∠FBE={139}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNow, using the angle addition postulate, we can find $∠AFB:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠AFB & =m∠BFC-m∠AFC \\\\  & ={139}^{∘}-{71}^{∘} \\\\  & ={68}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536071 17592186045417 true] [17592186193326 1074 "We will use a protractor to determine the measure of the angle $∠ABC.$ \n\nTo do this, we read the degrees where the side $\\overset{\\to }{BA}$ of the angle intersects the scale of numbers:\n\n![](images/8a/8a89b7b52135983b18892d07e173b95d7afdcc1efaa4b9c91fb0013b26d279fc.png)\n\nTherefore, we have that $m∠ABC={120}^{∘}.$" 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186193345 1074 "Let's use the upper scale of the protractor.\n\n- The side $\\overset{\\to }{BA}$ of the angle intersects the upper scale of numbers at ${145}^{∘}.$\n\n- The side $\\overset{\\to }{BC}$ intersects the upper scale of numbers at ${65}^{∘}.$\n\nTo find the measure of $∠ABC,$ we subtract the smaller reading from the larger one and get\n\n$$\nm∠ABC={145}^{∘}-{65}^{∘}={80}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139535005 17592186045417 true] [17592186198106 1074 "Since we have that\n\n$$\nP(x) \\Leftrightarrow Q(x) \\equiv \\big(P(x) \\land Q(x)\\big) \\lor \\big(\\neg P(x) \\land \\neg Q(x)\\big),\n$$\n\nthe corresponding truth set can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{P⇔Q}=({T}_{P}\\cap {T}_{Q})\\cup ({\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cap {\\overset{―}{T}}_{Q})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- The truth set of $P(x)$ is \n\n$$\nT_P = \\big\\{ 2,4,6,8,10 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nand its complement is\n\n$$\n\\overline{T}_P \n%= \\overline{\\big\\{ 4,6,8,9,10 \\big\\}} \n= \\big\\{1,3,5,7,9 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\n- The truth set of $Q(x)$ is \n\n$$\nT_Q = \\big\\{ 1,2,3,4,6 \\big\\}\n$$\n\n \nand its complement is\n\n$$\n\\overline{T}_Q \n%= \\overline{\\big\\{ \\big\\}} \n= \\big\\{ 5,7,8,9,10  \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{P}\\cap {T}_{Q} & =\\{2,4,6,8,10\\}\\cap \\{1,2,3,4,6\\} \\\\  & =\\{2,4,6\\} \\\\ {\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cap {\\overset{―}{T}}_{Q} & =\\{1,3,5,7,9\\}\\cap \\{5,7,8,9,10\\} \\\\  & =\\{5,7,9\\} \\\\ {T}_{P⇔Q} & =({T}_{P}\\cap {T}_{Q})\\cup ({\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cap {\\overset{―}{T}}_{Q}) \\\\  & =\\{2,4,6\\}\\cup \\{5,7,9\\} \\\\  & =\\{2,4,5,6,7,9\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534669 17592186045417 true] [17592186198108 1074 "Since $P(x) \\Rightarrow Q(x) \\equiv \\neg P(x) \\lor Q(x),$ the corresponding truth sets can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{P⇒Q} & ={T}_{¬P∨Q} \\\\  & ={T}_{¬P}\\cup {T}_{Q} \\\\  & ={\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cup {T}_{Q}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we sketch the set $\\overline{T}_P{:}$ \n\n![](images/1f/1fb4c7a43fde2587d01e810be898fba7c4a4acf066851ee636b8bc41cdfe7f0d.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, to get the truth set of $\\neg P(x) \\lor Q(x),$ we take all the points that belong to $\\overline{T}_p$ or $T_Q{:}$\n\n![](images/30/30eddda009ba76789427d5b76e9ed5aeb709dd75509fabd06e2a71c03483de70.png)\n\nAs a result, we get the following diagram:\n\n![](images/3e/3e44b650b220edc54c55a6407d3d8f221621fdd2109019cba04541239904eabf.png)" 13194139533457 17592186045417 true] [17592186198612 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s$ along any closed curve $C\\in D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}\\in D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1-y)=1-1=0,\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\nSince $F$ is source-free, we know the flux of $F$ is independent of the path. In particular, as we have that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{2}$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B,$ and ${n}_{2}$ is the unit normal to ${C}_{2},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/e2/e22757cf7c4e2be17cb71e63c550aa39a10ee1dc5895c63acd5a45f0baa94623.png)\n\nNotice that both ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotations of their respective unit tangent vectors.\n\nTo calculate the flux of $F$ across ${C}_{2},$ we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply the formula,\n\n- the curve should be parametrized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector ${n}_{2}$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit normal tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nWe have the parametrization\n\n$$\nr(t)=(1-2t)\\,i+(-1+2t)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =x=1-2t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =1-y=1-(2t-1)=2-2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-2t)=-2, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-1)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the flux integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-2t)\\cdot (2)-(2-2t)\\cdot (-2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-2t)+(2-2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}3-4t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\cdot {[3t-2{t}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (3-2) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s=2.\n$$" 13194139534761 17592186045417 true] [17592186198640 1074 "The center of mass, denoted $\\overset{―}{x},$ of a rod placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $a\\le x\\le b$ whose mass density function is $\\lambda (x)$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $m={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is the mass of the rod,\n\n- ${M}_{O}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\,x\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is the moment of the rod about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)=3x,\\,a=0,\\,b=2.\n$$\n\nSo, the moment of the rod about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\cdot 3x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =8-0 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m}=\\frac{8}{6}=\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$" 13194139533408 17592186045417 true] [17592186198708 1074 "The truth set of $¬P(x)$ is the *complement* of the truth set of $P(x):$\n\n$$\n{T}_{¬P}={\\overset{―}{T}}_{P},\n$$\n\nThe truth set of $P(x)$ is the set of primes between $1$ and $10,$ inclusive, i.e.,\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=\\{2,3,5,7\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{¬P} & ={\\overset{―}{T}}_{P} \\\\  & =\\overset{―}{\\{2,3,5,7\\}} \\\\  & =\\{1,4,6,8,9,10\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534632 17592186045417 true] [17592186198712 1074 "Since $P(x)⇒Q(x)≡¬P(x)∨Q(x),$ the corresponding truth sets can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{P⇒Q} & ={T}_{¬P∨Q} \\\\  & ={T}_{¬P}\\cup {T}_{Q} \\\\  & ={\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cup {T}_{Q}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note the following:\n\n- The truth set of $P(x)$ is \n\n$$\n{T}_{P}={4,6,8,9,10}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the complement is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\overset{―}{T}}_{P} & =\\overset{―}{{4,6,8,9,10}} \\\\  & ={2,3,5,7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The truth set of $Q(x)$ is \n\n$$\n{T}_{Q}={2,5,10}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{P⇒Q} & ={\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cup {T}_{Q} \\\\  & ={2,3,5,7}\\cup {2,5,10} \\\\  & ={2,3,5,7,10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533457 17592186045417 true] [17592186198714 1074 "Since we have that\n\n$$\nP(x)⇔Q(x)≡(P(x)∧Q(x))∨(¬P(x)∧¬Q(x)),\n$$\n\n \n\nthe corresponding truth set can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{P⇔Q}=({T}_{P}\\cap {T}_{Q})\\cup ({\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cap {\\overset{―}{T}}_{Q})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note the following:\n\n- To find the the truth set of $P(x),$ we solve the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+1 & \\le 7 \\\\ 2x & \\le 6 \\\\ x & \\le 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the truth set of $P(x)$ is\n${T}_{P}=(-\\infty ,3]$\nand its complement is\n${\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}=(3,\\infty ).$\n\n- The truth set of $Q(x)$ is \n${T}_{Q}=(1,\\infty )$ \nand its complement is\n${\\overset{―}{T}}_{Q}=(-\\in