of proportions $p_1-p_2$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}_1-\\widehat{\\,p}_2 \\pm z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}_1(1-\\widehat{\\,p}_1)}{n_1}+\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}_2(1-\\widehat{\\,p}_2)}{n_2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}\n$$\n\nand $Z \\sim N(0,1)$.\n\nWe are interested in finding a $98\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.98=0.02 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.01\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\nP(Z > 2.326) = 0.01\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\nz_{\\alpha/2} = z_{0.01} = 2.326\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $98\\%$ confidence interval for the difference of the population proportions $p_1-p_2$ is the following:\n\n$$\n(0.52 - 0.5) ± 2.326 \\cdot \\sqrt{(0.52(1 - 0.52))/(75) + (0.5(1 - 0.5))/(62)}\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n0.02 ± 0.20\n$$\n\nFinally, the confidence interval is\n\n$$\n(0.02 - 0.20, \\, 0.02 + 0.20) = \\left(-0.18, 0.22\\right)\n$$" 13194139533378 17592186045417 true] [17592186203777 1074 "![](images/2b/2b6d992c05a2e3286d5c8a7fc57b8d59e67f2faac291817d22ef5ba515877b6b.png)\n\nA sketch of the given information is shown above, and the correct proof is below.\n\n|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | $m\\angle ACD = 90^\\circ$ | Definition of perpendicular lines |\n| 2 | $AD^2 = AC^2 + DC^2$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Pythagorean theorem for}\\triangle ACD}$ |\n| 3 | $m\\angle DCB = 90^\\circ$ | Definition of perpendicular lines |\n| 4 | $DB^2 = CB^2 + DC^2$ | Pythagorean theorem for $\\triangle DCB$ |\n| 5 | $\\overline{CB} \\cong \\overline{AC}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Definition of bisector}}$ |\n| 6 | $CB = AC$ | Definition of congruent segments |\n| 7 | $DB^2 = AC^2 + DC^2$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Substitution}}$ |\n| 8 | $AC^2 + DC^2 = DB^2$ | Symmetric property of equality |\n| 9 | $AD^2 = DB^2$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Transitive property of equality}}$ |\n| 10 | $AD = DB$ | Algebra |\n\nLet's examine the rows of the table with missing parts in turn.\n\n- Consider row 2. From row 1, we have that $m\\angle ACD = 90^\\circ$. Then, by the Pythagorean theorem applied to $\\triangle ACD$, we have\n$AD^2 = AC^2 + DC^2$.\n- Consider row 5. From the given information, $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CD}$ is the perpendicular bisector of $\\overline{AB}$ and $C$ lies on $\\overline{AB}$. By the definition of a bisector, we have\n$\\overline{CB} \\cong \\overline{AC}$.\n- Consider row 7. From rows 6 and 4, we have that $CB = AC$ and $DB^2 = CB^2 + DC^2$. Therefore, by substituting the first into the second,\n$DB^2 = CB^2 + DC^2 = AC^2 + DC^2$.\n- Consider row 9. From rows 2 and 8, we have\n${\\color{blue}AD^2} = AC^2 + DC^2 \\quad\\text{and}\\quad AC^2 + DC^2 = {\\color{blue}DB^2}$.\nTherefore, by the transitive property of congruence, we have\n${\\color{blue}AD^2} = {\\color{blue}DB^2}$." 13194139533378 17592186045417 true] [17592186203778 1074 "The correct proof is below.\n\n|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | $m\\angle DCB = 90^\\circ$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Given}}$ |\n| 2 | $\\angle DCB$ and $\\angle DCA$ are supplementary | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Linear pair postulate}}$ |\n| 3 | $m\\angle DCB +m\\angle DCA = 180^\\circ$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Definition of supplementary angles}}$ |\n| 4 | $m\\angle DCA =180^\\circ - m\\angle DCB$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Addition principle}}$ |\n| 5 | $m\\angle DCA = 90^\\circ$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Substitution}}$ |\n| 6 | $m\\angle DCB = m\\angle DCA$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Transitive property of equality}}$ |\n| 7 | $\\angle DCB \\cong \\angle DCA$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{Definition of congruent angles}}$ |\n| 8 | $\\overline{AC} \\cong \\overline{CB}$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\