ngles are proportional (CPSTP). In particular, we have that\n$\\dfrac{AC}{EC} = \\dfrac{BC}{DC}$." 13194139533378 17592186045417 true] [17592186203800 1074 "Recall that the $k$th sample moment $M_k$ of a sample $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is given by\n\n$$\nM_k = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i^k\n$$\n\nIn particular, the first and second sample moments are given by\n\n$$\nM_1 = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i \\,, \\qquad M_2 = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i^2\n$$\n\nTo find the method of moments estimate for a parameter, we calculate a sample moment and the corresponding theoretical moment and equate them.\n\nWe are given the sum of the squared values in the sample. So, we can calculate the second sample moment:\n\n$$\nm_2 = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^{n} x_i^2 = \\dfrac{9}{15}=\\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nThe variables $X_i$ follow a Bernoulli distribution. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X_i] = \\theta, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X_i] = \\theta(1-\\theta)\n$$\n\nWe also have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X_i] = \\textrm{E}[X^2_i] - \\left(\\textrm{E}[X_i]\\right)^2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the second theoretical moment as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X_{i}^{2}] &= Var[X_{i}] + (E[X_{i}])^{2} \\\\\n&= θ(1 - θ) + θ^{2} \\\\\n&= θ - θ^{2} + θ^{2} \\\\\n&= θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, equating the sample and theoretical first moments, using the estimator $\\widehat\\theta_{MM}$ in place of $\\theta$, we get the following estimate for $\\theta{:}$\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\theta}_{MM} = \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\dfrac{3}{5}}\n$$" 13194139533378 17592186045417 true] [17592186203801 1074 "Recall that the $k$th sample moment $M_k$ of a sample $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is given by\n\n$$\nM_k = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i^k\n$$\n\nIn particular, the first and second sample moments are given by\n\n$$\nM_1 = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i \\,, \\qquad M_2 = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i^2\n$$\n\nTo find the method of moments estimator for a parameter, we calculate a sample moment and the corresponding theoretical moment and equate them.\n\nWe are given the sum of the squared values in the sample. So, we can calculate the second sample moment:\n\n$$\nm_2 = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^{n} x_i^2 = \\dfrac{140}{5}=28\n$$\n\nWe are given that the variables follow a uniform distribution $U(a,b)$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X_i] = \\dfrac{a+b}{2}, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X_i] = \\dfrac{(b-a)^2}{12}\n$$\n\nWe also have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X_i] = \\textrm{E}[X^2_i] - \\left(\\textrm{E}[X_i]\\right)^2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the second theoretical moment as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X_{i}^{2}] &= Var[X_{i}] + (E[X_{i}])^{2} \\\\\n&= (((θ + 6) - θ)^{2})/(12) + ((θ + (θ + 6))/(2))^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{36}{12} + (θ + 3)^{2} \\\\\n&= 3 + θ^{2} + 6θ + 9 \\\\\n&= θ^{2} + 6θ + 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sample and theoretical second moments and using the estimator $\\widehat\\theta_{MM}$ in place of $\\theta$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ^(^)_{MM}^{2} + 6θ^(^)_{MM} + 12 &= 28\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for $\\widehat\\theta_{MM}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ^(^)_{MM}^{2} + 6θ^(^)_{MM} + 12 &= 28 \\\\\nθ^(^)_{MM}^{2} + 6θ^(^)_{MM} - 16 &= 0 \\\\\n(θ^(^)_{MM} + 8)(θ^(^)_{MM} - 2) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the solutions\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\theta}_{MM}= -8, \\qquad \\widehat{\\theta}_{MM}= 2\n$$\n\nHowever, we discard the negative solution since we are given that $\\theta > 0$. Therefore, our method of moments estimate for $\\theta$ is\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\theta}_{MM}= \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2}\n$$" 13194139533378 17592186045417 true] [17592186203802 1074 "Let us call $n_1, n_2$ the sample sizes of eighth and ninth-grade students, and $\\widehat{\\,p}_1, \\widehat{\\,p}_2$ the proportion of students who vote in student elections in the two samples.\n\nSince $n_1=62$, $n_2=70$,\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}_1=\\dfrac{48}{62}=\\dfrac{24}{31}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}_2=\\dfrac{35}{70} = \\dfrac{1}{2}