 in place of $\\theta$, we get\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\theta}_{MM}^2 + \\widehat{\\theta}_{MM} = 30\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for $\\widehat{\\theta}_{MM}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ^(^)_{MM}^{2} + θ^(^)_{MM} &= 30 \\\\\nθ^(^)_{MM}^{2} + θ^(^)_{MM} - 30 &= 0 \\\\\n(θ^(^)_{MM} + 6)(θ^(^)_{MM} - 5) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the solutions\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\theta}_{MM} = -6, \\qquad \\widehat{\\theta}_{MM} = 5\n$$\n\nHowever, we discard the negative solution since $\\theta > 0$. Therefore, our method of moments estimate for $\\theta$ is\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\theta}_{MM} = \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}5}\n$$" 13194139533378 17592186045417 true] [17592186203811 1074 "Recall that the $k$th sample moment $M_k$ of a sample $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is given by\n\n$$\nM_k = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i^k\n$$\n\nIn particular, the first and second sample moments are given by\n\n$$\nM_1 = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i \\,, \\qquad M_2 = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i^2\n$$\n\nTo find the method of moments estimate for a parameter, we calculate a sample moment and the corresponding theoretical moment and equate them.\n\nWe are given the sum of the squared values in the sample. So, we can calculate the second sample moment:\n\n$$\nm_2 = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^{n} x_i^2 = \\dfrac{30}{15} =2\n$$\n\nWe are given that the variables $X_i$ follow a Geometric distribution. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X_i] = \\dfrac{1}{\\theta}, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X_i] = \\dfrac{1-\\theta}{\\theta^2}\n$$\n\nWe also have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X_i] = \\textrm{E}[X^2_i] - \\left(\\textrm{E}[X_i]\\right)^2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the second theoretical moment as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X_{i}^{2}] &= Var[X_{i}] + (E[X_{i}])^{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 - θ}{θ^{2}} + \\frac{1}{θ^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 - θ}{θ^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the sample and theoretical second moments, using the estimator $\\widehat\\theta_{MM}$ in place of $\\theta$, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2-\\widehat{\\theta}_{MM}}{\\widehat{\\theta}_{MM}^2} = 2\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for $\\widehat{\\theta}_{MM}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2 - θ^(^)_{MM})/(θ^(^)_{MM}^{2}) &= 2 \\\\\n2 - θ^(^)_{MM} &= 2θ^(^)_{MM}^{2} \\\\\n2θ^(^)_{MM}^{2} + θ^(^)_{MM} - 2 &= 0 \\\\\nθ^(^)_{MM} &= (-1 ± \\sqrt{1^{2} - 4(2)(-2)})/(2 \\cdot 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the solutions\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\theta}_{MM} \\approx 0.781, \\qquad \\widehat{\\theta}_{MM} \\approx -1.281\n$$\n\nHowever, we discard the negative solution since $\\theta > 0$. Therefore, our method of moments estimate for $\\theta$ is\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\theta}_{MM} = \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.781}\n$$" 13194139533378 17592186045417 true] [17592186203812 1074 "Recall that the $k$th sample moment $M_k$ of a sample $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is given by\n\n$$\nM_k = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i^k\n$$\n\nIn particular, the first and second sample moments are given by\n\n$$\nM_1 = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i \\,, \\qquad M_2 = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i^2\n$$\n\nTo find the method of moments estimate for a parameter, we calculate a sample moment and the corresponding theoretical moment and equate them.\n\nWe are given the sum of the values in the sample. So, we can calculate the first sample moment:\n\n$$\nm_1 = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^{n} x_i = \\dfrac{1}{7} \\cdot \\dfrac{7}{8} = \\dfrac{1}{8}\n$$\n\nWe are given that the variables $X_i$ follow an Exponential distribution. Therefore, the theoretical first moment is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X_i] = \\dfrac{1}{\\theta}\n$$\n\nFinally, equating the sample and theoretical first moments, using the estimator $\\widehat\\theta_{MM}$ in place of $\\theta$, we get the following estimate for $\\theta{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\widehat{\\theta}_{MM}} = \\dfrac{1}{8} \\quad \\Rightarrow\\quad \\widehat{\\theta}_{MM} = 8\n$$" 13194139533378 17592186045417 true] [17592186203813 1074 "Recall that the $k$th sample moment $M_k$ of a sample $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is given by\n\n$$\nM_k = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i^k\n$$\n\nIn particular, the first and second sample moments are given by\n\n$$\nM_1 = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i \\,, \\qquad M_2 = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i^2\n$$\n\nTo find the method of moments estimator for a parameter, we calculate a sample moment and the corresponding theoretical moment and equate them.\n\nWe are given the sum of the squared values in the sample. So, we can calculate the second sample moment:\n\n$$\nm_2 = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^{n} x_i^2 = \\dfrac{10}{6}=\\dfrac53\n$$\n\nWe are given that the variables follow a binomial distribution $B(m,\\theta)$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X_i] = m\\theta, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X_i] = m\\theta (1-\\theta)\n$$\n\nWe also have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X_i] = \\textrm{E}[X^2_i] - \\left(\\textrm{E}[X_i]\\right)^2\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the second theoretical moment as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X_{i}^{2}] &= Var[X_{i}] + (E[X_{i}])^{2} \\\\\n&= mθ(1 - θ) + m^{2}θ^{2} \\\\\n&= 3θ(1 - θ) + 3^{2}θ^{2} \\\\\n&= 3θ - 3θ^{2} + 9θ^{2} \\\\\n&= 6θ^{2} + 3θ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sample and theoretical second moments and using the estimator $\\widehat\\theta_{MM}$ in place of $\\theta$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6θ^(^)_{MM}^{2} + 3θ^(^)_{MM} = \\frac{5}{3} \\\\\n18θ^(^)_{MM}^{2} + 9θ^(^)_{MM} - 5 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for $\\widehat{\\theta}_{MM}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nθ^(^)_{MM} &= (-9 ± \\sqrt{9^{2} - 4(18)(-5)})/(2(18)) \\\\\nθ^(^)_{MM} &= \\frac{-3 ± 7}{12}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the solutions\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\theta}_{MM}= -\\dfrac{5}{6}, \\qquad \\widehat{\\theta}_{MM}= \\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nHowever, we discard the negative solution since $\\theta > 0$. Therefore, our method of moments estimate for $\\theta$ is\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\theta}_{MM} = \\dfrac 13\n$$" 13194139533378 17592186045417 true] [17592186203814 1074 "Recall that the $k$th sample moment $M_k$ of a sample $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is given by\n\n$$\nM_k = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i^k\n$$\n\nIn particular, the first and second sample moments are given by\n\n$$\nM_1 = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i \\,, \\qquad M_2 = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i^2\n$$\n\nTo find the method of moments estimator for a parameter, we calculate a sample moment and the corresponding theoretical moment and equate them.\n\nWe are given the sum of the values in the sample and the sum of squares. So, we can calculate the first and second sample moments:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm_{1} &= \\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i} = \\frac{70}{10} = 7 \\\\\nm_{2} &= \\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i}^{2} = \\frac{550}{10} = 55\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that the variables $X_i$ follow a normal distribution. Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X_{i}] &= μ \\\\\nE[X_{i}^{2}] &= Var[X_{i}] + (E[X_{i}])^{2} = σ^{2} + μ^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sample and theoretical first and second moments, using the estimators $\\widehat\\mu_{MM}$ and $\\widehat{\\sigma^2}_{MM}$ in place of $\\mu$ and $\\sigma^2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nμ^(^)_{MM} &= 7, \\\\\nσ^{2}^(^)_{MM} + μ^(^)_{MM}^{2} &= 55 \\\\\nσ^{2}^(^)_{MM} + 7^{2} &= 55 \\\\\nσ^{2}^(^)_{MM} &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533378 17592186045417 true] [17592186203835 1074 "A *walk* of length $n$ in a directed graph is a sequence of $n$ edges $e_1,e_2,\\ldots,e_n$ with the associated vertices $v_1,v_2,\\ldots,v_{n+1}$ where the edge $e_i$ is incident on vertices $v_i$ and $v_{i+1}$ (in this order) for each\n\n$$\ni=1,2,\\ldots,n\n$$\n\nThis can be written as follows:\n$v_1 \\overset{e_1}{\\to} v_2 \\overset{e_2}{\\to} v_3 \\overset{e_3}{\\to} \\cdots \\overset{e_n}{\\to} v_{n+1}$\nIf the graph has no multiple (parallel) edges, we can drop the $e_i$'s in the above notation since any corresponding pair of incident vertices will uniquely determine any edge.\n\n$$\nv_1 \\to v_2 \\to v_3 \\to \\cdots \\to v_{n+1}\n$$\n\nA *trail* is a walk in which all edges are distinct.\n\nA *path* is a walk in which all vertices (and, as a result, all edges) are distinct.\n\nWith that in mind, let's list all possible trails from $v_1$ to $v_4{:}$\n\n- $v_1 \\overset{e_1}{\\to} v_2 \\overset{e_5}{\\to} v_4$\n- $v_1 \\overset{e_4}{\\to} v_3 \\overset{e_3}{\\to} v_4$\n- $v_1 \\overset{e_4}{\\to} v_3 \\overset{e_2}{\\to} v_2 \\overset{e_5}{\\to} v_4$\n\nTherefore, there are $3$ distinct trails between the vertices $v_1$ and $v_4$." 13194139533378 17592186045417 true] [17592186203837 1074 "When a transversal cuts a pair of lines, we get four pairs of corresponding angles.\n\n![](images/e8/e830c42b6d7fbca76e0c9d68b6bdb4f91fb5c457fcf693f3557ae4733b773120.png)\n\nSo, in the given diagram, $\\angle FKG$ and $\\angle IJA$ are corresponding angles.\n\nThe **converse corresponding angles postulate** states that if a transversal cuts two lines and the corresponding angles are congruent, then the lines are parallel.\n\nTherefore, $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{GH}$ are parallel by the converse corresponding angles postulate." 13194139533378 17592186045417 true] [17592186204442 1074 "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ the sum of squared deviations from the mean for the data ${x}_{i}$ can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}-\\frac{1}{n}{({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given statistics in the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & =3\\,460-\\frac{1}{25}(250)^{2} \\\\  & =3\\,460-2\\,500 \\\\  & =960.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535595 17592186045417 true] [17592186204443 1074 "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ ${S}_{xy}$ is the sum of products of the deviations of ${x}_{i}$ and ${y}_{i}$ from their respective means, that is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given statistics, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =2.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186204444 1074 "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ ${S}_{yy}$ is the sum of the squared deviations of the data ${y}_{i}$ from the mean $\\overset{―}{y},$ that is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{yy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the sample mean $\\overset{―}{y}:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{16+14+23+17+15}{5}=17.\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the differences between the ${y}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}$, and their squares.\n\n| **$\\,{x}_{i}\\,$** | $5$ | $11$ | $9$ | $12$ | $18$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **$\\,{y}_{i}\\,$** | $16$ | $14$ | $23$ | $17$ | $15$ |\n| **$\\,({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y})\\,$** | $-1$ | $-3$ | $6$ | $0$ | $-2$ |\n| **$\\,({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y})^{2}\\,$** | $1$ | $9$ | $36$ | $0$ | $4$ |\n\nTherefore, ${S}_{yy}$ is\n\n$$\n{S}_{yy}=1+9+36+0+4=50.\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186204445 1074 "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ the statistic ${S}_{xy}$ be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}{y}_{i}-\\frac{1}{n}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i})({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given statistics in the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & =3\\,625-\\frac{1}{25}(250)(265) \\\\  & =3\\,625-2\\,650 \\\\  & =975.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535905 17592186045417 true] [17592186204446 1074 "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ the sum of products of deviations from the means can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}{y}_{i}-\\frac{1}{n}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i})({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the sum of the ${x}_{i}$ and ${y}_{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} & =2+3+9+7+5+4=30, \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i} & =16+15+14+23+18+15=101\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{6}{x}_{i}{y}_{i}=514.$\n\nFinally, substituting in the above formula we get\n\n$$\n{S}_{xy}=514-\\frac{1}{6}(30)(101)=514-505=9.\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186204455 1074 "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ the sum of squared deviations from the mean for the data ${y}_{i}$ can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{yy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y})^{2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i}{y}_{i}^{2}-\\frac{1}{n}{({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the sum of the ${y}_{i}$ data:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{y}_{i}=9+5+7+11+8=40.\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{y}_{i}^{2}=340.$\n\nFinally, by substituting into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n{S}_{yy}=340-\\frac{1}{5}(40)^{2}=340-320=20.\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186208337 1074 "To find the length of a circular arc with central angle $\\theta$ and circumference $C$, we use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Arc Length} = \\frac{\\theta}{360^\\circ} \\cdot C\n$$\n\nThe given circle has a circumference of $C = 50$, and the central angle is\n\n$$\n\\angle EOA = 72^\\circ\n$$\n\nso\n\n$$\n\\theta = 72^\\circ\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of minor arc $\\overset{\\frown}{EA}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nLength(EA^(⌢)) &= \\frac{72^{∘}}{360^{∘}} \\cdot 50 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5} \\cdot 50 \\\\\n&= 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045417 true] [17592186208338 1074 "We are told that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Length}(\\overset{\\frown}{FE}) = \\dfrac{1}{4} \\cdot \\textrm{Length}(\\overset{\\frown}{FG})\n$$\n\nThe diameters $\\overline{FD}$ and $\\overline{GE}$ divide the circle into four arcs: $\\overset{\\frown}{FG},\\, \\overset{\\frown}{GD},\\, \\overset{\\frown}{FE}$, and $\\overset{\\frown}{ED}$.\n\nLet\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Length}(\\overset{\\frown}{FG}) = x\n$$\n\nSince each pair of opposite arcs is congruent, we have:\n\n- $\\textrm{Length}(\\overset{\\frown}{FG}) = \\textrm{Length}(\\overset{\\frown}{ED}) = x$\n- $\\textrm{Length}(\\overset{\\frown}{GD}) = \\textrm{Length}(\\overset{\\frown}{FE}) = \\dfrac{1}{4}x$\n\nAs the total arc length (circumference) is $90\\pi$, we write:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Length}(\\overset{\\frown}{FG}) + \\textrm{Length}(\\overset{\\frown}{GD}) + \\textrm{Length}(\\overset{\\frown}{ED}) + \\textrm{Length}(\\overset{\\frown}{FE}) = 90\\pi\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute each arc length with its expression in terms of $x$ and solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + \\frac{1}{4}x + x + \\frac{1}{4}x &= 90π \\\\\n\\frac{5}{2}x &= 90π \\\\\nx &= 90π \\cdot \\frac{2}{5} \\\\\n&= 36π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of arc $\\overset{\\frown}{GD}$ is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Length}(\\overset{\\frown}{GD}) = \\dfrac{1}{4}x = \\dfrac{1}{4}(36\\pi) = 9\\pi\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045417 true] [17592186208539 1074 "The length of a circular arc with central angle $\\theta$ and circumference $C$ is given by:\n\n$$\n\\text{Arc Length} = \\dfrac{\\theta}{360^\\circ} \\cdot C\n$$\n\nWe are given:\n\n- Arc length of $\\overset{\\frown}{PQ}$ is $8\\textrm{m}$\n- The central angle subtending the arc is $\\theta = 80^\\circ$\n\nTherefore, using the arc length formula, we find that the circumference $C$ of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nLength(PQ^(⌢)) &= \\frac{80^{∘}}{360^{∘}} \\cdot C \\\\\n8 &= \\frac{2}{9} \\cdot C \\\\\nC &= 8 \\cdot \\frac{9}{2} \\\\\n&= 36m\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533379 17592186045417 true] [17592186208540 1074 "We are told:\n\n- The radius of the circle is $r = 9$\n- The arc length of $\\overset{\\frown}{LM}$ is $\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}$\n\nFirst, we compute the circumference of the circle:\n\n$$\nC = 2\\pi r = 2\\pi(9) = 18\\pi\n$$\n\nHence, the fraction of the circumference that this arc represents is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle \\text{Fraction} & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{( 3 \\pi  / 2 )}{18 \\pi }} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{3 \\pi }{2}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{18 \\pi }} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{3 \\cancel{\\pi}}{2}} \\cdot  {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{18 \\cancel{\\pi}}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{12}} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, arc $\\overset{\\frown}{LM}$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{12}\n$$\n\nof the circle's circumference." 13194139536831 17592186045436 true] [17592186216879 1074 "![](images/d4/d43c8834102c4d670436f42fdb287b19933e256b4a2b725cd3a92b04eda55739.png)\n\nThe central angle $\\angle AOB$ subtends arc $\\overset{\\frown}{AB}$, and since the measure of a central angle is equal to the measure of its intercepted arc, we have\n\n$$\nm \\overset{\\frown}{AB} = 82\\,^\\circ\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045417 true] [17592186216880 1074 "![](images/9d/9d29a77e0fcdc633d55ec14ab4e34689c3c09770d195fd337f350ed05e7d1e4a.png)\n\nSince $\\overline{OA}$ and $\\overline{OB}$ are both radii of the circle, triangle $OAB$ is isosceles with $OA = OB$. Thus, the base angles $\\angle OAB$ and $\\angle OBA$ are congruent.\n\nIn $\\triangle OAB$, the measures of the interior angles sum to $180^\\circ{:}$\n\n$$\nm\\angle OAB + m\\angle OBA + m\\angle AOB = 180^\\circ\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\nm\\overset{\\frown}{AB} = 84^\\circ\n$$\n\nSince $\\angle AOB$ is the central angle corresponding to arc $\\overset{\\frown}{AB}$, we have\n\n$$\nm\\angle AOB = 84^\\circ\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nm\\angle OAB = m\\angle OBA\n$$\n\nwe let each equal $x$. Substituting, we solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + x + 84^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n2x + 84^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n2x &= 96^{∘} \\\\\nx &= 48^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the measure of $\\angle OAB$ is\n\n$$\nm\\angle OAB = 48^\\circ\n$$" 13194139533381 17592186045417 true] [17592186216933 1074 "We'll solve this problem by considering the following ratio:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{arc length} \\::\\: \\textrm{proportion of a full turn}\n$$\n\nThe arc length of $\\overset{\\frown}{ACB}$ is $7\\pi$, which represents\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{84^\\circ}{360^\\circ} = \\dfrac{7}{30}\n$$\n\nof a full turn. Therefore, we have the following ratio of arc length to full turn proportion:\n\n$$\n7\\pi \\::\\: \\dfrac{7}{30}\n$$\n\nThus, the remaining arc $\\overset{\\frown}{ADB}$ corresponds to\n\n$$\n1 - \\dfrac{7}{30} = \\dfrac{23}{30}\n$$\n\nof a full turn. Our goal is to manipulate the above ratio to yield the arc length corresponding to this proportion.\n\nDividing our ratio by $7$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}7π &:\\frac{7}{30} \\\\ \\frac{7π}{7} &:\\frac{1}{30} \\\\ π &:\\frac{1}{30}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis tells us that an arc length of $\\pi$ corresponds to one-thirtieth of a full turn.\n\nFinally, multiplying this ratio by $23$, we have\n\n$$\n23 \\cdot \\pi \\::\\: \\dfrac{23 \\cdot 1}{30}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n23\\pi \\::\\: \\dfrac{23}{30}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of the arc $\\overset{\\frown}{ADB}$ is $23\\pi$." 13194139533381 17592186045417 true] [17592186216948 1074 "We'll solve this problem by considering the following ratio:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{arc length} \\::\\: \\textrm{proportion of a full turn}\n$$\n\nThe arc length of $\\overset{\\frown}{PTQ}$ is $20\\pi$, which represents\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{100^\\circ}{360^\\circ}=\\dfrac{5}{18}\n$$\n\nof a full turn. We can express this relationship as the following ratio:\n\n$$\n20\\pi \\::\\: \\dfrac{5}{18}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the arc length of $\\overset{\\frown}{QRS}$ is $15\\pi$. Our goal is to manipulate the above ratio to yield the full turn proportion corresponding to this arc length, and use that to compute the value of $y$.\n\nDividing the above ratio by $20$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}20π &:\\frac{5}{18} \\\\ \\frac{20π}{20} &:\\frac{5}{18 \\cdot 20} \\\\ π &:\\frac{1}{72}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying this ratio by $15$, we have\n\n$$\n15\\cdot \\pi \\::\\: \\dfrac{15\\cdot 1}{72}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n15\\pi \\::\\: \\dfrac{5}{24}\n$$\n\nThis means that the arc length $\\overset{\\frown}{QRS}$ represents\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{24}\n$$\n\nof a full circle. Hence, the measure of the corresponding central angle is\n\n$$\ny^\\circ=\\dfrac{5}{24}\\cdot 360^\\circ=75^\\circ\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=75$." 13194139533381 17592186045417 true] [17592186216980 1074 "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\textrm{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{Q}\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right)=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)=\\exp\\left(\\int g(x)\\,\\textrm{d}x\\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\exp(x)$ denotes the exponential function $e^x.$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{Q}\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}-\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}\\right)=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\dfrac{1}{x}$ is a function of $x$ only, we can conclude that the integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int {x}^{-1}\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our equation by the integrating factor, we arrive at the following exact differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x[(x+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x+(xy+\\frac{2}{x})\\,\\text{d}y] & =0 \\\\ \\underbrace{({x}^{2}+x{y}^{2})}_{\\overset{~}{P}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\underbrace{({x}^{2}y+2)}_{\\overset{~}{Q}}\\,\\text{d}y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor this exact equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\widetilde{P}(x,y)=x^2+xy^2,\\qquad\n\\widetilde{Q}(x,y)=x^2y+2.\n$$\n\nTo solve the equation, let's first integrate $\\widetilde{P}(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $y,$ which we'll call $F(y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{P}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\int ({x}^{2}+x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{2}+F(y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's integrate $\\widetilde{Q}(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $x,$ which we'll call $G(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\int ({x}^{2}y+2)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{2}+2y+G(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we must have\n\n$$\nF(y)=2y,\\qquad G(x)=\\frac{x^3}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^3}{3}+\\frac{x^2y^2}{2}+2y=c.\n$$" 13194139536958 17592186576411 true] [17592186217792 1074 "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)=\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}⁡(x)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{x}.$\n\nWe are given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=\\frac{3}{x}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{3}{x}$ is a function of $x$ only, we can conclude that the integrating factor for the equation is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int 3{x}^{-1}\\text{ }\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our equation by the integrating factor, we arrive at the following exact differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}[(\\frac{6{y}^{2}}{x}+4{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+(4y+3x{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})\\,\\text{d}y] & =0 \\\\ \\underbrace{(6{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4{x}^{3}{y}^{3})}_{\\overset{~}{P}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\underbrace{(4{x}^{3}y+3{x}^{4}{y}^{2}+1)}_{\\overset{~}{Q}}\\,\\text{d}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor this exact equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)=6{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4{x}^{3}{y}^{3},\\,\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)=4{x}^{3}y+3{x}^{4}{y}^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nTo solve the equation, let's first integrate $\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $y,$ which we'll call $F(y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{P}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\int (6{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4{x}^{3}{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+{x}^{4}{y}^{3}+F(y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's integrate $\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $x,$ which we'll call $G(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\int (4{x}^{3}y+3{x}^{4}{y}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+{x}^{4}{y}^{3}+y+G(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we must have\n\n$$\nF(y)=y,\\,G(x)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+{x}^{4}{y}^{3}+y=c.\n$$" 13194139536958 17592186576411 true] [17592186221108 1074 "![](images/9d/9d29a77e0fcdc633d55ec14ab4e34689c3c09770d195fd337f350ed05e7d1e4a.png)\n\nSince $\\overline{OA}$ and $\\overline{OB}$ are both radii of the circle, triangle $OAB$ is isosceles with $OA = OB$. Thus, the base angles $\\angle OAB$ and $\\angle OBA$ are congruent.\n\nIn $\\triangle OAB$, the measures of the interior angles sum to $180^\\circ{:}$\n\n$$\nm\\angle OAB + m\\angle OBA + m\\angle AOB = 180^\\circ\n$$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\nm\\overset{\\frown}{AB} = 84^\\circ\n$$\n\nSince $\\angle AOB$ is the central angle corresponding to arc $\\overset{\\frown}{AB}$, we have\n\n$$\nm\\angle AOB = 84^\\circ\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nm\\angle OAB = m\\angle OBA\n$$\n\nwe let each equal $x$. Substituting, we solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + x + 84^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n2x + 84^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n2x &= 96^{∘} \\\\\nx &= 48^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the measure of $\\angle OAB$ is\n\n$$\nm\\angle OAB = 48^\\circ\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045417 true] [17592186221111 1074 "To find the length of a circular arc with central angle $\\theta$ and radius $r$, we use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Arc Length} = \\dfrac{\\theta}{360^\\circ} \\cdot 2\\pi r\n$$\n\nSince $\\overline{OC}, \\overline{OD}$, and $\\overline{OE}$ are all radii of the circle, triangle $COD$ is isosceles with\n\n$$\n\\overline{OC} = \\overline{OD}\n$$\n\nThus, the base angles are equal:\n\n$$\nm\\angle ODC = m\\angle OCD = 25^\\circ\n$$\n\nUsing the triangle angle sum formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠ODC + m∠OCD + m∠COD &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠COD &= 180^{∘} - (m∠OCD + m∠COD) \\\\\n&= 180^{∘} - (25^{∘} + 25^{∘}) \\\\\n&= 180^{∘} - 50^{∘} \\\\\n&= 130^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the central angle of arc $\\overset{\\frown}{CD}$ is\n\n$$\n\\theta = 130^\\circ\n$$\n\nand the radius of the circle is $r = 18$, the length of arc $\\overset{\\frown}{CD}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nLength(CD^(⌢)) &= \\frac{130^{∘}}{360^{∘}} \\cdot 2π \\cdot 18 \\\\\n&= \\frac{13}{36} \\cdot 36π \\\\\n&= 13π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533382 17592186045417 true] [17592186228874 1074 "We'll solve this problem by considering the following ratio:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{arc length} \\::\\: \\textrm{proportion of a full turn}\n$$\n\nThe arc length of $\\overset{\\frown}{ACB}$ is $7\\pi$, which represents\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{84^\\circ}{360^\\circ} = \\dfrac{7}{30}\n$$\n\nof a full turn. Therefore, we have the following ratio of arc length to full turn proportion:\n\n$$\n7\\pi \\::\\: \\dfrac{7}{30}\n$$\n\nThus, the remaining arc $\\overset{\\frown}{ADB}$ corresponds to\n\n$$\n1 - \\dfrac{7}{30} = \\dfrac{23}{30}\n$$\n\nof a full turn. Our goal is to manipulate the above ratio to yield the arc length corresponding to this proportion.\n\nDividing our ratio by $7$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}7π &:\\frac{7}{30} \\\\ \\frac{7π}{7} &:\\frac{1}{30} \\\\ π &:\\frac{1}{30}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis tells us that an arc length of $\\pi$ corresponds to one-thirtieth of a full turn.\n\nFinally, multiplying this ratio by $23$, we have\n\n$$\n23 \\cdot \\pi \\::\\: \\dfrac{23 \\cdot 1}{30}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n23\\pi \\::\\: \\dfrac{23}{30}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of the arc $\\overset{\\frown}{ADB}$ is $23\\pi$." 13194139533384 17592186045417 true] [17592186228875 1074 "We'll solve this problem by considering the following ratio:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{arc length} \\::\\: \\textrm{proportion of a full turn}\n$$\n\nThe arc length of $\\overset{\\frown}{PTQ}$ is $20\\pi$, which represents\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{100^\\circ}{360^\\circ}=\\dfrac{5}{18}\n$$\n\nof a full turn. We can express this relationship as the following ratio:\n\n$$\n20\\pi \\::\\: \\dfrac{5}{18}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the arc length of $\\overset{\\frown}{QRS}$ is $15\\pi$. Our goal is to manipulate the above ratio to yield the full turn proportion corresponding to this arc length, and use that to compute the value of $y$.\n\nDividing the above ratio by $20$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}20π &:\\frac{5}{18} \\\\ \\frac{20π}{20} &:\\frac{5}{18 \\cdot 20} \\\\ π &:\\frac{1}{72}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying this ratio by $15$, we have\n\n$$\n15\\cdot \\pi \\::\\: \\dfrac{15\\cdot 1}{72}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n15\\pi \\::\\: \\dfrac{5}{24}\n$$\n\nThis means that the arc length $\\overset{\\frown}{QRS}$ represents\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{24}\n$$\n\nof a full circle. Hence, the measure of the corresponding central angle is\n\n$$\ny^\\circ=\\dfrac{5}{24}\\cdot 360^\\circ=75^\\circ\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=75$." 13194139533384 17592186045417 true] [17592186233042 1074 "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{5}({v}^{2}+36) & ={log}_{5}(-16v-28) \\\\ {v}^{2}+36 & =-16v-28 \\\\ {v}^{2}+16v+64 & =0 \\\\ (v+8)^{2} & =0 \\\\ v & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation:\n\n- Substituting $v=-8$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{5}({v}^{2}+36) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{5}(-16v-28) \\\\ {log}_{5}((-8)^{2}+36) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{5}(-16(-8)-28) \\\\ {log}_{5}(100) & ={log}_{5}(100).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $v=-8$ is a valid solution." 13194139533386 17592186045417 true] [17592186233044 1074 "We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OC}=c=3i+8j+3k, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{OD}=d=-5i-6k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates of $c+d$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c+d & =(3i+8j+3k)+(-5i-6k) \\\\  & =-2i+8j-3k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|c+d| & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{8}^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+64+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{77}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045417 true] [17592186233045 1074 "We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OR}=r=-i+j+4k, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{OS}=s=8i-2j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates of $3r+s$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3r+s & =3(-i+j+4k)+(8i-2j) \\\\  & =5i+j+12k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|3r+s| & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{12}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25+1+144} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{170}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045417 true] [17592186233046 1074 "First, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OA}=a=6i+7j+6k, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{OB}=b=8i+9j+5k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ as the difference of position vectors, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{OB}-\\overset{\\to }{OA} \\\\  & =b-a \\\\  & =8i+9j+5k-(6i+7j+6k) \\\\  & =2i+2j-k \\\\  & =⟨2,2,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{|\\,\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\,|}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\,| & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+4+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{|\\,\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\overset{\\to }{AB} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ⟨2,2,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{1}{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045417 true] [17592186233139 1074 "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}(x-2) & ={log}_{2}(2x-3) \\\\ x-2 & =2x-3 \\\\ -x & =-1 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=1$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}(x-2) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{2}(2x-3) \\\\ {log}_{2}(1-2) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{2}(2\\cdot 1-3) \\\\ {log}_{2}(-1) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{2}(-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, ${log}_{2}(-1)$ is not a real number.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the equation has no real solutions." 13194139533386 17592186045417 true] [17592186233140 1074 "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡({x}^{2}+4) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x+4) \\\\ {x}^{2}+4 & =2x+4 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x & =0 \\\\ x(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation:\n\n- Substituting $x=0$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡({x}^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x+4) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({0}^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}⁡(2(0)+4) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡(4) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(4).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=0$ is a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $x=2$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡({x}^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x+4) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({2}^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}⁡(2(2)+4) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡(8) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(8).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=2$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=0,2.$" 13194139533397 17592186045417 true] [17592186233265 1074 "The length of $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is equal to the distance between $P$ and $Q.$\n\nThe distance $d$ between two points $P({x}_{1},{y}_{1},{z}_{1})$ and $Q({x}_{2},{y}_{2},{z}_{2})$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}+({z}_{2}-{z}_{1})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSince $P$ and $Q$ have coordinates $\\underbrace{(6,\\,0,\\,1)}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{, }{y}_{1}\\text{, }{z}_{1}\\text{)}}$ and $\\underbrace{(-1,4,2)}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{, }{y}_{2}\\text{, }{z}_{2}\\text{)}},$ respectively, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}PQ & =\\sqrt{(-1-6)^{2}+(4-0)^{2}+(2-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-7)^{2}+{4}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{66}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536958 17592186576411 true] [17592186233292 1074 "Relative to $∠TVU,$ \n\n- the opposite side is $\\overset{―}{TU},$ and its length is $1,$\n\n- the adjacent side is $\\overset{―}{UV},$ and its length is $2,$ and\n\n- the hypotenuse is $\\overset{―}{TV},$ and its length is $\\sqrt{5}.$" 13194139533386 17592186045417 true] [17592186233366 1074 "We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OP}=p=i+2j, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{OQ}=q=-2i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as the difference of position vectors, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =q-p \\\\  & =(-2i+j)-(i+2j) \\\\  & =-3i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045417 true] [17592186233382 1074 "To compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{u}+\\overset{\\to }{v} & =⟨-3,1⟩+⟨4,6⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3+4,1+6⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,7⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045417 true] [17592186233383 1074 "To compute $\\overset{\\to }{A}+\\overset{\\to }{B},$ we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{A}+\\overset{\\to }{B} & =⟨10,5⟩+⟨8,-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨18,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045417 true] [17592186233386 1074 "First, we isolate the term $\\frac{\\sqrt{3{v}^{2}-4v+12}}{2}$ by adding $2$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3{v}^{2}-4v+12}}{2}-2 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3{v}^{2}-4v+12}}{2} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we isolate the square root by multiplying by $2$ on both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3{v}^{2}-4v+12}}{2} & =2 \\\\ \\sqrt{3{v}^{2}-4v+12} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the square root is isolated, we can proceed as usual. Squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3{v}^{2}-4v+12} & =4 \\\\ (\\sqrt{3{v}^{2}-4v+12})^{2} & ={4}^{2} \\\\ 3{v}^{2}-4v+12 & =16 \\\\ 3{v}^{2}-4v-4 & =0 \\\\ (3v+2)(v-2) & =0 \\\\ v & =-\\frac{2}{3},\\,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the result by plugging it into the original equation:\n\n- For $v=-\\frac{2}{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3{(-\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}-4(-\\frac{2}{3})+12}}{2}-2 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{8}{3}+12}}{2}-2 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{16}}{2}-2 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{2}-2 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ 2-2 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $v=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3({2}^{2})-4(2)+12}}{2}-2 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{12-8+12}}{2}-2 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{16}}{2}-2 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{2}-2 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ 2-2 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth $v=-\\frac{2}{3}$ and $v=2$ satisfy the original equation, so we can accept them both as solutions.\n\nTherefore, the solutions to $\\frac{\\sqrt{3{v}^{2}-4v+12}}{2}-2=0$ are $v=-\\frac{2}{3}$ and $v=2.$" 13194139533386 17592186045417 true] [17592186233395 1074 "To compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{U}+\\overset{\\to }{V} & =⟨1,3⟩+⟨6,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1+6,3+3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨7,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045417 true] [17592186233442 1074 "To compute $-4\\overset{\\to }{A},$ we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4\\overset{\\to }{A} & =-4\\cdot ⟨4,\\frac{1}{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(-4)\\cdot 4,(-4)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-16,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045417 true] [17592186233467 1074 "We have to solve $h(x)=5,$ or equivalently, $\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{x}+3=5.$\n\nFirst, we isolate the term $\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{x}$ by subtracting $3$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{x}+3 & =5 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{x} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we isolate the square root by multiplying by $\\frac{3}{2}$ on both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{x} & =2 \\\\ \\sqrt{x} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the square root is isolated, we can proceed as usual. Squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x} & =3 \\\\ (\\sqrt{x})^{2} & ={3}^{2} \\\\ x & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the result by plugging it into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{9}+3 & \\overset{?}{=}5 \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 3+3 & \\overset{?}{=}5 \\\\ 2+3 & =5\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $x=9$ satisfies the original equation, so we can accept it as a solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to $\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{x}+3=5$  is $x=9.$" 13194139533386 17592186045417 true] [17592186233596 1074 "We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OC}=c=-i+3j, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{OD}=d=i-2j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing $\\overset{\\to }{CD}$ as the difference of position vectors, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{CD} & =\\overset{\\to }{OD}-\\overset{\\to }{OC} \\\\  & =d-c \\\\  & =(i-2j)-(-i+3j) \\\\  & =2i-5j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{\\to }{CD}| & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{29}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045417 true] [17592186233598 1074 "From the picture, we obtain $P(1,1)$ and $Q(2,3).$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OP}=p=i+j, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{OQ}=q=2i+3j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as the difference of position vectors, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =q-p \\\\  & =(2i+3j)-(i+j) \\\\  & =i+2j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045417 true] [17592186233602 1074 "Remember that in general, if a point $Q$ has coordinates $({q}_{x},{q}_{y},{q}_{z}),$ then its position vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OQ}=q={q}_{x}i+{q}_{y}j+{q}_{z}k.\n$$\n\nHere, the point $Q$ has the coordinates $(1,1,2),$ so its position vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OQ}=q=i+j+2k.\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045417 true] [17592186233664 1074 "The length of $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is equal to the distance between $A$ and $B.$\n\nThe distance $d$ between two points $A({x}_{1},{y}_{1},{z}_{1})$ and $B({x}_{2},{y}_{2},{z}_{2})$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}+({z}_{2}-{z}_{1})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSince $A$ and $B$ have coordinates $\\underbrace{(1,\\,3,\\,-4)}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{, }{y}_{1}\\text{, }{z}_{1}\\text{)}}$ and $\\underbrace{(-2,1,2)}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{, }{y}_{2}\\text{, }{z}_{2}\\text{)}},$ respectively, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\sqrt{(-2-1)^{2}+(1-3)^{2}+(2-(-4))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+4+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{49} \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536958 17592186576411 true] [17592186233680 1074 "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ the sum of products of deviations from the means can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}{y}_{i}-\\frac{1}{n}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i})({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the sum of the ${x}_{i}$ and ${y}_{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} & =1+7+3+4+6+10=31, \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i} & =11+3+7+2+1+18=42\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{6}{x}_{i}{y}_{i}=247.$\n\nFinally, by substituting in the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n{S}_{xy}=247-\\frac{1}{6}(31)(42)=247-217=30.\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045417 true] [17592186233682 1074 "Given a frequency table containing continuous data split into $K$ classes, we can estimate the variance by treating all observations within each class as though they were equal to the midpoint of that class. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }_{n}^{2}≈\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{K}{f}_{i}\\cdot ({m}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${f}_{i}$ is the frequency of the $i$th class,\n\n- ${m}_{i}$ is the midpoint of the $i$th class,\n\n- $n$ is the total number of observations, and\n\n- $\\overset{―}{x}$ is the estimated mean, calculated using the class midpoints.\n\nTo find the variance, we add columns to our table for the midpoints of each interval, the squared deviations of the midpoints from the mean, and the products of the squared deviations and frequencies.\n\n| Range | ${f}_{i}$ | ${m}_{i}$ | ${({m}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}$ | ${f}_{i}{({m}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0\\le x<10$ | $8$ | $5$ | $72.25$ | $578$ |\n| $10\\le x<20$ | $7$ | $15$ | $2.25$ | $15.75$ |\n| $20\\le x<30$ | $5$ | $25$ | $132.25$ | $661.25$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nThen, we divide the sum of all the values in the last column by $20$ (the total number of observations):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{n}^{2} & ≈\\frac{578+15.75+661.25}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{1255}{20} \\\\  & =62.75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the estimated standard deviation is the square root of the estimated variance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{n} & ≈\\sqrt{62.75}≈7.9\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186233699 1074 "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡(3x+1) & ={log}_{2}⁡(5-x) \\\\ 3x+1 & =5-x \\\\ 4x & =4 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=1$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡(3(1)+1) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{2}⁡(5-1) \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡(4) & ={log}_{2}⁡(4)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=1$ is a valid solution." 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186233737 1074 "We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OR} & =r=6j-2k, \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{OS} & =s=-5i+j+k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing $\\overset{\\to }{SR}$ as the difference of position vectors, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{SR} & =\\overset{\\to }{OR}-\\overset{\\to }{OS} \\\\  & =r-s \\\\  & =(6j-2k)-(-5i+j+k) \\\\  & =5i+5j-3k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{\\to }{SR}| & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{5}^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25+25+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{59}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045417 true] [17592186233767 1074 "Squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{2a+4}{5}}=2 \\\\ {(\\sqrt{\\frac{2a+4}{5}})}^{2} & ={2}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{2a+4}{5} & =4 \\\\ 2a+4 & =20 \\\\ a & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the result by plugging it into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{2\\cdot 8+4}{5}} & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{\\frac{16+4}{5}} & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{4} & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\\\ 2 & =2\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $a=8$ satisfies the original equation, so we can accept it as a solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to $\\sqrt{\\frac{2a+4}{5}}=2$  is $a=8.$" 13194139533386 17592186045417 true] [17592186233826 1074 "Remember that in general, if a point $U$ has coordinates $({u}_{x},{u}_{y},{u}_{z}),$ then its position vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OU}=u={u}_{x}i+{u}_{y}j+{u}_{z}k.\n$$\n\nHere, the point $U$ has the coordinates $(-1,-3,0),$ so its position vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OU}=(-1)i+(-3)j+0k=-i-3j.\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045417 true] [17592186233827 1074 "Remember that in general, if a point $B$ has coordinates $({b}_{x},{b}_{y},{b}_{z}),$ then its position vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OB}=b=⟨{b}_{x},{b}_{y},{b}_{z}⟩=\\begin{bmatrix}{b}_{x} \\\\ {b}_{y} \\\\ {b}_{z}\\end{bmatrix}={b}_{x}\\,i+{b}_{y}\\,j+{b}_{z}\\,k.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & =-7i+2j+5k \\\\  & =-7\\cdot i+2\\cdot j+5\\cdot k \\\\  & =⟨-7,2,5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535474 17592186045443 true] [17592186234021 1074 "Squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{8x-5} & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ {(\\sqrt{8x-5})}^{2} & =(\\sqrt{3})^{2} \\\\ 8x-5 & =3 \\\\ 8x & =8 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the result by plugging it into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{8\\cdot 1-5} & \\overset{?}{=}\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{8-5} & \\overset{?}{=}\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & =\\sqrt{3}\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $x=1$ satisfies the original equation, so we can accept it as a solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to $\\sqrt{8x-5}=\\sqrt{3}$  is $x=1.$" 13194139533386 17592186045417 true] [17592186234022 1074 "First, we isolate the term $-\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}}{6}$ by subtracting $8$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8-\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}}{6} & =6 \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}}{6} & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we isolate the square root by multiplying by $-6$ on both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}}{6} & =-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{x+2} & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the square root is isolated, we can proceed as usual. Squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x+2} & =12 \\\\ (\\sqrt{x+2})^{2} & ={12}^{2} \\\\ x+2 & =144 \\\\ x & =142.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the result by plugging it into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8-\\frac{\\sqrt{142+2}}{6} & \\overset{?}{=}6 \\\\ 8-\\frac{\\sqrt{144}}{6} & \\overset{?}{=}6 \\\\ 8-\\frac{12}{6} & \\overset{?}{=}6 \\\\ 6 & =6\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $x=142$ satisfies the original equation, so we can accept it as a solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to $8-\\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}}{6}=6$  is $x=142.$" 13194139533386 17592186045417 true] [17592186234023 1074 "Squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-x-4} & =4 \\\\ {(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-x-4})}^{2} & ={4}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-x-4 & =16 \\\\ {x}^{2}-x-20 & =0 \\\\ (x-5)(x+4) & =0 \\\\ x & =-4,\\,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the result by plugging the solutions into the original equation. \n\n- For $x=-4$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}-(-4)-4} & \\overset{?}{=}4 \\\\ \\sqrt{16+4-4} & \\overset{?}{=}4 \\\\ \\sqrt{16} & \\overset{?}{=}4 \\\\ 4 & =4\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $x=5$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{5}^{2}-5-4} & \\overset{?}{=}4 \\\\ \\sqrt{25-5-4} & \\overset{?}{=}4 \\\\ \\sqrt{16} & \\overset{?}{=}4 \\\\ 4 & =4\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe values $x=-4,\\,5$ satisfy the original equation, so we can accept them as solutions.\n\nTherefore, the solutions to $\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-x-4}=4$  are $x=-4$ and $x=5.$" 13194139533386 17592186045417 true] [17592186234024 1074 "Squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-8x+37} & =5 \\\\ {(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-8x+37})}^{2} & ={5}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-8x+37 & =25 \\\\ {x}^{2}-8x+12 & =0 \\\\ (x-6)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =2,\\,6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the result by plugging the solutions into the original equation. \n\n- For $x=2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{2}^{2}-8\\cdot 2+37} & \\overset{?}{=}5 \\\\ \\sqrt{4-16+37} & \\overset{?}{=}5 \\\\ \\sqrt{25} & \\overset{?}{=}5 \\\\ 5 & =5\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $x=6$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{6}^{2}-8\\cdot 6+37} & \\overset{?}{=}5 \\\\ \\sqrt{36-48+37} & \\overset{?}{=}5 \\\\ \\sqrt{25} & \\overset{?}{=}5 \\\\ 5 & =5\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe values $x=2,\\,6$ satisfy the original equation, so we can accept them as solutions.\n\nTherefore, the solutions to $\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-8x+37}=5$  are $x=2$ and $x=6.$" 13194139533386 17592186045417 true] [17592186234032 1074 "The given repeating decimal can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2.3\\overset{―}{43} & =2.343\\,43... \\\\  & =2.3+0.043+0.000\\,43+0.000\\,004\\,3+… \\\\  & =2.3+\\underbrace{\\frac{43}{1\\,000}+\\frac{43}{100\\,000}+\\frac{43}{10\\,000\\,000}+⋯.}_{\\text{geometric series}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that we have a geometric series where the first term is ${a}_{1}=\\frac{43}{1\\,000}$ and the common ratio is $\\frac{1}{100}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2.3\\overset{―}{43} & =2.3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =2.3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{43}{1\\,000}{(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n-1} \\\\  & =2.3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{43\\cdot 100}{1\\,000}{(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n} \\\\  & =2.3+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{43}{10}{(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536958 17592186576411 true] [17592186234275 1074 "Remember that in general, if a point $D$ has coordinates $({d}_{x},{d}_{y},{d}_{z}),$ then its position vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OD}=d=⟨{d}_{x},{d}_{y},{d}_{z}⟩=\\begin{bmatrix}{d}_{x} \\\\ {d}_{y} \\\\ {d}_{z}\\end{bmatrix}={d}_{x}\\,i+{d}_{y}\\,j+{d}_{z}\\,k.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =4j-i \\\\  & =-1\\cdot i+4\\cdot j+0\\cdot k \\\\  & =⟨-1,4,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535475 17592186045443 true] [17592186234292 1074 "First, we isolate the square root by adding $4$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-3x+6}-4 & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-3x+6} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the square root is isolated, we can proceed as usual. Squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-3x+6} & =4 \\\\ {(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-3x+6})}^{2} & ={4}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-3x+6 & =16 \\\\ {x}^{2}-3x-10 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)(x-5) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,\\,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the results by plugging them into the original equation.\n\n- For $x=-2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}-3(-2)+6}-4 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\sqrt{4+6+6}-4 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\sqrt{16}-4 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ 4-4 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ 0 & =0.\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For $x=5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{5}^{2}-3(5)+6}-4 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\sqrt{25-15+6}-4 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ \\sqrt{16}-4 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ 4-4 & \\overset{?}{=}0 \\\\ 0 & =0.\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth $x=-2$ and $x=5$ satisfy the original equation, so we can accept them both as solutions.\n\nTherefore, the solutions to $\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-3x+6}-4=0$ are $x=-2$ and $x=5.$" 13194139533386 17592186045417 true] [17592186234387 1074 "We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OP}=p=i-2k, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{OQ}=q=3i-4j+2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can express $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as the difference of position vectors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =q-p \\\\  & =(3i-4j+2k)-(i-2k) \\\\  & =2i-4j+4k\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045417 true] [17592186234388 1074 "We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OM}=m=5i-k, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{ON}=n=8i+3j-k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can express $\\overset{\\to }{MN}$ as the difference of position vectors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{MN} & =n-m \\\\  & =(8i+3j-k)-(5i-k) \\\\  & =3i+3j+0k \\\\  & =⟨3,3,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045417 true] [17592186234391 1074 "Our vector is shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/9b/9b2d2c2a56e095f5bed1d5b128196cf2e959e6a75559e08c9956be5fbc507dfd.png)\n\nWe have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OA}=a=-2i+j, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{OB}=b=i-5j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, from $△OAB,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{AO}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =b-a \\\\  & =(i-5j)-(-2i+j) \\\\  & =3i-6j.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045417 true] [17592186234392 1074 "Our vector is shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/7b/7b2a5e4f68dda0e74d18eb9ede16b52ab48d0d7810717d09bc77fb115145146a.png)\n\nWe have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OA}=a=3i-5j, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{OB}=b=-4i-j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, from $△OAB,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{BA} & =\\overset{\\to }{BO}+\\overset{\\to }{OA} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OB}+\\overset{\\to }{OA} \\\\  & =a-b \\\\  & =(3i-5j)-(-4i-j) \\\\  & =7i-4j.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045417 true] [17592186234420 1074 "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{4}⁡({x}^{2}+4) & ={log}_{4}⁡(-3x+2) \\\\ {x}^{2}+4 & =-3x+2 \\\\ {x}^{2}+3x+2 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)(x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation:\n\n- Substituting $x=-2$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{4}⁡({x}^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{4}⁡(-3x+2) \\\\ {log}_{4}⁡((-2)^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{4}⁡(-3(-2)+2) \\\\ {log}_{4}⁡(8) & ={log}_{4}⁡(8).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-2$ is a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $x=-1$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{4}⁡({x}^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{4}⁡(-3x+2) \\\\ {log}_{4}⁡((-1)^{2}+4) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{4}⁡(-3(-1)+2) \\\\ {log}_{4}⁡(5) & ={log}_{4}⁡(5).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-1$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=-2,-1.$" 13194139533432 17592186045417 true] [17592186234467 1074 "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the variance of the data set is given by \n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}$ is the mean of the data set and $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}$ is the mean of the squares of the points in the data set.\n\nFirst, let's compute $\\overset{―}{x}.$ There are $n=9$ points in our data set, and we know that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{x}_{i}=27,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot 27 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}.$ We know that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{x}_{i}^{2}=117,$ so we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot 117 \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =13-{(3)}^{2} \\\\  & =13-9 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533386 17592186045417 true] [17592186235343 1074 "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+2b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $2b.$ So, we first draw the vector $2b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/a8/a8309dc45ac43c6642010e744de5a062d6575d48ba85bd642df39dd261f47422.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $2b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=2|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-2a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =2b-2a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+2b=-2a+2b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-2\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045417 true] [17592186235344 1074 "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a-2b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $-2b.$ So, we first draw the vector $-2b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/40/40a0137d6227212db9b7ef9f349029838f3f51398790ec5e1c304e12c3743a0f.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $-2b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=\\frac{1}{2}|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-\\frac{1}{2}a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =-2b-\\frac{1}{2}a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a-2b=-\\frac{1}{2}a-2b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045417 true] [17592186235345 1074 "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a-3b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $-3b.$ So, we first draw the vector $-3b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/24/2484cf9fdda85c5c516babe6814f34c369ebdd91d6f100f0ce0fe4102e3c90a6.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $-3b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=3|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-3a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =-3b-3a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a-3b=-3a-3b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-3\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045417 true] [17592186235439 1074 "We have to solve $g(x)=3,$ or equivalently, $2\\sqrt{x}-1=3.$\n\nFirst, we isolate the term $2\\sqrt{x}$ by adding $1$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{x}-1 & =3 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{x} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we isolate the square root by multiplying by $\\frac{1}{2}$ on both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{x} & =4 \\\\ \\sqrt{x} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the square root is isolated, we can proceed as usual. Squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x} & =2 \\\\ (\\sqrt{x})^{2} & ={2}^{2} \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the result by plugging it into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{4}-1 & \\overset{?}{=}3 \\\\ 2\\cdot 2-1 & \\overset{?}{=}3 \\\\ 4-1 & =3\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $x=4$ satisfies the original equation, so we can accept it as a solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to $2\\sqrt{x}-1=3$  is $x=4.$" 13194139533387 17592186045417 true] [17592186235489 1074 "From the picture, the points $A$ and $B$ have coordinates $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(3,-2)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(1,4),$ respectively.\n\nTo find the length of $\\overset{―}{AB},$ we use the distance formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{―}{AB}| & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(1-3)^{2}+(4-(-2))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{40} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045417 true] [17592186235492 1074 "Previously we found that the line $\\overset{↔}{AB}$ has the equation\n\n$$\ny=-3x+7.\n$$\n\nNow, we need to decide which inequality symbol to use. \n\n* Since the line is *dashed*, we will use a *strict* inequality. \n\n* Since the shaded region is *below* the line, the inequality refers to the $y$-values that are *less than* those on the line.\n\nTherefore, the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny<-3x+7.\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045417 true] [17592186235493 1074 "Previously, we found that $\\overset{↔}{AB}$ has equation\n\n$$\ny=-3x+7.\n$$\n\nAt the $x$-intercept, the $y$-coordinate is equal to $0.$ So, we can substitute $y=0$ into the equation and then solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3x+7 \\\\ 0 & =-3x+7 \\\\ -7 & =-3x \\\\ \\frac{-7}{-3} & =\\frac{-3x}{-3} \\\\ \\frac{7}{3} & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercept is $x=\\frac{7}{3}.$ The coordinates of the $x$-intercept are $(\\frac{7}{3},0).$" 13194139533387 17592186045417 true] [17592186235495 1074 "Perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other. Therefore, since $\\overset{↔}{AB}$ has the slope $m=-3,$ our perpendicular line must have a slope of \n\n$$\n{m}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{(-3)}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, our perpendicular line has the slope ${m}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}$ and passes through the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(3,-5).$ \n\nFinally, using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & ={m}_{1}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-5) & =\\frac{1}{3}(x-3) \\\\ y+5 & =\\frac{1}{3}x-1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3}x-6\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045417 true] [17592186235509 1074 "Remember that in general, if a point $A$ has coordinates $({a}_{x},{a}_{y},{a}_{z}),$ then its position vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OA}=⟨{a}_{x},{a}_{y},{a}_{z}⟩.\n$$\n\nHere, the point $P$ has coordinates $(0,0,3),$ so its position vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =⟨0,0,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe point $Q$ has coordinates $(0,-3,2),$ so its position vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q & =⟨0,-3,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe point $R$ has coordinates $(0,4,3),$ so its position vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨0,4,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045417 true] [17592186235510 1074 "According to the triangle law of addition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{QR} & =\\overset{\\to }{OR}-\\overset{\\to }{OQ} \\\\ x & =r-q.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045417 true] [17592186235520 1074 "Previously, we found that the general solution is\n\n$$\nT=25+K{e}^{-0.03t}.\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now apply the condition $T(0)=28$ to find $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}28 & =25+K\\,{e}^{-0.03(0)} \\\\ 28 & =25+K\\,{e}^{0} \\\\ K & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the required particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{T}(t)=25+3{e}^{-0.03t}.\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045417 true] [17592186235521 1074 "From the previous part, we know that\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{T}(t)=25+3{e}^{-0.03t}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the laws of exponents and the algebra of limits to rewrite the given limit, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to \\,-\\infty }\\overset{^}{T}(t) & ={lim}_{t\\to \\,-\\infty }(25+3{e}^{-0.03t}) \\\\  & =25+3{lim}_{t\\to \\,-\\infty }{e}^{-0.03t} \\\\  & =25+3\\,{e}^{\\,\\,{lim}_{t\\to \\,-\\infty }(-0.03t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{\\,\\,{lim}_{t\\to \\,-\\infty }(-0.03t)}=\\infty ,$ and the positive factor of $3$ does not affect the sign of the limit, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to -\\,\\infty }\\overset{^}{T}(t)=\\infty .\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045417 true] [17592186235552 1074 "The center of mass of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{M},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{M}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n* $M={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A$ is the mass of the lamina,\n\n* ${M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\,$ is the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis, and\n\n* ${M}_{y}={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A$ is the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis.\n\nIn part 1, we found that the mass of the plate is\n\n$$\nM=24.\n$$\n\nAlso, from parts 2 and 3, we know that \n\n$$\n{M}_{x}=36,\\,{M}_{y}=48.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{y}}{M}=\\frac{48}{24}=2,\\,\\,\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{{M}_{x}}{M}=\\frac{36}{24}=\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the center of mass of the lamina is $(2,\\frac{3}{2}).$" 13194139533387 17592186045417 true] [17592186235565 1074 "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a-4b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $-4b.$ So, we first draw the vector $-4b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/13/139a4d4312ecbe42cd3a3859af4f4d7b93f8bbc54548ac5a0244c04e7ba5287b.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $-4b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=3|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-3a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =-4b-3a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a-4b=-3a-4b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-3.\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045417 true] [17592186235566 1074 "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a-2b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $-2b.$ So, we first draw the vector $-2b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/64/646b8f1322f815e786f82eb8cfb3fa9e84a3c8d588c94910b0b702b607bfb2d8.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $-2b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=\\frac{3}{2}|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-\\frac{3}{2}a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =-2b-\\frac{3}{2}a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a-2b=-\\frac{3}{2}a-2b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-\\frac{3}{2}\n$$" 13194139533387 17592186045417 true] [17592186235567 1074 "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+4b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $4b.$ So, we first draw the vector $4b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/6a/6af6aff8a4aa111e6eb