\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}=5-3\\text{i}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}({\\lambda }_{1}-{\\lambda }_{2}) & =\\frac{2}{3}(5+3\\text{i}-(5-3\\text{i})) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(6\\text{i}) \\\\  & =4\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045417 true] [17592186238014 1074 "Since ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4+6\\text{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of ${\\lambda }_{1}.$\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}=-4-6\\text{i}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}({\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2}) & =\\frac{3}{2}(-4+6\\text{i}+(-4-6\\text{i})) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(-8) \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045417 true] [17592186238015 1074 "Since ${\\lambda }_{1}=-5+4\\text{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of ${\\lambda }_{1}.$\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}=-5-4\\text{i}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}({\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2}) & =\\frac{2}{5}((-5+4\\text{i})+(-5-4\\text{i})) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}(-10) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533394 17592186045417 true] [17592186240824 1074 "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nThe picture shows a half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis.\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- If we project the vector $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ onto the $xy$-plane for every point $P$ on our surface, the resulting vector makes an angle of $2$ radians with the positive $x$-axis. Therefore, we must have $\\theta =2$ for every point on the surface.\n\n- On the other hand, $r$ and $z$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains.\n\nTherefore, the equation of the surface in cylindrical polar coordinates is $\\theta =2.$" 13194139533395 17592186045417 true] [17592186240825 1074 "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nThe picture shows a half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis.\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- If we project the vector $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ onto the $xy$-plane for every point $P$ on our surface, the resulting vector makes an angle of $\\frac{11\\pi }{8}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis. Therefore, we must have $\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{8}$ for every point on the surface.\n\n- On the other hand, $r$ and $z$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains.\n\nTherefore, the equation of the surface in cylindrical polar coordinates is $\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{8}.$" 13194139533395 17592186045417 true] [17592186240828 1074 "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{8}\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that if we project the vector $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ onto the $xy$-plane for every point $P$ on our surface, the resulting vector makes an angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{8}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis. So, we must have $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{8}$ for every point on the surface. On the other hand, $r$ and $z$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains.\n\nTherefore, our surface is a half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis, shown below.\n\n![](images/01/0118d67477cd0665d4ae5cbebbf3c3d6f3a6a24f4944977d4301cbaf99d3ecaa.png)" 13194139533395 17592186045417 true] [17592186240830 1074 "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that if we project the vector $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ onto the $xy$-plane for every point $P$ on our surface, the resulting vector makes an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis. So, we must have $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ for every point on the surface. On the other hand, $r$ and $z$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains.\n\nTherefore, our surface is a half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis, shown below.\n\n![](images/4e/4eefd7f719ccb0c34407d6c02ef1adeb283f45e80ed6a95525661b84e39b3a8d.png)" 13194139533395 17592186045417 true] [17592186240831 1074 "The position of a point in cylindrical polar coordinates is written as $(r,\\theta ,z),$ where \n\n- $r\\ge 0$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ are the usual plane polar coordinates, and\n\n- $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ is the usual Cartesian $z$-coordinate.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our equation:\n\n$$\n\\theta =5\n$$\n\nThis equation requires that if we project the vector $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ onto the $xy$-plane for every point $P$ on our surface, the resulting vector makes an angle of $5$ radians with the positive $x$-axis. So, we must have $\\theta =5$ for every point on the surface. On the other hand, $r$ and $z$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains.\n\nTherefore, our surface is a half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis, shown below.\n\n![](images/a7/a7e2f3f2ad24c353bc3dc63d82971b4888ccffc198479456beddd7fea185bd07.png)" 13194139533395 17592186045417 true] [17592186240891 1074 "First, we let $f(x)={x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-2x+20.$ \n\nSince $f(x)$ has real coefficients and ${x}_{1}=3-\\text{i}$ is a root of $f(x),$ the complex conjugate \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{{x}_{1}}=\\overset{―}{3-\\text{i}}=3+\\text{i}\n$$\n\nmust also be a root of $f(x).$\n\nNow, according to the factor theorem, \n$(x-(3-\\text{i}))(x-(3+\\text{i}))$\nis a factor of $f(x).$ Expanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-(3-\\text{i}))(x-(3+\\text{i})) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-(3-\\text{i}+(3+\\text{i)})x+(3-\\text{i})(3+\\text{i}) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x+({3}^{2}-(\\text{i})^{2}) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x+10 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by ${x}^{2}-6x+10$ using polynomial division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,x+2 \\\\  & {x}^{2}-6x+10\\,\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-\\phantom{2}2x+20} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+10x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2{x}^{2}-12x+20 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{2{x}^{2}-12x+20}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n     \n\nThus, $f(x)=({x}^{2}-6x+10)(x+2).$ \n\nTo find the remaining root, we set the linear factor equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2=0\\,⟹\\,x=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the roots are $x=-2,3\\pm \\text{i}.$" 13194139534482 17592186045417 true] [17592186240892 1074 "First, we let $f(x)={x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-11x-116.$\n\nSince $f(x)$ has real coefficients and ${x}_{1}=-5+2\\text{i}$ is a root of $f(x),$ the complex conjugate \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{{x}_{1}}=\\overset{―}{-5+2\\text{i}}=-5-2\\text{i}\n$$\n\nmust also be a root of $f(x).$\n\nNow, according to the factor theorem, \n$(x-(-5+2\\text{i}))(x-(-5-2\\text{i}))$\nis a factor of $f(x).$ Expanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-(-5+2\\text{i}))(x-(-5-2\\text{i})) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-(-5+2\\text{i}+(-5-2\\text{i)})x+(-5+2\\text{i})(-5-2\\text{i}) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+10x+((-5)^{2}-(2\\text{i})^{2}) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+10x+29 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by ${x}^{2}+10x+29$ using polynomial division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,x-4 \\\\  & {x}^{2}+10x+29\\,\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,{x}^{3}+\\phantom{1}6{x}^{2}-11x-116} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{x}^{3}+10{x}^{2}+29x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,-4{x}^{2}+40x-116 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,{-4{x}^{2}+40x-116}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n        \n\nThus, $f(x)=({x}^{2}+10x+29)(x-4).$ \n\nTo find the remaining root, we set the linear factor equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-4=0\\,⟹\\,x=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the roots are $x=4,-5\\pm 2\\text{i}.$" 13194139534482 17592186045417 true] [17592186240998 1074 "The complement of $C,$ denoted $\\overset{―}{C},$ consists of every element in the universal set $U$ that is not in the set $C.$\n\nThe elements of $U$ that are not in $C$ are $10,20,30,40,$ and $50.$\n\nTherefore, the complement of $C$ is\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{C}={10,20,30,40,50}.\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045417 true] [17592186240999 1074 "The complement of $B,$ denoted $\\overset{―}{B},$ consists of every element in the universal set $U$ that is not in the set $B.$\n\nThe elements of $U$ that are not in $B$ are $2,$ $6,$ $10,$ $14,$ and $18.$\n\nTherefore, the complement of $B$ is\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{B}=\\{2,6,10,14,18\\}.\n$$" 13194139534465 17592186045417 true] [17592186241000 1074 "The complement of $E,$ denoted $\\overset{―}{E},$ consists of every element in the universal set $U$ that is not in the set $E.$\n\nThe only elements of $U$ that are not in $E$ are $s,$ $t,$ $u,$ and $v.$\n\nTherefore, the complement of $E$ is\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{E}={s,t,u,v}.\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045417 true] [17592186241001 1074 "The complement of $X,$ denoted $\\overset{―}{X},$ consists of every element in the universal set $U$ that is not in the set $X.$\n\nThe elements of $U$ that are not in $X$ are $4,$ $8,$ $16,$ and $32.$\n\nTherefore, the complement of $X$ is\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}={4,8,16,32}.\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045417 true] [17592186241004 1074 "The complement of $X,$ denoted $\\overset{―}{X},$ consists of every element in the universal set $U$ that is not in the set $X.$\n\nThe universal set $U$ consists of the integers $0$ through $50,$ while the set $X$ consists of the integers $40$ through $50.$\n\nSo, the only elements of $U$ that are not in $A$ are the integers $0$ through $39.$\n\nTherefore, the complement of $X$ is\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}={0,1,2,…,39}.\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045417 true] [17592186241005 1074 "The complement of $Y,$ denoted $\\overset{―}{Y},$ consists of every element in the universal set $U$ that is not in the set $Y.$\n\nThe universal set $U$ consists of the letters $a$ through $z,$ while the set $Y$ consists of the letters $a$ through $j.$\n\nSo, the only elements of $U$ that are not in $Y$ are the letters $k$ through $z.$\n\nTherefore, the complement of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{Y}=\\{k,l,m,…,z\\}.\n$$" 13194139534465 17592186045417 true] [17592186241006 1074 "The complement of $X,$ denoted $\\overset{―}{X},$ consists of every element in the universal set $U$ that is not in the set $X.$\n\nThe universal set $U$ consists of the integer numbers while the set $X$ consists of the negative integers and $0.$\n\nSo, the only elements of $U$ that are not in $X$ are the natural numbers.\n\nTherefore, the complement of $X$ is\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}={1,2,3,…}.\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045417 true] [17592186241075 1074 "The complement of $Q,$ denoted $\\overset{―}{Q},$ consists of all the elements in $U$ that are not in $Q.$ To represent $\\overset{―}{Q},$ we will shade in all the area of $U$ that is *outside* of $Q,$ as follows:\n\n![](images/c9/c94ed2827be8a72e798295f16870b4873940f5dffe5b8e17786985a97ccbacb0.png)\n\nThe elements in the shaded area are $♣,♡,$ and $△.$ These are the elements of $U$ that are not in $Q.$ Therefore, the complement of $Q$ is\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{Q}={♣,♡,△}.\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045417 true] [17592186241127 1074 "The complement of $A,$ denoted $\\overset{―}{A},$ consists of every element in the universal set $U$ that is not in the set $A.$\n\nThe universal set $U$ consists of the integers from $-8$ through $8,$ while the set $A$ consists of the odd numbers from  $-7$ through $7.$\n\nSo, the only elements of $U$ that are not in $A$ are the even numbers from $-8$ through $8.$\n\nTherefore, the complement of $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{A}={-8,-6,-4,…,8}.\n$$" 13194139533395 17592186045417 true] [17592186243889 1074 "First, we let $f(x)={x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}+14{x}^{2}-8x+40.$ \n\nSince $f(x)$ has real coefficients and ${x}_{1}=1+3\\text{i}$ is a root of $f(x),$ the complex conjugate \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{{x}_{1}}=\\overset{―}{1+3\\text{i}}=1-3\\text{i}\n$$\n\nmust also be a root of $f(x).$\n\nNow, according to the factor theorem, \n$(x-(1+3\\text{i}))(x-(1-3\\text{i}))$\nis a factor of $f(x).$ Expanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-(1+3\\text{i}))(x-(1-3\\text{i})) & ={x}^{2}-(1+3\\text{i})x-(1-3\\text{i})x+(1+3\\text{i})(1-3\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-2x+({1}^{2}-(3\\text{i})^{2}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-2x+10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by ${x}^{2}-2x+10$ using polynomial division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{x}^{2}+4 \\\\  & {x}^{2}-2x+10\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,{x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}+14{x}^{2}-8x+40\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\,\\,{x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}+10{x}^{2}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,4{x}^{2}-8x+40 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\,\\,\\,\\,4{x}^{2}-8x+40}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $f(x)=({x}^{2}-2x+10)({x}^{2}+4).$\n\nTo find the other two roots, we solve ${x}^{2}+4=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the roots of our quartic equation are $x=1\\pm 3\\text{i},\\,\\pm 2\\text{i}.$" 13194139533396 17592186045417 true] [17592186244076 1074 "First, we let $f(x)={x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+9x-9.$ \n\nSince $f(x)$ has real coefficients and ${x}_{1}=-3\\text{i}$ is a root of $f(x),$ the complex conjugate \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{{x}_{1}}=\\overset{―}{-3\\text{i}}=3\\text{i}\n$$\n\nmust also be a root of $f(x).$\n\nNow, according to the factor theorem, \n$(x-3\\text{i})(x-(-3\\text{i}))$\nis a factor of $f(x).$ Expanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3\\text{i})(x-(-3\\text{i})) & ={x}^{2}+(3\\text{i})(-3\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by ${x}^{2}+9$ using polynomial division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,x-1\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & {x}^{2}+9\\,\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+9x-9\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{x}^{3}+9x}_{―} \\\\  & -{x}^{2}\\,\\,-9 \\\\  & \\,{-\\,{x}^{2}\\,\\,-9}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nThus, $f(x)=({x}^{2}+9)(x-1).$ \n\nTo find the remaining root, we set the linear factor equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-1=0\\,⟹\\,x=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the roots are $x=1,\\pm 3\\text{i}.$" 13194139533396 17592186045417 true] [17592186244125 1074 "First, we let $f(x)={x}^{4}-6{x}^{3}+10{x}^{2}-30x+25.$ \n\nSince $f(x)$ has real coefficients and ${x}_{1}=\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i}$ is a root of $f(x),$ the complex conjugate \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{{x}_{1}}=\\overset{―}{\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i}}=-\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i}\n$$\n\nmust also be a root of $f(x).$\n\nNow, according to the factor theorem, \n$(x-\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i})(x-(-\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i}))$\nis a factor of $f(x).$ Expanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i})(x-(-\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{i