rix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\overset{A-1I\\,}{\\to }0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe other vectors do not satisfy this condition." 13194139535476 17592186045443 true] [17592186247336 1074 "We want to find a \"path\" from $X$ to $W$ consisting only of the vectors $a,b,c,$ and $d.$  \n\nAccording to the triangle law of addition,\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{XW}=\\overset{\\to }{XY}+\\overset{\\to }{YW},\n$$\n\nas shown below. \n\n![](images/47/4758b51fd3dc076059d60e944de0ad8375bb304240365959d4c6cae94f95a535.png)\n\n \n\nNow, from the original diagram, we have that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{XY}=-a,\\,\\overset{\\to }{YW}=d.\n$$\n\nLet's add these to our diagram.\n\n![](images/e9/e942d61c1f51c36dc6cd04f88ff5e37f538920a34c1af5bc780c47691567b668.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{XW} & =\\overset{\\to }{XY}+\\overset{\\to }{YW} \\\\  & =-a+d \\\\  & =d-a.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533397 17592186045417 true] [17592186253291 1074 "The equation of a circle with radius $r,$ centered at the origin, is \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the length of the diameter $\\overset{―}{XY}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XY & =\\sqrt{(-1-1)^{2}+(2-(-2))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+16} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\overset{―}{XY}$ is a diameter, the radius is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{XY}{2}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{2}=\\sqrt{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={(\\sqrt{5})}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533399 17592186045417 true] [17592186253335 1074 "Let's consider $△BCD.$ We can see that $\\overset{―}{BC}$ is the height corresponding to the base $\\overset{―}{CD}$ and that $CD=12\\text{ in}.$ \n\nSince the area of $△BCD$ is $42{\\text{ in}}^{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2} \\\\ 42 & =\\frac{CD\\cdot BC}{2} \\\\ 42 & =\\frac{12\\cdot BC}{2} \\\\ 84 & =12\\cdot BC \\\\ BC & =\\frac{84}{12} \\\\ BC & =7\\text{ in}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that $\\overset{―}{BC}$ is also the height of $△ABC$ corresponding to the base $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ Therefore, we find the area of $△ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{AB\\cdot BC}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot 7}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{35}{2} \\\\  & =17.5{\\text{ in}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533399 17592186045417 true] [17592186253354 1074 "Let's sketch the situation.\n\n![](images/c4/c4fd419e12879af4910e5d8364426c14342c3dadb970d8bbc43d31213a6ff947.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The dotted line is the *perpendicular bisector* of the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$\n\n- All the points on this perpendicular bisector are equidistant to $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Our task is to find the point $Q$ on this line that's closest to $P.$\n\nFirst, we create a function $S$ which represents the *squared* distance between the point $P(6,7)$ and a general point $Q(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-6)^{2}+(y-7)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-12x-14y+85\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required point $Q(x,y)$ is equidistant to the points $A(0,1)$ and $B(0,6).$ So, by writing down $(QA)^{2}=(QB)^{2},$ we have the following constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{(x-0)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}}_{(QA)^{2}} & =\\underbrace{(x-0)^{2}+(y-6)^{2}}_{(QB)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe make $y$ the subject in the constraint equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-0)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =(x-0)^{2}+(y-6)^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2y+1 & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-12y+36 \\\\ \\cancel{{x}^{2}}+\\cancel{{y}^{2}}-2y+1 & =\\cancel{{x}^{2}}+\\cancel{{y}^{2}}-12y+36 \\\\ 10y & =35 \\\\ y & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our expression for $S(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-12x-14y+85 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-12x-14(\\frac{7}{2})+85 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-12x+\\frac{193}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the first derivative,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =2x-12,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve ${S}^{′}(x)=0$ to find the stationary point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 2x-12 & =0 \\\\ x & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's verify that this stationary point is a minimum. Taking the second derivative, we find\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(6)=2>0.\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the stationary point $x=6$ indeed minimizes the squared distance. \n\nFinally, the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{7}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the required point is $(6,\\frac{7}{2}).$" 13194139536958 17592186576411 true] [17592186253422 1074 "To find the $x$-coordinate of $A$, we set $y=0$ in the equation of the line and solve for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(0)+x & =12 \\\\ x & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to find the $y$-coordinate of $B$, we set $x=0$ and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y-0 & =12 \\\\ 3y & =12 \\\\ y & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $A$ and $B$ have the coordinates $(12,0)$ and $(0,4)$, respectively.\n\nIf the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter of the circle, then the midpoint must be the center of the circle. Using the midpoint formula, we find that the center of the circle is at\n\n$$\n(\\frac{12+0}{2},\\frac{0+4}{2})=(6,2).\n$$\n\nNow, to find the radius of the circle, we compute the distance between the center $(6,2)$ and a point on the circle, say $A(12,0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{(6-12)}^{2}+(2-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-6)}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{40} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, since the circle has center $(6,2)$ and radius $r=2\\sqrt{10},$ the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-6)}^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & ={(2\\sqrt{10})}^{2} \\\\ (x-6)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533399 17592186045417 true] [17592186253423 1074 "To find the $x$-coordinate of $A$, we set $y=0