256375 1074 "![](images/d5/d585c949d7de8cc3fa2d62bfb60990af12242c32ef3390e563f7e627844c5a88.png)\n\nIn $△ORT$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OR=3|r|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OR}$ and $r$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OR}=3r.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $RT=2|s|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{RT}$ has the opposite direction to $s$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{RT}=-2s.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\overset{\\to }{OR}+\\overset{\\to }{RT} \\\\  & =3r-2s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $t=\\lambda r+\\mu s=3r-2s.$ \n\nSince $s∦r,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =3$ and $\\mu =-2$" 13194139533400 17592186045417 true] [17592186256377 1074 "![](images/a9/a9dcb3e3cd825cf24103f3636b79418531f8b8a14a5f6c83b9b94780fdf8739b.png)\n\nIn $△OPR$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OP=4|p|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ and $p$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OP}=4p.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $PR=2|q|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ has the opposite direction to $q$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PR}=-2q.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR} \\\\  & =4p-2q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q=4p-2q.$\n\nSince $p∦q,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =4$ and $\\mu =-2$" 13194139533400 17592186045417 true] [17592186256378 1074 "![](images/6c/6c9273c0b89d4c06c8a8591b8e8e94b0c83b2d1d4bd04b64ed8cb0a185619b5f.png)\n\nIn $△OJL$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OJ=|j|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OJ}$ has the opposite direction to $j$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=-j.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $JL=2|k|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{JL}$ has the opposite direction to $k$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{JL}=-2k.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l & =\\overset{\\to }{OJ}+\\overset{\\to }{JL} \\\\  & =-j-2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $l=\\lambda j+\\mu k=-j-2k.$\n\nSince $j∦k,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-1$ and $\\mu =-2$" 13194139533400 17592186045417 true] [17592186256379 1074 "![](images/cc/ccc52cbcbf17db97ad3849f157b5d6ab3e10f59846e2cd4b75507fc0a37c3c98.png)\n\nIn $△OAC$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OA=3|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OA}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=-3a.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $AC=2|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $b$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=-2b.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =-3a-2b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+\\mu b=-3a-2b.$ \n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-3$ and $\\mu =-2$" 13194139533400 17592186045417 true] [17592186256381 1074 "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=\\overset{\\to }{OH}+\\overset{\\to }{HJ}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OH}=h,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{HJ}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OHI,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{HI} & =\\overset{\\to }{HO}+\\overset{\\to }{OI} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OH}+\\overset{\\to }{OI} \\\\  & =-h+i \\\\  & =i-h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $HJ:JI=3:7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{HJ}=\\frac{3}{10}\\overset{\\to }{HI}=\\frac{3}{10}(i-h).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OJ} & =\\overset{\\to }{OH}+\\overset{\\to }{HJ} \\\\  & =h+\\frac{3}{10}(i-h) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{10}h+\\frac{3}{10}i.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045417 true] [17592186256383 1074 "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OW}=\\overset{\\to }{OU}+\\overset{\\to }{UW}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OU}=u,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{UW}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OUV,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{UV} & =\\overset{\\to }{UO}+\\overset{\\to }{OV} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OU}+\\overset{\\to }{OV} \\\\  & =-u+v \\\\  & =v-u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $UW:WV=7:2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{UW}=\\frac{7}{9}\\overset{\\to }{UV}=\\frac{7}{9}(v-u).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OW} & =\\overset{\\to }{OU}+\\overset{\\to }{UW} \\\\  & =u+\\frac{7}{9}(v-u) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}u+\\frac{7}{9}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045417 true] [17592186256386 1074 "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OP}=\\overset{\\to }{OM}+\\overset{\\to }{MP}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OM}=m,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{MP}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OMN,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{MN} & =\\overset{\\to }{MO}+\\overset{\\to }{ON} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OM}+\\overset{\\to }{ON} \\\\  & =-m+n \\\\  & =n-m.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $MP:PN=2:1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{MP}=\\frac{2}{3}\\overset{\\to }{MN}=\\frac{2}{3}(n-m).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OP} & =\\overset{\\to }{OM}+\\overset{\\to }{MP} \\\\  & =m+\\frac{2}{3}(n-m) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}m+\\frac{2}{3}n.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045417 true] [17592186256419 1074 "We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OA}=a=i-k, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{OB}=b=j+k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates of $a+2b$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+2b & =(i-k)+2(j+k) \\\\  & =i+2j+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a+2b| & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045417 true] [17592186256582 1074 "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AT}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=a,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{AT}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OAB,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{AO}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-a+b \\\\  & =b-a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $AT:TB=1:6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{AT}=\\frac{1}{7}\\overset{\\to }{AB}=\\frac{1}{7}(b-a).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OT} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AT} \\\\  & =a+\\frac{1}{7}(b-a) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{7}a+\\frac{1}{7}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045417 true] [17592186256611 1074 "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OZ}=\\overset{\\to }{OX}+\\overset{\\to }{XZ}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OX}=a,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{XZ}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OXY,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{XY} & =\\overset{\\to }{XO}+\\overset{\\to }{OY} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OX}+\\overset{\\to }{OY} \\\\  & =-a+b \\\\  & =b-a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $XZ:ZY=5:2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{XZ}=\\frac{5}{7}\\overset{\\to }{XY}=\\frac{5}{7}(b-a).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OZ} & =\\overset{\\to }{OX}+\\overset{\\to }{XZ} \\\\  & =a+\\frac{5}{7}(b-a) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{7}a+\\frac{5}{7}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045417 true] [17592186256624 1074 "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OC}=\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=a,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{AC}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OAB,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{AO}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-a+b \\\\  & =b-a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $AC:CB=5:1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{AC}=\\frac{5}{6}\\overset{\\to }{AB}=\\frac{5}{6}(b-a).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OC} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =a+\\frac{5}{6}(b-a) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}a+\\frac{5}{6}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045417 true] [17592186256745 1074 "The complement of $S,$ denoted $\\overset{―}{S},$ consists of every element in the universal set $U$ that is not in the set $S.$\n\nThe universal set $U$ consists of the integers $1$ through $100,$ while the set $S$ consists of the odd integers $1$ through $99.$\n\nSo, the only elements of $U$ that are not in $S$ are the even integers $2$ through $100.$\n\nTherefore, the complement of $S$ is\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{S}={2,4,6,...,100}.\n$$" 13194139533400 17592186045417 true] [17592186259521 1074 "The complement of $X,$ denoted $\\overset{―}{X},$ consists of every element in the universal set $U$ that is not in the set $X.$\n\nThe elements of $U$ that are not in $X$ are $0,$$1,$ $2,$ $3,$ $4,$ $5,$ $6,$ $7,$ and $8.$\n\nTherefore, the complement of $X$ is\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}={0,1,2,...,8}.\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045417 true] [17592186259531 1074 "The complement of $D,$ denoted $\\overset{―}{D},$ consists of every element in the universal set $U$ that is not in the set $D.$\n\nThe elements of $U$ that are not in $D$ are $b,$ $c,$ $e,$ $f,$ and $h.$\n\nTherefore, the complement of $D$ is\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{D}={b,c,e,f,h}.\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045417 true] [17592186259571 1074 "For a function $f(x,y),$ the double integral of $f$ over a rectangular region $R$ is the unique number $I$ (if it exists) such that\n\n$$\nL(f,P)\\le I\\le U(f,P)\n$$\n\nover all partitions $P$ of $R.$\n\nFirst, notice that ${\\overset{¯}{x}}_{i}=\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})$ will always lie between ${x}_{i-1}$ and ${x}_{i}$, and similarly ${\\overset{¯}{y}}_{j}=\\frac{1}{2}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})$ will always lie between ${y}_{j-1}$ and ${y}_{j}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n-2{x}_{i}-5{y}_{j}\\le -2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})-5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})\\le -2{x}_{i-1}-5{y}_{j-1}\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to \n\n$$\n-2{x}_{i}-5{y}_{j}\\le -({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})-\\frac{5}{2}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})\\le -2{x}_{i-1}-5{y}_{j-1}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying this by $Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}$ and applying the double summation gives:\n\n$$\nL(f,P)\\le {\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[-({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})-\\frac{5}{2}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})]Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\le U(f,P)\n$$\n\nSeparating the sums using the addition and constant multiple rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & \\le -{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}-\\frac{5}{2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\  & \\le U(f,P).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the hint, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} & =({0}^{2}-(-4)^{2})(-2-(-5))=-48 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} & =\\frac{5}{2}((-2)^{2}-(-5)^{2})(0-(-4))=-210\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P)\\le -(-48)-(-210)\\le U(f,P)\\,⟹\\,L(f,P)\\le 258\\le U(f,P)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=258.\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045417 true] [17592186259572 1074 "For a function $f(x,y),$ the double integral of $f$ over a rectangular region $R$ is the unique number $I$ (if it exists) such that\n\n$$\nL(f,P)\\le I\\le U(f,P)\n$$\n\nover all partitions $P$ of $R.$\n\nFirst, notice that ${\\overset{¯}{x}}_{i}=\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})$ will always lie between ${x}_{i-1}$ and ${x}_{i}$, and similarly ${\\overset{¯}{y}}_{j}=\\frac{1}{2}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})$ will always lie between ${y}_{j-1}$ and ${y}_{j}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3-4{x}_{i}-6{y}_{j} & \\le -3-4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})-6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j}) \\\\  & \\le -3-4{x}_{i-1}-6{y}_{j-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to \n\n$$\n-3-4{x}_{i}-6{y}_{j}\\le -3-2({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})-3({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})\\le -3-4{x}_{i-1}-6{y}_{j-1}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying this by $Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}$ and applying the double summation gives:\n\n$$\nL(f,P)\\le {\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[-3-2({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})-3({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})]Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\le U(f,P)\n$$\n\nSeparating the sums using the addition and constant multiple rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & \\le -3{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\  & \\,-2{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\  & \\,-3{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\  & \\le U(f,P).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the hint, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} & =2({2}^{2}-(-1)^{2})(2-(-2))=24 \\\\ 3{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} & =3({2}^{2}-(-2)^{2})(2-(-1))=0 \\\\ 3{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} & =3(2-(-1))(2-(-2))=36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P)\\le -36-24-0\\le U(f,P)\\,⟹\\,L(f,P)\\le -60\\le U(f,P)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=-60.\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045417 true] [17592186259599 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that we can express the given transformation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2u+3v+2 \\\\ v-2u\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\,2u+3v \\\\ -2u+v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{u}+\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe transformation $T$ maps the endpoints of $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ to the endpoints of $\\overset{―}{{P}^{′}{Q}^{′}}.$ So, to find the image of $\\overset{―}{PQ},$ we find the images of each endpoint.\n\nTo quickly carry out this calculation, we set up the following matrices:\n\n- The matrix $U$ containing the endpoints of our segment:\n\n$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ that has the same shape as $U$ and with the vector $b$ in each column:\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe image of $u$ under $T$ is given by $Au+b.$ So, by applying $T$ to $U,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AU+B & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 8 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 10 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the endpoints of the image in the $xy$-plane are $(-5,3)$ and $(10,0).$\n\nDrawing this segment in the $xy$-plane, we get the following: \n\n![](images/e4/e4bea3edd3fcde575e68e36e08fc4186863b12450802eb0ee37c841975983486.png)" 13194139536958 17592186576411 true] [17592186259607 1074 "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that we can express the given transformation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2u+2v-2 \\\\ v-u+2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2u+2v \\\\ -u+v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{u}+\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe transformation $T$ maps the endpoints of $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ to the endpoints of $\\overset{―}{{P}^{′}{Q}^{′}}.$ So, to find the image of $\\overset{―}{PQ},$ we find the images of each endpoint.\n\nTo quickly carry out this calculation, we set up the following matrices:\n\n- The matrix $U$ containing the endpoints of our segment:\n\n$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ that has the same shape as $U$ and with the vector $b$ in each column:\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe image of $u$ under $T$ is given by $Au+b.$ So, by applying $T$ to $U,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AU+B & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 10 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the endpoints of the image in the $xy$-plane are $(-6,2)$ and $(8,1).$\n\nDrawing this segment in the $xy$-plane, we get the following:\n\n![](images/8e/8e57b4d47ef3c6ad1b1d70a97126aa865c4481ee67e16eaf4e79c09e3146b30d.png)" 13194139536958 17592186576411 true] [17592186259685 1074 "In this case, the area of the right triangle is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}AC\\cdot AB.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{3},$ where $\\theta =m∠B.$ Notice we're also given the length of the side $\\overset{―}{AB}$ adjacent to $∠B.$ Hence, we can use the tangent ratio to find the length of the opposite side $\\overset{―}{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{AC}{AB} \\\\ \\frac{5}{3} & =\\frac{AC}{18} \\\\ \\frac{5}{3}\\cdot 18 & =AC \\\\ AC & =30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of $△ABC$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}AC\\cdot AB \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 30\\cdot 18 \\\\  & =270.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045417 true] [17592186259686 1074 "In this case, the area of the right triangle is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}BC\\cdot AB.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $m∠A={30}^{∘}.$ Notice that relative to $∠A,$ we have expressions for the lengths of the opposite side $\\overset{―}{BC}$ and adjacent side $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ Hence, we can use the tangent ratio to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(m∠A) & =\\frac{BC}{AB} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡({30}^{∘}) & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}\\,x}{5x+2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}\\,x}{5x+2} \\\\ \\sqrt{3}(5x+2) & =3\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3}\\,x \\\\ 5\\sqrt{3}\\,x+2\\sqrt{3} & =6\\sqrt{3}\\,x \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3} & =\\sqrt{3}\\,x \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the side lengths are \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =2\\sqrt{3}\\,x=2\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 2=4\\sqrt{3}, \\\\ AB & =5x+2=5(2)+2=12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of $△ABC$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot BC\\cdot AB \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =24\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045417 true] [17592186259687 1074 "In this case, the area of the right triangle is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}BC\\cdot AB.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $m∠A={30}^{∘}.$ Notice that relative to $∠A,$ we have expressions for the lengths of the opposite side $\\overset{―}{BC}$ and adjacent side $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ Hence, we can use the tangent ratio to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(m∠A) & =\\frac{BC}{AB} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡({30}^{∘}) & =\\frac{x-1}{x+2} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡({30}^{∘})(x+2) & =x-1 \\\\ x\\operatorname{tan}⁡({30}^{∘})+2\\operatorname{tan}⁡({30}^{∘}) & =x-1 \\\\ x\\operatorname{tan}⁡({30}^{∘})-x & =-1-2\\operatorname{tan}⁡({30}^{∘}) \\\\ x(\\operatorname{tan}⁡({30}^{∘})-1) & =-1-2\\operatorname{tan}⁡({30}^{∘}) \\\\ x(1-\\operatorname{tan}⁡({30}^{∘})) & =1+2\\operatorname{tan}⁡({30}^{∘}) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1+2\\operatorname{tan}⁡({30}^{∘})}{1-\\operatorname{tan}⁡({30}^{∘})} \\\\ x & =5.098\\,0…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the side lengths are \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =x-1=5.098\\,0-1=4.098\\,0…, \\\\ AB & =x+2=5.098\\,0+2=7.098\\,0….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\