\to \\infty $ does not tell us anything about the convergence or divergence of the series.\n\nThe comparison test for divergence states the following:\n\n*Consider two series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ and ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ such that ${a}_{n}$ and ${b}_{n}$ are both positive and ${a}_{n}\\ge {b}_{n}$ for all $n.$ If ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is divergent, then ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is also divergent.*\n\nWe follow four steps to show that $S$ is divergent.\n\n**Step 1**: First, we let ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{3}+3\\sqrt{n}}{2{n}^{4}}$ and ${b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{2n}.$ We note that ${a}_{n}>0$ and ${b}_{n}>0$ for all $n\\ge 1.$\n\n**Step 2**: We show that ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}$ is divergent. We have,\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{2}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n}\n$$\n\nwhich is divergent by the $p$-series test.\n\n**Step 3**: We show that ${a}_{n}\\ge {b}_{n}$ for all $n\\ge 1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & \\overset{?}{\\ge }{b}_{n} \\\\ \\frac{{n}^{3}+3\\sqrt{n}}{2{n}^{4}} & \\ge \\frac{{n}^{3}}{2{n}^{4}}=\\frac{1}{2n}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We conclude that ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ is divergent by the comparison test." 13194139533401 17592186045417 true] [17592186259778 1074 "Note that the position vectors of the points $A$, $B$, $C$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a=⟨t,\\,1,\\,1⟩,\\,b=⟨3t,\\,1,\\,3⟩,\\,c=⟨3,\\,-2,\\,{t}^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle $△ABC$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠BAC=\\theta .$\n\n![](images/28/285b8343208079181ac6036cf9ca16abc974d59b9262878ed40c0da9af5683d0.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =⟨3t,\\,1,\\,3⟩-⟨t,\\,1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,\\,0,\\,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =⟨3,\\,-2,\\,{t}^{2}⟩-⟨t,\\,1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3-t,\\,-3,\\,{t}^{2}-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC}=4$, we can solve for $t$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =4 \\\\ 2t\\cdot (3-t)+0\\cdot (-3)+2\\cdot ({t}^{2}-1) & =4 \\\\ 6t-2{t}^{2}+0+2{t}^{2}-2 & =4 \\\\ 6t-2 & =4 \\\\ 6t & =6 \\\\ t & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨2,0,2⟩, \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =⟨3-1,\\,3,\\,{1}^{2}-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,-3,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC}}{|\\overset{\\to }{AB}||\\overset{\\to }{AC}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{8}\\cdot \\sqrt{13}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2\\sqrt{26}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{26}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{26}})≈{67}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139533401 17592186045417 true] [17592186262615 1074 "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $C$ and parallel to $v$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=c+tv,\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $c$ is the position vector of $C.$ \n\nWe can find a direction vector by computing the displacement vector between the given points $C(-1,0,4)$ and $D(-7,2,11)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{CD} \\\\  & =⟨-7,2,11⟩-⟨-1,0,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-6,2,7⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector equation of the line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535476 17592186045443 true] [17592186262646 1074 "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $F$ and parallel to $v$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=f+tv,\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $f$ is the position vector of $F