ume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}|-0\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}|+3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(0\\cdot (-1)-2\\cdot 1)-0+3((-2)\\cdot 2-(-3)\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =2-12 \\\\  & =-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|-10|=10.\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045417 true] [17592186262927 1074 "First, we need to find two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other). Let's use the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{UV}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{UW},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{UV} & =⟨4,4,-4⟩-⟨7,-2,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,6,-7⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{UW} & =⟨3,7,6⟩-⟨7,-2,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4,9,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we consider the position vector $u=⟨7,-2,3⟩$ of the point $U,$ then the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =u+s\\overset{\\to }{UV}+t\\overset{\\to }{UW} \\\\ r & =⟨7,-2,3⟩+s⟨-3,6,-7⟩+t⟨-4,9,3⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045417 true] [17592186262928 1074 "First, we need to find two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other). We already have one vector $v=⟨-4,2,6⟩.$ Let's also use the displacement vector $\\overset{\\to }{UV}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{UV} & =⟨2,-1,-3⟩-⟨5,3,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,-4,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we consider the position vector $u=⟨5,3,1⟩$ of the point $U,$ then the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =u+sv+t\\overset{\\to }{UV} \\\\ r & =⟨5,3,1⟩+s⟨-4,2,6⟩+t⟨-3,-4,-4⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045417 true] [17592186262929 1074 "First, we need to find two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other). We already have one vector $v=⟨\\sqrt{5},-3,2⟩.$ Let's also use the displacement vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨5,1,-1⟩-⟨7,-3,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,4,-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we consider the position vector $p=⟨7,-3,4⟩$ of the point $P,$ then the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+sv+t\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\ r & =⟨7,-3,4⟩+s⟨\\sqrt{5},-3,2⟩+t⟨-2,4,-5⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533402 17592186045417 true] [17592186262936 1074 "Let's pick a point on the plane, for instance, $Q(1,-2,1).$ Then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, from the equation of the plane, we get a normal vector $n=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|0\\cdot (-2)+(-4)\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 2|}{\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-4+8|}{\\sqrt{4+1+4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|4|}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535477 17592186045443 true] [17592186262937 1074 "Let's pick a point on the plane, for instance, $Q(2,-1,1).$ Then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -