t $y=0$ in the equation of the line and solve for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-4 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =4 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to find the $y$-coordinate of $B$, we set $x=0$ and solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(0)-4 & =y \\\\ y & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $A$ and $B$ have the coordinates $(2,0)$ and $(0,-4)$, respectively.\n\nIf the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a diameter of the circle, then the midpoint must be the center of the circle. Using the midpoint formula, we find that the center of the circle is at\n\n$$\n(\\frac{2+0}{2},\\frac{0-4}{2})=(1,-2).\n$$\n\nNow, to find the radius of the circle, we compute the distance between the center $(1,-2)$ and a point on the circle, say $A(2,0),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{(1-2)}^{2}+(-2-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-1)}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, since the circle has center $(1,-2)$ and radius $r=\\sqrt{5},$ the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-1)}^{2}+(y-(-2))^{2} & ={(\\sqrt{5})}^{2} \\\\ {(x-1)}^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533406 17592186045417 true] [17592186275189 1074 "The equation of a circle with radius $r,$ centered at the origin, is \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the length of the diameter $\\overset{―}{XY}:$\n  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XY & =\\sqrt{{(4-(-4))}^{2}+{(1-(-1))}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{8}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{68} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4\\cdot 17} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{17}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\overset{―}{XY}$ is a diameter, the radius is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{XY}{2}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{17}}{2}=\\sqrt{17}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={(\\sqrt{17})}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =17.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533406 17592186045417 true] [17592186275190 1074 "The equation of a circle with radius $r,$ centered at the origin, is \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}