rt and get $\\overset{―}{z}=-9+\\text{i}.$\n\nAdding, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+\\overset{―}{z} & =(-9-\\text{i})+(-9+\\text{i}) \\\\  & =(-9+(-9))+\\text{i}(-1+1) \\\\  & =-18+\\text{i}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045417 true] [17592186278256 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nWhen we multiply a complex number and its conjugate, the result is always the sum of squares of the real part and the imaginary part.\n\nFor the given complex number $z=9+3\\text{i},$ the real part is $\\text{Re}(z)=9,$ and the imaginary part is $\\text{Im}(z)=3,$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z\\overset{―}{z} & =(\\text{Re}(z))^{2}+(\\text{Im}(z))^{2} \\\\  & ={9}^{2}+{3}^{2} \\\\  & =81+9 \\\\  & =90.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nAlternatively, we can actually carry out the process of multiplying the complex number and its conjugate.\n\nTo obtain the conjugate of the complex number $z=9+3\\text{i},$ we flip the sign of the imaginary part and get $\\overset{―}{z}=9-3\\text{i}.$\n\nMultiplying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z\\overset{―}{z} & =(9+3\\text{i})(9-3\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={9}^{2}+(9)(3)\\text{i}+(-3)(9)\\text{i}-{\\text{i}}^{2}(3)^{2} \\\\  & =81+27\\text{i}-27\\text{i}-(-1)(9) \\\\  & =81+9 \\\\  & =90.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533407 17592186045417 true] [17592186278263 1074 "| ${x}_{1}$ | **$-2$** | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${x}_{2}$ | **$0$** | **$14$** | **$-3$** | **$19$** | **$-1$** |\n| $y$ | $3$ | $5$ | $13$ | $21$ | $26$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the plane \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{1}{x}_{1}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 14 \\\\ 0 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 19 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}^{X}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}^{\\beta } & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 21 \\