aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(1\\cdot 0-0\\cdot 1)i-(2\\cdot 0-0\\cdot 3)j+(2\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 3)k \\\\  & =-k \\\\  & =⟨0,0,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,LMN$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$" 13194139533410 17592186045417 true] [17592186287925 1074 "Note that the position vectors of the points $A$, $B$, $C$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a=⟨2,1,-1⟩,\\,b=⟨3,-2,2⟩,\\,c=⟨-1,-2,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle $△ABC$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠BAC=\\theta .$\n\n![](images/28/285b8343208079181ac6036cf9ca16abc974d59b9262878ed40c0da9af5683d0.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =⟨3,-2,2⟩-⟨2,1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-3,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =⟨-1,-2,1⟩-⟨2,1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,-3,2⟩\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC}}{|\\overset{\\to }{AB}||\\overset{\\to }{AC}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot (-3)+(-3)\\cdot (-3)+3\\cdot 2}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{3}^{2}}\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{19}\\cdot \\sqrt{22}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{418}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n