\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =3b+3a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+3b=3a+3b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =3.\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045417 true] [17592186292807 1074 "![](images/c0/c0f7f4df52373ca602aff41c97a5b8413f510353874540b76147c527a2f4b18c.png)\n\nIn $△ORT$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OR=\\frac{1}{2}|r|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OR}$ has the opposite direction to $r$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OR}=-\\frac{1}{2}r.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $RT=\\frac{5}{2}|s|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{RT}$ has the opposite direction to $s$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{RT}=-\\frac{5}{2}s.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\overset{\\to }{OR}+\\overset{\\to }{RT} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}r-\\frac{5}{2}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $t=\\lambda r+\\mu s=-\\frac{1}{2}r-\\frac{5}{2}s.$ \n\nSince $s∦r,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\mu =-\\frac{5}{2}$" 13194139533412 17592186045417 true] [17592186292808 1074 "First, we let $f(x)={x}^{4}-8{x}^{3}+26{x}^{2}-72x+153.$ \n\nSince $f(x)$ has real coefficients and ${x}_{1}=4-\\text{i}$ is a root of $f(x),$ the complex conjugate \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{{x}_{1}}=\\overset{―}{4-\\text{i}}=4+\\text{i}\n$$\n\nmust also be a root of $f(x).$\n\nNow, according to the factor theorem, \n$(x-(4+\\text{i}))(x-(4-\\text{i}))$\nis a factor of $f(x).$ Expanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-(4+\\text{i}))(x-(4-\\text{i})) & ={x}^{2}-(4-\\text{i})x-(4+\\text{i})x+(4-\\text{i})(4+\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-8x+({4}^{2}-{\\text{i}}^{2}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-8x+17.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by ${x}^{2}-8x+17$ using polynomial division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,{x}^{2}+9 \\\\ {x}^{2}-8x+17 & \\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,{x}^{4}-8{x}^{3}+26{x}^{2}-72x+153\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,{{x}^{4}-8{x}^{3}+17{x}^{2}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,9{x}^{2}-72x+153 \\\\  & \\,{9{x}^{2}-72x+153}_{―} \\\\  & \\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $f(x)=({x}^{2}-8x+17)({x}^{2}+9).$\n\nTo find the other two roots, we solve ${x}^{2}+9=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+9 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-9 \\\\ x & =\\pm 3\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the remaining solutions to our original equation, apart from $x=4-\\text{i},$ are\n$x=4+1\\,\\text{i}$\nand\n$x=0\\pm 3\\,\\text{i}.$" 13194139533412 17592186045417 true] [17592186292815 1074 "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(-3i+3j-4k)-(-2i-k) \\\\  & =-i+3j-3k \\\\  & =⟨-1,3,-3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(-2j+2k)-(-2i-k) \\\\  & =2i-2j+3k \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 3-(-2)\\cdot (-3))i-((-1)\\cdot 3-2\\cdot (-3))j+((-1)\\cdot (-2)-2\\cdot 3)k \\\\  & =3i-3j-4k \\\\  & =⟨3,-3,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{34}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{34}}{2}.\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045417 true] [17592186292816 1074 "Note that the length of the altitude $CH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB\n$$\n\nSo, to find $CH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(-2j-k)-(-2i-k) \\\\  & =2i-2j \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(-3i-2j)-(-2i-k) \\\\  & =-i-2j+k \\\\  & =⟨-1,-2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot 1-(-2)\\cdot 0)i-(2\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 0)j+(2\\cdot (-2)-(-1)\\cdot (-2))k \\\\  & =-2i-2j-6k \\\\  & =⟨-2,-2,-6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+(-6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{44} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{2\\sqrt{11}}{2}=\\sqrt{11}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB \\\\ \\sqrt{11} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{11} & =CH\\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{\\sqrt{22}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533412 17592186045417 true] [17592186292882 1074 "To find the image of $\\overset{―}{AB}$ under the action of $