\to }{TU}+\\overset{\\to }{UP}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/b4/b4ebdca78235f8f82564db1821320c7bb886447cb012813173e9c50558dbc9bb.png)\n\n \n\nNow, from the original diagram, we have that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{RT}=d,\\,\\overset{\\to }{TU}=-c,\\,\\overset{\\to }{TU}=b.\n$$\n\nLet's add these to our diagram.\n\n![](images/54/540f94306cc497d87dc1f15d61ea929e042944f7c6f711a2bcf6b1f944c48228.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{RP} & =\\overset{\\to }{RT}+\\overset{\\to }{TU}+\\overset{\\to }{UP} \\\\  & =d-c+b.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045417 true] [17592186294966 1074 "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OF}=\\overset{\\to }{OD}+\\overset{\\to }{DF}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OD}=d,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{DF}.$\n\nFirst, from $△ODE,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DE} & =\\overset{\\to }{DO}+\\overset{\\to }{OE} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OD}+\\overset{\\to }{OE} \\\\  & =-d+e \\\\  & =e-d.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $DF:FE=2:3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{DF}=\\frac{2}{5}\\overset{\\to }{DE}=\\frac{2}{5}(e-d).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OF} & =\\overset{\\to }{OD}+\\overset{\\to }{DF} \\\\  & =d+\\frac{2}{5}(e-d) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}d+\\frac{2}{5}e.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045417 true] [17592186294974 1074 "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OP}=\\overset{\\to }{OF}+\\overset{\\to }{FP}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OF}=f,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{FP}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OFG,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{FG} & =\\overset{\\to }{FO}+\\overset{\\to }{OG} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OF}+\\overset{\\to }{OG} \\\\  & =-f+g \\\\  & =g-f.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $FP:PG=8:5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{FP}=\\frac{8}{13}\\overset{\\to }{FG}=\\frac{8}{13}(g-f).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OP} & =\\overset{\\to }{OF}+\\overset{\\to }{FP} \\\\  & =f+\\frac{8}{13}(g-f) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{13}f+\\frac{8}{13}g.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045417 true] [17592186295073 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nWhen we add a complex number and its conjugate, the result is always twice the real part of the complex number.\n\nFor the given complex number $z=6-10\\text{i},$ the real part is $Re(z)=6,$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+\\overset{―}{z} & =2\\text{Re}(z) \\\\  & =2(6) \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nAlternatively, we can actually carry out the process of adding the complex number and its conjugate.\n\nTo obtain the conjugate of the complex number $z=6-10\\text{i},$ we flip the sign of the imaginary part and get $\\overset{―}{z}=6+10\\text{i}.$\n\nAdding, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+\\overset{―}{z} & =(6-10\\text{i})+(6+10\\text{i}) \\\\  & =(6+6)+\\text{i}(-10+10) \\\\  & =12+\\text{i}0 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045417 true] [17592186295074 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nWhen we multiply a complex number and its conjugate, the result is always the sum of squares of the real part and the imaginary part.\n\nFor the given complex number $z=-6+\\text{i},$ the real part is $\\text{Re}(z)=6,$ and the imaginary part is $\\text{Im}(z)=1,$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z\\overset{―}{z} & =(\\text{Re}(z))^{2}+(\\text{Im}(z))^{2} \\\\  & =(-6)^{2}+{1}^{2} \\\\  & =36+1 \\\\  & =37.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nAlternatively, we can actually carry out the process of multiplying the complex number and its conjugate.\n\nTo obtain the conjugate of the complex number $z=-6+\\text{i},$ we flip the sign of the imaginary part and get $\\overset{―}{z}=-6-\\text{i}.$\n\nMultiplying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z\\overset{―}{z} & =(-6+\\text{i})(-6-\\text{i}) \\\\  & =(-6)^{2}+(-6)(-1)\\text{i}+(1)(-6)\\text{i}-{\\text{i}}^{2}(1)^{2} \\\\  & =36+\\cancel{6\\text{i}}-\\cancel{6\\text{i}}-(-1)(1) \\\\  & =36+1 \\\\  & =37.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045417 true] [17592186295075 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nWhen we multiply a complex number and its conjugate, the result is always the sum of squares of the real part and the imaginary part.\n\nFor the given complex number $z=-2+7\\text{i},$ the real part is $\\text{Re}(z)=-2,$ and the imaginary part is $\\text{Im}(z)=7,$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z\\overset{―}{z} & =(\\text{Re}(z))^{2}+(\\text{Im}(z))^{2} \\\\  & =(-2)^{2}+{7}^{2} \\\\  & =4+49 \\\\  & =53.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nAlternatively, we can actually carry out the process of multiplying the complex number and its conjugate.\n\nTo obtain the conjugate of the complex number $z=-2+7\\text{i},$ we flip the sign of the imaginary part and get $\\overset{―}{z}=-2-7\\text{i}.$\n\nMultiplying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z\\overset{―}{z} & =(-2+7\\text{i})(-2-7\\text{i}) \\\\  & =(-2)^{2}+(-2)(-7)\\text{i}+(7)(-2)\\text{i}-{\\text{i}}^{2}(7)^{2} \\\\  & =4+\\cancel{14\\text{i}}-\\cancel{14\\text{i}}-(-1)(49) \\\\  & =4+49 \\\\  & =53.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533413 17592186045417 true] [17592186295076 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nWhen we multiply a complex number and its conjugate, the result is always the sum of squares of the real part and the imaginary part.\n\nFor the given complex number $z=-2-11\\text{i},$ the real part is $\\text{Re}(z)=-2,$ and the imaginary part is $\\text{Im}(z)=-11,$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z\\overset{―}{z} & =(\\text{Re}(z))^{2}+(\\text{Im}(z))^{2} \\\\  & =(-2)^{2}+(-11)^{2} \\\\  & =4+121 \\\\  & =125.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nAlternatively, we can actually carry out the process of multiplying the complex number and its conjugate.\n\nTo obtain the conjugate of the complex number $z=-2-11\\text{i},$ we flip the sign of the imaginary part and get $\\overset{―}{z}=-2+11\\text{i}.$\n\nMultiplying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z\\overset{―}{z} & =(-2-11\\text{i})(-2+11\\text{i}) \\\\  & =(2)^{2}+(-2)(