 area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{7}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{62}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{62}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CK\\cdot AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{62}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}CK\\cdot \\sqrt{13} \\\\ CK & =\\frac{\\sqrt{62}}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ CK & =\\frac{\\sqrt{806}}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045417 true] [17592186297492 1074 "Note that the length of the altitude $HK$ can be related to the area of $△\\,GHI$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}HK\\cdot GI\n$$\n\nSo, to find $HK,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{GI}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{GH}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{GI} & =i-g \\\\  & =(2i+4j+2k)-(i+j+k) \\\\  & =i+3j+k \\\\  & =⟨1,3,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{GH} & =h-g \\\\  & =(3i+2j+4k)-(i+j+k) \\\\  & =2i+j+3k \\\\  & =⟨2,1,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{GI}\\times \\overset{\\to }{GH} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(3\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 1)i-(1\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 2)j+(1\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 2)k \\\\  & =8i-j-5k \\\\  & =⟨8,-1,-5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{GI}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{GH}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{GI}\\times \\overset{\\to }{GH}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{8}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-5)^{2}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,GHI$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $GI$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}GI & =|\\overset{\\to }{GI}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}HK\\cdot GI,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}HK\\cdot \\sqrt{11} \\\\ HK & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{\\sqrt{11}} \\\\ HK & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{110}}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045417 true] [17592186297493 1074 "Note that the length of the altitude $TK$ can be related to the area of $△\\,RST$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}TK\\cdot RS\n$$\n\nSo, to find $TK,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{RS}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{RT}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{RS} & =s-r \\\\  & =(7i+4j+5k)-(4i+5j+6k) \\\\  & =3i-j-k \\\\  & =⟨3,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{RT} & =t-r \\\\  & =(6i+8j+4k)-(4i+5j+6k) \\\\  & =2i+3j-2k \\\\  & =⟨2,3,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{RS}\\times \\overset{\\to }{RT} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =((-1)(-2)-(-1)\\cdot 3)i-(3(-2)-(-1)\\cdot 2)j+(3\\cdot 3-(-1)\\cdot 2)k \\\\  & =5i+4j+11k \\\\  & =⟨5,4,11⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{RS}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{RT}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{RS}\\times \\overset{\\to }{RT}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{11}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{162} \\\\  & =9\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,RST$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $RS$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RS & =|\\overset{\\to }{RS}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}TK\\cdot RS,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}TK\\cdot \\sqrt{11} \\\\ TK & =\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{11}} \\\\ TK & =\\frac{9\\sqrt{22}}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533414 17592186045417 true] [17592186299666 1074 "First, we let $f(x)={x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+25x-25.$ \n\nSince $f(x)$ has real coefficients and ${x}_{1}=5\\text{i}$ is a root of $f(x),$ the complex conjugate \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{{x}_{1}}=\\overset{―}{5\\text{i}}=-5\\text{i}\n$$\n\nmust also be a root of $f(x).$\n\nNow, according to the factor theorem, $(x-5\\text{i})(x-(-5\\text{i}))$ is a factor of $f(x).$ Expanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-5\\text{i})(x-(-5\\text{i})) & ={x}^{2}+(5\\text{i})(-5\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by ${x}^{2}+25$ using polynomial division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,x-1\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & {x}^{2}+25\\,\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+25x-25\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{x}^{3}+25x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-{x}^{2}\\,-25 \\\\  & \\,\\,{\\,-{x}^{2}\\,\\,-25}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nThus, $f(x)=({x}^{2}+25)(x-1).$ \n\nTo find the remaining root, we set the linear factor equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-1=0\\,⟹\\,x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the roots are $x=1$ and $x=\\pm \\,5\\text{i}.$" 13194139533415 17592186045417 true] [17592186299823 1074 "To find the complex conjugate, we flip the sign of the imaginary part.\n\nIn the given number $z=-1+10i,$ the imaginary part is $10i.$ Flipping the sign, we get $-10i.$\n\nTherefore, the complex conjugate is given by $\\overset{―}{z}=-1-10i.$" 13194139533415 17592186045417 true] [17592186299824 1074 "To find the complex conjugate, we flip the sign of the imaginary part.\n\nIn the given number $z=-\\sqrt{2}\\,i,$ the imaginary part is $-\\sqrt{2}\\,i$ (there is no real part, just an imaginary part). Flipping the sign, we get $\\sqrt{2}\\,i.$\n\nTherefore, the complex conjugate is given by $\\overset{―}{z}=\\sqrt{2}\\,i.$" 13194139533415 17592186045417 true] [17592186299825 1074 "To find the complex conjugate, we flip the sign of the imaginary part.\n\nIn the given number $z=-\\frac{5}{4}\\,i,$ the imaginary part is $-\\frac{5}{4}\\,i$ (there is no real part, just an imaginary part). Flipping the sign, we get $\\frac{5}{4}\\,i.$\n\nTherefore, the complex conjugate is given by $\\overset{―}{z}=\\frac{5}{4}\\,i.$" 13194139533415 17592186045417 true] [17592186299826 1074 "The number $z=-\\sqrt{7}$ is a real number, and the complex conjugate of any real number is just the number itself.\n\nSo here, the complex conjugate is $\\overset{―}{z}=z=-\\sqrt{7}.$" 13194139533415 17592186045417 true] [17592186299827 1074 "The number $z=-2\\sqrt{2}$ is a real number, and the complex conjugate of any real number is just the number itself.\n\nSo here, the complex conjugate is $\\overset{―}{z}=z=-2\\sqrt{2}.$" 13194139533415 17592186045417 true] [17592186299918 1074 "First, we let $f(x)={x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}+76x+100.$ \n\nSince $f(x)$ has real coefficients and ${x}_{1}=3-4\\,\\text{i}$ is a root of $f(x),$ the complex conjugate \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{{x}_{1}}=\\overset{―}{3-4\\,\\text{i}}=3+4\\,\\text{i}\n$$\n\nmust also be a root of $f(x).$\n\nNow, according to the factor theorem, $(x-(3-4\\,\\text{i}))(x-(3+4\\,\\text{i}))$\nis a factor of $f(x).$ Expanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-(3-4\\,\\text{i}))(x-(3+4\\,\\text{i})) & ={x}^{2}-(3+4\\,\\text{i})x-(3-4\\,\\text{i})x+(3-4\\,\\text{i})(3+4\\,\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-6x+({3}^{2}-(4\\text{i})^{2}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-6x+25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by ${x}^{2}-6x+25$ using polynomial division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,{x}^{2}+4x+4 \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x+25 & \\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,{x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}+76x+100\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,{{x}^{4}-6{x}^{3}+25{x}^{2}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,4{x}^{3}-20{x}^{2}+76x \\\\  & \\,{\\,\\,4{x}^{3}-24{x}^{2}+100x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,4{x}^{2}-24x+100 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,{4{x}^{2}-24x+100}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $f(x)=({x}^{2}-6x+25)({x}^{2}+4x+4).$\n\nTo find the remaining two roots, we solve ${x}^{2}+4x+4=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4x+4 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)(x+2) & =0 \\\\ (x+2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the remaining solutions to our original equation, apart from $x=3-4\\,\\text{i},$ are\n$x=3+4\\,\\text{i}$\nand\n$x=-2.$" 13194139533415 17592186045417 true] [17592186299921 1074 "First, we let $f(x)={x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}+18{x}^{2}-122x+377.$ \n\nSince $f(x)$ has real coefficients and ${x}_{1}=-2-5\\text{i}$ is a root of $f(x),$ the complex conjugate \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{{x}_{1}}=\\overset{―}{-2-5\\text{i}}=-2+5\\text{i}\n$$\n\nmust also be a root of $f(x).$\n\nNow, according to the factor theorem, \n$(x-(-2+5\\text{i}))(x-(-2-5\\text{i}))$\nis a factor of $f(x).$ Expanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-(-2+5\\text{i}))(x-(-2-5\\text{i})) & ={x}^{2}-(-2-5\\text{i})x-(-2+5\\text{i})x+(-2-5\\text{i})(-2+5\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+4x+((-2)^{2}-(5\\text{i})^{2}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+4x+29.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by ${x}^{2}+4x+29$ using polynomial division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,{x}^{2}-6x+13 \\\\ {x}^{2}+4x+29 & \\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,{x}^{4}-2{x}^{3}+18{x}^{2}-122x+377\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,{{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}+29{x}^{2}}_{―} \\\\  & \\,-6{x}^{3}-11{x}^{2}-122x \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,{-6{x}^{3}-24{x}^{2}-174x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,13{x}^{2}+52x+377 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,{13{x}^{2}+52x+377}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $f(x)=({x}^{2}+4x+29)({x}^{2}-6x+13).$\n\nTo find the other two roots, we solve ${x}^{2}-6x+13=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-6x+13 & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-6x+9)+13-9 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ x-3 & =\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ x & =3\\pm 2\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the remaining solutions to our original equation, apart from $x=-2-5\\text{i},$ are\n$x=-2+5\\,\\text{i}$\nand\n$x=3\\pm 2\\,\\text{i}.$" 13194139533415 17592186045417 true] [17592186302041 1074 "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=a+\\lambda b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $a.$ So, we begin with the vector $a,$ and then find the remaining vector needed to reach the point $C.$\n\n![](images/9c/9c8d67718152bc1bc9569213369580b6fc6f5bc1fe0c63b637f14c9f659dfe27.png)\n\nNote that in $△OAC,$ we have:\n\n- $AC=\\frac{2}{3}|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $b$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=\\frac{2}{3}b.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =a+\\frac{2}{3}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=a+\\lambda b=a+\\frac{2}{3}b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $b,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\lambda =\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045417 true] [17592186302111 1074 "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OF}=\\overset{\\to }{OD}+\\overset{\\to }{DF}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OD}=d,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{DF}.$\n\nFirst, from $△ODE,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DE} & =\\overset{\\to }{DO}+\\overset{\\to }{OE} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OD}+\\overset{\\to }{OE} \\\\  & =-d+e \\\\  & =e-d.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $DF:FE=3:4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{DF}=\\frac{3}{7}\\overset{\\to }{DE}=\\frac{3}{7}(e-d).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OF} & =\\overset{\\to }{OD}+\\overset{\\to }{DF} \\\\  & =d+\\frac{3}{7}(e-d) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{7}d+\\frac{3}{7}e.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045417 true] [17592186302112 1074 "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=\\overset{\\to }{OG}+\\overset{\\to }{GJ}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OG}=g,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{GJ}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OGH,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{GH} & =\\overset{\\to }{GO}+\\overset{\\to }{OH} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OG}+\\overset{\\to }{OH} \\\\  & =-g+h \\\\  & =h-g.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $GJ:JH=6:7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{GJ}=\\frac{6}{13}\\overset{\\to }{GH}=\\frac{6}{13}(h-g).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OJ} & =\\overset{\\to }{OG}+\\overset{\\to }{GJ} \\\\  & =g+\\frac{6}{13}(h-g) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{13}g+\\frac{6}{13}h.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045417 true] [17592186302113 1074 "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OR}=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OP}=p,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{PR}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OPQ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =\\overset{\\to }{PO}+\\overset{\\to }{OQ} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{OQ} \\\\  & =-p+q \\\\  & =q-p.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $PR:RQ=4:9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{PR}=\\frac{4}{13}\\overset{\\to }{PQ}=\\frac{4}{13}(q-p).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OR} & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR} \\\\  & =p+\\frac{4}{13}(q-p) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{13}p+\\frac{4}{13}q.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045417 true] [17592186302114 1074 "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OT}=\\overset{\\to }{OM}+\\overset{\\to }{MT}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OM}=d,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{MT}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OMN,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{MN} & =\\overset{\\to }{MO}+\\overset{\\to }{ON} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OM}+\\overset{\\to }{ON} \\\\  & =-d+e \\\\  & =e-d.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $MT:TN=8:3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{MT}=\\frac{8}{11}\\overset{\\to }{MN}=\\frac{8}{11}(e-d).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OT} & =\\overset{\\to }{OM}+\\overset{\\to }{MT} \\\\  & =d+\\frac{8}{11}(e-d) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{11}d+\\frac{8}{11}e.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045417 true] [17592186302115 1074 "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OW}=\\overset{\\to }{OU}+\\overset{\\to }{UW}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OU}=u,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{UW}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OUV,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{UV} & =\\overset{\\to }{UO}+\\overset{\\to }{OV} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OU}+\\overset{\\to }{OV} \\\\  & =-u+v \\\\  & =v-u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $UW:WV=9:8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{UW}=\\frac{9}{17}\\overset{\\to }{UV}=\\frac{9}{17}(v-u).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OW} & =\\overset{\\to }{OU}+\\overset{\\to }{UW} \\\\  & =u+\\frac{9}{17}(v-u) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{17}u+\\frac{9}{17}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045417 true] [17592186302116 1074 "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OL}=\\overset{\\to }{OS}+\\overset{\\to }{SL}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OS}=s,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{SL}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OST,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{ST} & =\\overset{\\to }{SO}+\\overset{\\to }{OT} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OS}+\\overset{\\to }{OT} \\\\  & =-s+t \\\\  & =t-s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $SL:LT=11:4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{SL}=\\frac{11}{15}\\overset{\\to }{ST}=\\frac{11}{15}(t-s).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OL} & =\\overset{\\to }{OS}+\\overset{\\to }{SL} \\\\  & =s+\\frac{11}{15}(t-s) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{15}s+\\frac{11}{15}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045417 true] [17592186302117 1074 "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OY}=\\overset{\\to }{OK}+\\overset{\\to }{KY}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OK}=k,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{KY}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OKZ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{KZ} & =\\overset{\\to }{KO}+\\overset{\\to }{OZ} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OK}+\\overset{\\to }{OZ} \\\\  & =-k+z \\\\  & =z-k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $KY:YZ=10:7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{KY}=\\frac{10}{17}\\overset{\\to }{KZ}=\\frac{10}{17}(z-k).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OY} & =\\overset{\\to }{OK}+\\overset{\\to }{KY} \\\\  & =k+\\frac{10}{17}(z-k) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{17}k+\\frac{10}{17}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045417 true] [17592186302118 1074 "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{ON}=\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AN}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=a,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{AN}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OAQ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AQ} & =\\overset{\\to }{AO}+\\overset{\\to }{OQ} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{OQ} \\\\  & =-a+q \\\\  & =q-a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $AN:NQ=12:7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{AN}=\\frac{12}{19}\\overset{\\to }{AQ}=\\frac{12}{19}(q-a).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{ON} & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AN} \\\\  & =a+\\frac{12}{19}(q-a) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{19}a+\\frac{12}{19}q.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045417 true] [17592186302119 1074 "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OQ}=\\overset{\\to }{OL}+\\overset{\\to }{LQ}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\overset{\\to }{OL}=l,$ so we just need to work out $\\overset{\\to }{LQ}.$\n\nFirst, from $△OLP,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LP} & =\\overset{\\to }{LO}+\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OL}+\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =-l+p \\\\  & =p-l.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $LQ:QP=7:9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{LQ}=\\frac{7}{16}\\overset{\\to }{LP}=\\frac{7}{16}(p-l).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OQ} & =\\overset{\\to }{OL}+\\overset{\\to }{LQ} \\\\  & =l+\\frac{7}{16}(p-l) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{16}l+\\frac{7}{16}p.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045417 true] [17592186302254 1074 "![](images/2d/2d3a5c31af776cc49c2af118edc7256ca533320c7d53245593e5de6649ce03cf.png)\n\nIn $△OXT$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OX=|u|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OX}$ has the opposite direction to $u$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OX}=-u.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $XT=2|v|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{XT}$ has the opposite direction to $v$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{XT}=-2v.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\nt=\\overset{\\to }{OX}+\\overset{\\to }{XT}=-u-2v.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $t=\\lambda u+\\mu v=-1u-2v.$\n\nSince $v∦u,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-1$ and $\\mu =-2$" 13194139533416 17592186045417 true] [17592186302255 1074 "![](images/1b/1be50e101b386f53ea5a2f1474f2e1d2debb8f98380d49a346dda32b37db70f1.png)\n\nIn $△OXT$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OX=2|j|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OX}$ has the opposite direction to $j$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OX}=-2j.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $XT=\\frac{1}{2}|k|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{XT}$ has the opposite direction to $k$\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{XT}=-\\frac{1}{2}k.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\nt=\\overset{\\to }{OX}+\\overset{\\to }{XT}=-2j-\\frac{1}{2}k.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $t=\\lambda j+\\mu k=-2j-\\frac{1}{2}k.$\n\nSince $k∦j,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-2$ and $\\mu =-\\frac{1}{2}$" 13194139533416 17592186045417 true] [17592186302256 1074 "![](images/56/563522ad9e5cf8e134a6669364986de592c7d63da244b050ef466e9f6c971a39.png)\n\nIn $△OPR$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OP=3|p|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ and $p$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OP}=3p.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $PR=2|q|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ and $q$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PR}=2q.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR} \\\\  & =3p+2q.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q=3p+2q.$\n\nSince $p∦q,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =3$ and $\\mu =2$" 13194139533416 17592186045417 true] [17592186302257 1074 "![](images/f7/f7acd51dcca9ad48f4aa7ec026beaeb67ddc9b1e88ab9b5042016674ba6ea542.png)\n\nIn $△OPR$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OP=|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OP}$ and $a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OP}=a.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $PR=4|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ and $b$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PR}=4b.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR} \\\\  & =a+4b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $r=\\lambda a+\\mu b=1a+4b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =1$ and $\\mu =4$" 13194139533416 17592186045417 true] [17592186302311 1074 "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=a+\\lambda b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $a.$ So, we begin with the vector $a,$ and then find the remaining vector needed to reach the point $C.$\n\n![](images/c0/c074e514a4ca52868a5c01d7a29182d62493f2cc955fd0582e52c78bdde2bf7d.png)\n\nNote that in $△OAC,$ we have:\n\n- $AC=2|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $b$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=2b.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =a+2b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=a+\\lambda b=a+2b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $b,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\lambda =2\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045417 true] [17592186302312 1074 "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+2b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $2b.$ So, we first draw the vector $2b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/1d/1d266a8324d18191f5bc8560be6539d54d1a11916f33ead7432d9f528769fc22.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $2b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=\\frac{1}{2}|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=\\frac{1}{2}a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =2b+\\frac{1}{2}a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+2b=\\frac{1}{2}a+2b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045417 true] [17592186302315 1074 "![](images/67/6708537b79154a7778fe52e8d82b6577f93d40a06d3af0267e3e4b7eaa6468a9.png)\n\nIn $△OMR$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OM=3|q|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OM}$ and $q$ have the opposite direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OM}=-3q.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $MR=2|p|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{MR}$ and $p$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{MR}=2p.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\overset{\\to }{OM}+\\overset{\\to }{MR} \\\\  & =-3q+2p.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $r=\\lambda p+\\mu q=2p-3q.$\n\nSince $p∦q,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =2$ and $\\mu =-3$" 13194139533416 17592186045417 true] [17592186302316 1074 "![](images/cb/cb8209592ec6d5be435622999c1b3eb86e1756cbe9852be0efdbbf41d41777ff.png)\n\nIn $△OJL$ shown above, we have  \n\n- $OJ=2|j|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OJ}$ and $j$ have the opposite direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OJ}=-2j.$  \n\nSimilarly:  \n\n- $JL=|k|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{JL}$ and $k$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{JL}=k.$  \n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\ni=\\overset{\\to }{OJ}+\\overset{\\to }{JL}=-2j+k.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $i=\\lambda j+\\mu k=-2j+k.$  \n\nSince $j∦k,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way.  Equating the coefficients, we get \n\n$$\n\\lambda =-2,\\,\\mu =1.\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045417 true] [17592186302317 1074 "![](images/e0/e018cc5b4760b578d9ca514f9de6641e823bf5f6a8e17f019ebf24fc6ccb2248.png)\n\nIn $△OTS$ shown above, we have \n\n- $OT=4|m|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OT}$ and $m$ have the opposite direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OT}=-4m.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $TS=2|n|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{TS}$ and $n$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{TS}=2n.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =\\overset{\\to }{OT}+\\overset{\\to }{TS} \\\\  & =-4m+2n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $s=\\lambda m+\\mu n=-4m+2n.$\n\nSince $m∦n,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda =-4$ and $\\mu =2$" 13194139533416 17592186045417 true] [17592186302356 1074 "We want to find a \"path\" from $R$ to $P$ consisting only of the vectors $a,d,$ and $c.$\n\nAccording to the triangle law of addition,\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{RP}=\\overset{\\to }{RQ}+\\overset{\\to }{QP}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/b6/b6eb4bdf68dcecd124ec7129b94b6c5adccc826f505ecda653069640efa439e2.png)\n\n \n\nNow, from the original diagram, we have that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{RQ}=d,\\,\\overset{\\to }{QP}=a.\n$$\n\nLet's add these to our diagram.\n\n![](images/90/90749b45c5e992ba17f09c6a3597adebcdad2ab9d354b2aee7e41058af69863a.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{RP} & =\\overset{\\to }{RQ}+\\overset{\\to }{QP} \\\\  & =d+a.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045417 true] [17592186302393 1074 "We want to find a \"path\" from $R$ to $Q$ consisting only of the vectors $a,b,c,$ and $d.$\n\nAccording to the triangle law of addition,\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{RQ}=\\overset{\\to }{RP}+\\overset{\\to }{PQ},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/c3/c3abc51a0c48faa7853eb2ef605412ca6311866994b15a579b7bc7e7db63ee15.png)\n\nNow, from the original diagram, we have that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{RP}=-d,\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}=-a.\n$$\n\nLet's add these to our diagram.\n\n![](images/a5/a53de4a8dcbd4a5d1f7640d5e1d4c8b6fd6e5e24388f432d279392727b11dd59.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{RQ} & =\\overset{\\to }{RP}+\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =-d+(-a) \\\\  & =-d-a.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045417 true] [17592186302394 1074 "We want to find a \"path\" from $Q$ to $T$ consisting only of the vectors $a,b,c,$ and $d.$\n\nAccording to the triangle law of addition,\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{QT}=\\overset{\\to }{QS}+\\overset{\\to }{ST},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/cd/cd8dc0d2e01927c8b2ca116265b257d835a1baae62272b703610f2947d92a8c2.png)\n\n \n\nNow, from the original diagram, we have that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{QS}=-b,\\,\\overset{\\to }{ST}=-c.\n$$\n\nLet's add these to our diagram.\n\n![](images/8e/8ed8c71e0d45ae0c9cf7d8a7d2889ee3a79471aaeb408ad0f53f487523daac13.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{QT} & =\\overset{\\to }{QS}+\\overset{\\to }{ST} \\\\  & =-b+(-c) \\\\  & =-b-c.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045417 true] [17592186302396 1074 "We want to find a \"path\" from $S$ to $P$ consisting only of the vectors $a,b,c,$ and $d.$\n\nAccording to the triangle law of addition,\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{SP}=\\overset{\\to }{ST}+\\overset{\\to }{TQ}+\\overset{\\to }{QP}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/07/07aebf5a6aaa98c9ec5d4e9801dbe7d2cf3ade86573bcd8ee56db375f3e38575.png)\n\nNow, from the original diagram, we have that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{ST}=a,\\,\\overset{\\to }{TQ}=b,\\,\\overset{\\to }{QP}=d.\n$$\n\nLet's add these to our diagram.\n\n![](images/7c/7c8c193472acbe72979f8a78fe9bd02726ad98c166381434175d0586bb574cb6.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{SP} & =\\overset{\\to }{ST}+\\overset{\\to }{TQ}+\\overset{\\to }{QP} \\\\  & =a+b+d.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045417 true] [17592186302397 1074 "We know that the line passes through the points $P(-1,3,2)$ and $Q(2,4,5).$ Now, we need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3t \\\\ t \\\\ 3t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1+3t \\\\ 3+t \\\\ 2+3t\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ And finally, the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-1+3t \\\\ y &=3+t \\\\ z &=2+3t\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" 13194139535661 17592186045443 true] [17592186302398 1074 "We know that the line passes through the points $P(3,6,8)$ and $Q(-2,-5,4).$ Now, we need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -11 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -11 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-5t \\\\ -11t \\\\ -4t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3-5t \\\\ 6-11t \\\\ 8-4t\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Therefore, the parametric equations are:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=3-5t \\\\ y &=6-11t \\\\ z &=8-4t\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$" 13194139535661 17592186045443 true] [17592186302415 1074 "We know that the line passes through the points $P(-1,1,4)$ and $Q(3,-2,2).$ Now, we need to find a vector parallel to the given line. Let's consider the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ which is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨3,-2,2⟩-⟨-1,1,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,-3,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the point $P$ and the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$, we have that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the given line is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-(-1)}{4}=\\frac{y-1}{-3}=\\frac{z-4}{-2}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+1}{4}=\\frac{y-1}{-3}=\\frac{z-4}{-2}.\n$$" 13194139533416 17592186045417 true] [17592186304912 1074 "Note that the position vectors of the points $A,P$ and $Q$ are $a=⟨2,-5,1⟩,\\,p=⟨4,-2,3⟩,$ and $q=⟨5,1,0⟩.$\n\nFirst, using the $3$ points we build $2$ vectors parallel to the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} & =\\overset{\\to }{AP} \\\\  & =⟨4,-2,3⟩-⟨2,-5,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,3,2⟩ \\\\ {v}_{2} & =\\overset{\\to }{AQ} \\\\  & =⟨5,1,0⟩-⟨2,-5,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,6,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow,  we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\times \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 6 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-15i+8j+3k \\\\  & =⟨-15,8,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-15,8,3⟩ & =⟨4,-2,3⟩\\cdot ⟨-15,8,3⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-15,8,3⟩ & =-67\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-15,8,3⟩ & =-67 \\\\ -15x+8y+3z & =-67 \\\\ -15x+8y+3z+67 & =0 \\\\ 15x-8y-3z-67 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535481 17592186045443 true] [17592186304926 1074 "The direction vectors of the lines are ${v}_{1}=⟨-2,-2,-2⟩$ and ${v}_{2}=⟨1,1,1⟩,$ respectively.\nBut ${v}_{2}=-2{v}_{1}.$ So, our lines are parallel. \n\nDespite this fact, two distinct parallel lines still determine a plane. Now: \n\n- from the equation of the first line, we get the point $P$ with the position vector $p=⟨-1,5,7⟩$ that lies on it, and\n\n- from the equation of the second line, we get the point $Q$ with the position vector $q=⟨0,3,-19⟩$ that lies on it.\n\nThe vector $u=q-p$ will be parallel to the required plane. \n\nComputing the coordinates of $u,$  we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =q-p \\\\  & =⟨0,3,-19⟩-⟨-1,5,7⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-2,-26⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}∦{v}_{1},$ we can find the equation of our plane.\n\nWe know that the point $C(1,4,-14)$ lies on the plane and its position vector is $c=⟨1,4,-14⟩.$  \n\nSo, the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =c+s{v}_{1}+tu \\\\ r & =⟨1,4,-14⟩+s⟨1,1,1⟩+t⟨1,-2,-26⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045417 true] [17592186304972 1074 "The $z$-score of an observation $x$ is defined by\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{x-\\overset{―}{x}}{\\sigma }.\n$$\n\nThe $z$-score represents how many standard deviations an observation is above or below the mean of the data set.\n\nHere, we're told that the mean is $\\overset{―}{x}=4,$ and we can calculate the standard deviation as\n\n$$\n\\sigma =\\sqrt{{\\sigma }^{2}}=\\sqrt{0.25}=0.5.\n$$\n\nSo, the $z$-score of the observation $x=3.4$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{x-\\overset{―}{x}}{\\sigma } \\\\  & =\\frac{3.4-4}{0.5} \\\\  & =-1.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045417 true] [17592186304973 1074 "The $z$-score of an observation $x$ is defined by\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{x-\\overset{―}{x}}{\\sigma }.\n$$\n\nThe $z$-score represents how many standard deviations an observation is above or below the mean of the data set.\n\nHere, we're told that the mean is $\\overset{―}{x}=7,$ and we can calculate the standard deviation as\n\n$$\n\\sigma =\\sqrt{{\\sigma }^{2}}=\\sqrt{1.7}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $z$-score of the observation $x=6.2$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{x-\\overset{―}{x}}{\\sigma } \\\\  & =\\frac{6.2-7}{\\sqrt{1.7}} \\\\  & ≈-0.61,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." 13194139533417 17592186045417 true] [17592186304974 1074 "The $z$-score of an observation $x$ is defined by\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{x-\\overset{―}{x}}{\\sigma }.\n$$\n\nThe $z$-score represents how many standard deviations an observation is above or below the mean of the data set.\n\nHere, we're told that the mean is $\\overset{―}{x}=-1.2,$ and we can calculate the standard deviation as\n\n$$\n\\sigma =\\sqrt{{\\sigma }^{2}}=\\sqrt{0.32}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $z$-score of the observation $x=-2.1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{x-\\overset{―}{x}}{\\sigma } \\\\  & =\\frac{-2.1-(-1.2)}{\\sqrt{0.32}} \\\\  & ≈-1.59,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." 13194139533417 17592186045417 true] [17592186304975 1074 "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overset{―}{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sigma ,$ we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma \n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=11,$ $z=0.8,$ and $\\sigma =2.5$ into the formula and solving for $\\overset{―}{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 11 & =\\overset{―}{x}+0.8\\cdot 2.5 \\\\ 11 & =\\overset{―}{x}+2 \\\\ \\overset{―}{x} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045417 true] [17592186304976 1074 "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overset{―}{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sigma ,$ we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma \n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=12.1,$ $\\overset{―}{x}=10,$ and $z=1.05$ into the formula and solving for $\\sigma ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 12.1 & =10+1.05\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 2.1 & =1.05\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ \\sigma  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045417 true] [17592186304977 1074 "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overset{―}{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sigma ,$ we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma \n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-1,$ $\\overset{―}{x}=-2,$ and $z=1.25$ into the formula and solving for $\\sigma ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ -1 & =-2+1.25\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 1 & =1.25\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ \\sigma  & =0.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045417 true] [17592186304978 1074 "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overset{―}{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sigma ,$ we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma \n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=5.08,$ $\\overset{―}{x}=6.2,$ and $z=-0.7$ into the formula and solving for $\\sigma ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 5.08 & =6.2+(-0.7)\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ -1.12 & =-0.7\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ \\sigma  & =1.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045417 true] [17592186305001 1074 "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overset{―}{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sigma ,$ we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma \n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\overset{―}{x}=-0.3,$ $z=1.4,$ and $\\sigma =0.5$ into the formula and solving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ x & =-0.3+1.4\\cdot 0.5 \\\\ x & =-0.3+0.7 \\\\ x & =0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045417 true] [17592186305005 1074 "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overset{―}{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sigma ,$ we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma \n$$\n\nFrom the first observation, we have\n\n$$\n-1.2=\\overset{¯}{x}+(-0.5)\\cdot \\sigma .\n$$\n\nFrom the second observation, we have\n\n$$\n2.8=\\overset{¯}{x}+2\\cdot \\sigma .\n$$\n\nSubtracting the first equation from the second and solving for $\\sigma ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2.8-(-1.2) & =(2-(-0.5))\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 4 & =2.5\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ \\sigma  & =1.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this value back into our first equation and solving for $\\overset{¯}{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1.2 & =\\overset{¯}{x}+(-0.5)\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ -1.2 & =\\overset{¯}{x}+(-0.5)\\cdot 1.6 \\\\ -1.2 & =\\overset{¯}{x}-0.8 \\\\ \\overset{¯}{x} & =-0.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045417 true] [175921