592186305008 1074 "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overset{―}{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sigma ,$ we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma \n$$\n\nFrom the first observation, we have\n\n$$\n0.5=\\overset{¯}{x}+(-3)\\cdot \\sigma .\n$$\n\nFrom the second observation, we have\n\n$$\n4.5=\\overset{¯}{x}+5\\cdot \\sigma .\n$$\n\nSubtracting the first equation from the second and solving for $\\sigma ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4.5-0.5 & =(5-(-3))\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 4 & =8\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ \\sigma  & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045417 true] [17592186305009 1074 "The $z$-score of an observation $x$ is defined by\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{x-\\overset{―}{x}}{\\sigma }.\n$$\n\nThe $z$-score represents how many standard deviations an observation is above or below the mean of the data set.\n\nSo, the $z$-score of the observation $x=65$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{x-\\overset{¯}{x}}{\\sigma } \\\\  & =\\frac{65-80}{5} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533417 17592186045417 true] [17592186305010 1074 "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overset{―}{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sigma ,$ we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma \n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\overset{―}{x}=70,$ $z=2.5,$ and $x=74$ into the formula and solving for $\\sigma ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 74 & =70+2.5\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 4 & =2.5\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{4}{2.5} \\\\ \\sigma  & =1.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the standard deviation of the patients' heart rates in the group is $1.6$ beats per minute." 13194139533417 17592186045417 true] [17592186305011 1074 "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overset{―}{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sig