ow, from the original diagram, we have that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{AF}=c,\\,\\overset{\\to }{FE}=-a,\\,\\overset{\\to }{EG}=-b\\,\\overset{\\to }{GC}=d.\n$$\n\nLet's add these to our diagram.\n\n![](images/37/37c3758dc7a4c8a5b79c3e687e77f62090282410e3bf45bfade3494cd3b8055b.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\overset{\\to }{AF}+\\overset{\\to }{FE}+\\overset{\\to }{EG}+\\overset{\\to }{GC} \\\\  & =c+(-a)+(-b)+d \\\\  & =c-a-b+d.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533419 17592186045417 true] [17592186311695 1074 "Note that the position vectors of the points $A,P$ and $Q$ are $a=⟨1,1,-1⟩,\\,p=⟨3,4,2⟩,$ and $q=⟨-1,2,0⟩.$\n\nFirst, using the $3$ points we build $2$ vectors parallel to the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} & =\\overset{\\to }{AP} \\\\  & =⟨3,4,2⟩-⟨1,1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,3,3⟩ \\\\ {v}_{2} & =\\overset{\\to }{AQ} \\\\  & =⟨-1,2,0⟩-⟨1,1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow,  we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\times \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 3 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0i-8j+8k \\\\  & =⟨0,-8,8⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨0,-8,8⟩ & =⟨3,4,2⟩\\cdot ⟨0,-8,8⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨0,-8,8⟩ & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,-8,8⟩ & =-16 \\\\ -8y+8z & =-16 \\\\ -8y+8z+16 & =0 \\\\ y-z-2 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535482 17592186045443 true] [17592186311696 1074 "Note that the position vectors of the points $A,P$ and $Q$ are $a=⟨2,-2,1⟩,\\,p=⟨5,0,3⟩,$ and $q=⟨1,1,-1⟩.$\n\nFirst, using the $3$ points we build $2$ vectors parallel to the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} & =\\overset{\\to }{AP} \\\\  & =⟨5,0,3⟩-⟨2,-2,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,2,2⟩ \\\\ {v}_{2} & =\\overset{\\to }{AQ} \\\\  & =⟨1,1,-1⟩-⟨2,-2,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,3,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow,  we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\times \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-10i+4j+11k \\\\  & =⟨-10,4,11⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-10,4,11⟩ & =⟨5,0,3⟩\\cdot ⟨-10,4,11⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-10,4,11⟩ & =-17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-10,4,11⟩ & =-17 \\\\ -10x+4y+11z & =-17 \\\\ 10x-4y-11z & =17 \\\\ 10x-4y-11z-17 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535482 17592186045443 true] [17592186311864 1074 "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)=\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}⁡(x)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{x}.$\n\nWe are given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=\\frac{2}{x}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{2}{x}$ is a function of $x$ only, we can conclude that the integrating factor for the equation is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int 2{x}^{-1}\\text{ }\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our equation by the integrating factor, we arrive at the following exact differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}[(\\frac{5{y}^{2}}{x}+2{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+(5y+2x{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}y] & =0 \\\\ \\underbrace{(5x{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2}{y}^{3})}_{\\overset{~}{P}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\underbrace{(5{x}^{2}y+2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+2)}_{\\overset{~}{Q}}\\,\\text{d}y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor this exact equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)=5x{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2}{y}^{3},\\,\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)=5{x}^{2}y+2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+2.\n$$\n\nTo solve the equation, let's first integrate $\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $y,$ which we'll call $F(y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{P}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\int (5x{y}^{2}+2{x}^{2}{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}{y}^{3}+F(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's integrate $\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $x,$ which we'll call $G(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\int (5{x}^{2}y+2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}{y}^{3}+2y+G(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two expressions, we must have\n\n$$\nF(y)=2y,\\,G(x)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}{y}^{3}+2y=c.\n$$" 13194139536958 17592186576411 true] [17592186311913 1074 "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=h(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $h(y)$ is independent of $x,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(y)=\\operatorname{exp}⁡(-\\int h(y)\\,\\text{d}y)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}⁡(y)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{y}.$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=\\frac{3}{y}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{3}{y}$ is a function of $y$ only, we can conclude that the integrating factor for the equation is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x,y) & ={e}^{-\\int 3/y\\text{ }\\text{d}y} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡y} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our equation by the integrating factor, we arrive at the following exact differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{y}^{3}}(yx\\,\\text{d}x-({x}^{2}+5{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y) & =0 \\\\ \\underbrace{(\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}})}_{\\overset{~}{P}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\underbrace{(-1)(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{3}}+5)}_{\\overset{~}{Q}}\\,\\text{d}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor this exact equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)=\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}},\\,\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{3}}-5.\n$$\n\nTo solve the equation, let's first integrate $\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $y,$ which we'll call $F(y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{P}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{y}^{2}}+F(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's integrate $\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $x,$ which we'll call $G(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\int (-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{3}}-5)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{y}^{2}}-5y+G(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\nF(y)=-5y,\\,G(x)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2{y}^{2}}-5y=c.\n$$" 13194139536958 17592186576411 true] [17592186314742 1074 "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=h(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $h(y)$ is independent of $x,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(y)=\\operatorname{exp}⁡(-\\int h(y)\\,\\text{d}y)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}⁡(y)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{y}.$\n\nWe are given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=\\frac{2}{y}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{2}{y}$ is a function of $y$ only, we can conclude that the integrating factor for the equation is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x,y) & ={e}^{-\\int 2/y\\text{ }\\text{d}y} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡y} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying our equation by the integrating factor, we arrive at the following exact differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}({x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}x-(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y) & =0 \\\\ \\underbrace{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y})}_{\\overset{~}{P}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\underbrace{(-1)(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3{y}^{2}}+y)}_{\\overset{~}{Q}}\\,\\text{d}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor this exact equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y},\\,\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)=-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3{y}^{2}}-y.\n$$\n\nTo solve the equation, let's first integrate $\\overset{~}{P}(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $y,$ which we'll call $F(y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{P}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}\\,\\text{d}x+F(y) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3y}+F(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's integrate $\\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$\nThe result should include an arbitrary function of $x,$ which we'll call $G(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int \\overset{~}{Q}(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\int (-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3{y}^{2}}-y)\\,\\text{d}y+G(x) \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3y}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+G(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\nF(y)=-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2},\\,G(x)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3y}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=c.\n$$" 13194139536958 17592186576411 true] [17592186314753 1074 "Note that the position vectors of the points $A,P$ and $Q$ are $a=⟨1,-1,1⟩,\\,p=⟨2,0,3⟩,$ and $q=⟨1,1,0⟩.$\n\nFirst, using the $3$ points we build $2$ vectors parallel to the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} & =\\overset{\\to }{AP} \\\\  & =⟨2,0,3⟩-⟨1,-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1,2⟩ \\\\ {v}_{2} & =\\overset{\\to }{AQ} \\\\  & =⟨1,1,0⟩-⟨1,-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,2,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\times \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-5i+j+2k \\\\  & =⟨-5,1,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-5,1,2⟩ & =⟨2,0,3⟩\\cdot ⟨-5,1,2⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-5,1,2⟩ & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-5,1,2⟩ & =-4 \\\\ -5x+y+2z & =-4 \\\\ -5x+y+2z+4 & =0 \\\\ 5x-y-2z-4 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535482 17592186045443 true] [17592186315010 1074 "First, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨2,4,1⟩-⟨6,-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4,5,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the equation of the plane, we get the normal vector $n=⟨-6,2,9⟩.$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|(-4)\\cdot (-6)+5\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 9|}{\\sqrt{(-6)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{9}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|24+10+9|}{\\sqrt{36+4+81}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|43|}{\\sqrt{121}} \\\\  & =\\frac{43}{11}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533420 17592186045417 true] [17592186317534 1074 "Let's sketch the situation.\n\n![](images/a8/a86fbc456930e18159b1745f5a71fe82bf7075f3aa6d9fd4307a71a583a37a7a.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The dotted line is the *perpendicular bisector* of the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$\n\n- All the points on this perpendicular bisector are equidistant to $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Our task is to find the point $Q$ on this line that's closest to $P.$\n\nFirst, we create a function $S$ which represents the *squared* distance between the point $P(5,-3)$ and a general point $Q(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-5)^{2}+(y-(-3))^{2} \\\\  & =(x-5)^{2}+(y+3)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-10x+6y+34\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required point $Q(x,y)$ is equidistant to the points $A(-2,1)$ and $B(6,1).$ So, by writing down $(QA)^{2}=(QB)^{2},$ we have the following constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{(x+2)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}}_{(QA)^{2}} & =\\underbrace{(x-6)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}}_{(QB)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe make $x$ the subject in the constraint equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =(x-6)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+4x+4+(y-1)^{2} & ={x}^{2}-12x+36+(y-1)^{2} \\\\ \\cancel{{x}^{2}}+\\cancel{(y-1)^{2}}+4x+4 & =\\cancel{{x}^{2}}-12x+36+\\cancel{(y-1)^{2}} \\\\ 4x+12x & =36-4 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our expression for $S(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(y) & =(2)^{2}+{y}^{2}-10(2)+6y+34 \\\\  & =4+{y}^{2}-20+6y+34 \\\\  & ={y}^{2}+6y+18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the first derivative,\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(y)=2y+6,\n$$\n\nand solve ${S}^{′}(y)=0$ to find the stationary point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ 2y+6 & =0 \\\\ y & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's verify that this stationary point is a minimum. Taking the second derivative, we find\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(-3)=2>0.\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the stationary point $y=-3$ indeed minimizes the squared distance. \n\nTherefore, the required point is $(\\,2,-3\\,).$" 13194139536958 17592186576411 true] [17592186317535 1074 "Let's sketch the situation.\n\n![](images/e2/e283660466c42f7723d9e044bb2eefe148c468f7dae5a1b5882e2589e7e5f60a.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The dotted line is the *perpendicular bisector* of the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$\n\n- All the points on this perpendicular bisector are equidistant to $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Our task is to find the point $Q$ on this line that's closest to $P.$\n\nFirst, we create a function $S$ which represents the *squared* distance between the point $P(4,5)$ and a general point $Q(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x,y) & =(x-4)^{2}+(y-5)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-4)^{2}+(y-5)^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8x-10y+41\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required point $Q(x,y)$ is equidistant to the points $A(-7,2)$ and $B(3,2).$ So, by writing down $(QA)^{2}=(QB)^{2},$ we have the following constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{(x+7)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}}_{(QA)^{2}} & =\\underbrace{(x-3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}}_{(QB)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe make $x$ the subject in the constraint equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+7)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =(x-3)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+14x+49+(y-2)^{2} & ={x}^{2}-6x+9+(y-2)^{2} \\\\ \\cancel{{x}^{2}}+\\cancel{(y-2)^{2}}+14x+49 & =\\cancel{{x}^{2}}-6x+9+\\cancel{(y-2)^{2}} \\\\ 14x+6x & =9-49 \\\\ x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our expression for $S(x,y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(y) & =(-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}-8(-2)-10y+41 \\\\  & =4+{y}^{2}+16-10y+41 \\\\  & ={y}^{2}-10y+61.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the first derivative,\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(y)=2y-10,\n$$\n\nand solve ${S}^{′}(y)=0$ to find the stationary point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ 2y-10 & =0 