 have expressions for the lengths of the opposite side $\\overset{―}{BC}$ and adjacent side $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ Hence, we can use the tangent ratio to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(m∠A) & =\\frac{BC}{AB} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡({45}^{∘}) & =\\frac{x+3}{2x} \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{x+3}{2x} \\\\ 2x & =x+3 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the side lengths are \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =x+3=3+3=6, \\\\ AB & =2x=2(3)=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of $△ABC$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot AB\\cdot BC \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045417 true] [17592186335937 1074 "The height (or altitude) corresponding to $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is a line segment that \n\n- runs from the opposite vertex $C$ to the line $\\overset{⟷}{AB},$ and\n\n- is perpendicular to $\\overset{⟷}{AB}.$\n\nTo draw the height, we proceed as follows:\n\n- Extend the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$\n\n![](images/ff/ff359409206e3762233b6db6159b00b69d95124e8c1bd21597c36a9fde535f3f.png)\n\n- Draw the perpendicular from $C$ to $\\overset{⟷}{AB}.$\n\n![](images/71/71ec54229cf44e84d09a0ba315318a185ac83a235df109d17428581d21154a93.png)" 13194139533428 17592186045417 true] [17592186335980 1074 "We want to find a \"path\" from $T$ to $S$ consisting only of the vectors $a,b,c,$ and $d.$\n\nAccording to the triangle law of addition, \n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{TS}=\\overset{\\to }{TP}+\\overset{\\to }{PS},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/6d/6d9225f220f50031fcbc7680c697b31eb8ce7733f07b83713c0dbe0c2ad89490.png)\n\n \n\nNow, from the original diagram, we have that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{TP}=-c,\\,\\overset{\\to }{PS}=a.\n$$\n\nLet's add these to our diagram.\n\n![](images/53/533805fc0d558bde49590a6dd82414de22efeeadbc76e91760bea33bcd0ceeff.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{TS} & =\\overset{\\to }{TP}+\\overset{\\to }{PS} \\\\  & =-c+a \\\\  & =a-c.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045417 true] [17592186335981 1074 "- For the $1$st expression, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC}+\\overset{\\to }{CB} & =(\\overset{\\to }{DA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC})+\\overset{\\to }{CB} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{DC}+\\overset{\\to }{CB} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{DB}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the $2$nd expression, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DA}+\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{DB}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the $3$rd expression, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{BA}-\\overset{\\to }{CA}+\\overset{\\to }{CD} & =(\\overset{\\to }{BA}-\\overset{\\to }{CA})+\\overset{\\to }{CD} \\\\  & =(\\overset{\\to }{BA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC})+\\overset{\\to }{CD} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{BC}+\\overset{\\to }{CD} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{BD} \\\\  & \\ne \\overset{\\to }{DB}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." 13194139533428 17592186045417 true] [17592186336003 1074 "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $K$ and parallel to $v$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=k+tv,\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $k$ is the position vector of $K.$ \n\nWe can find a direction vector by computing the displacement vector between the given points $K(-4,-3,-1)$ and $L(1,2,4),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{KL} \\\\  & =⟨1,2,4⟩-⟨-4,-3,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5,5,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector equation of the line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535483 17592186045443 true] [17592186336005 1074 "First, we find the vector equation of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =⟨1,0,0⟩+t⟨1,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSince $R$ lies on the line, we know that there must be 