}{\\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-1)={180}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045417 true] [17592186336008 1074 "Note that the position vectors of the points $A$, $B$, $C$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a=⟨1,-1,0⟩,\\,b=⟨2,-2,-1⟩,\\,c=⟨0,0,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $△ABC$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠ABC=\\theta .$ \n\n![](images/2c/2c81d48afacc4ac5e81ab501bc713009ea4425451fcf8dbf414672aeadbb1679.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{BA}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{BC}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{BA} & =a-b \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,0⟩-⟨2,-2,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,1,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{BC} & =c-b \\\\  & =⟨0,0,1⟩-⟨2,-2,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,2,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{BA}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{BC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{BA}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{BC}}{|\\overset{\\to }{BA}||\\overset{\\to }{BC}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 2}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{12}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{36}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(1)={0}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045417 true] [17592186336009 1074 "Note that the position vectors of the points $P$, $Q$, $R$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p=⟨2,1,2⟩,\\,q=⟨1,3,2⟩,\\,r=⟨4,5,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle $△PQR$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠QPR=\\theta .$\n\n![](images/30/3003f0cb329a8f8ef9bc315c70df4773ac1f0c54843dea1208c68bd0f6227bd1.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =q-p \\\\  & =⟨1,3,2⟩-⟨2,1,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,2,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =r-p \\\\  & =⟨4,5,-2⟩-⟨2,1,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,4,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{PR}}{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}||\\overset{\\to }{PR}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 4+0\\cdot (-4)}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}}\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}+(-4)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{36}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{6\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5})≈{63}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045417 true] [17592186336010 1074 "Note that the position vectors of the points $X$, $Y$, $Z$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=⟨-1,0,3⟩,\\,y=⟨-2,2,3⟩,\\,z=⟨-2,-1,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $△XYZ$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠XZY=\\theta .$ \n\n![](images/87/87453fecb9da6b9e673ab5a85a289a3fac73f4fb90fc8f1b33e7c0a80b1ea77f.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{ZX}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{ZY}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{ZX} & =x-z \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,3⟩-⟨-2,-1,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{ZY} & =y-z \\\\  & =⟨-2,2,3⟩-⟨-2,-1,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,3,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{ZX}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{ZY}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{ZX}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{ZY}}{|\\overset{\\to }{ZX}||\\overset{\\to }{ZY}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 3+0\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})={45}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139533428 17592186045417 true] [17592186338753 1074 "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=a+\\lambda b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $a.$ So, we begin with the vector $a,$ and then find the remaining vector needed to reach the point $C.$\n\n![](images/47/4797ffa54b3c2e4d177a6c3d527b1aaf3f1460d3c509bca86395e5f360314d25.png)\n\nNote that in $△OAC,$ we have:\n\n- $AC=3|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $b$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=3b.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =a+3b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=a+\\lambda b=a+3b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $b,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =3\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045417 true] [17592186338754 1074 "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=a+\\lambda b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $a.$ So, we begin with the vector $a,$ and then find the remaining vector needed to reach the point $C.$\n\n![](images/45/45a4735bf7710ff7c2699a9b42b10c34807d6162a3d7da38842ba87f13158788.png)\n\nNote that in $△OAC,$ we have:\n\n- $AC=\\frac{1}{2}|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $b$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=\\frac{1}{2}b.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =a+\\frac{1}{2}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=a+\\lambda b=a+\\frac{1}{2}b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $b,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045417 true] [17592186338756 1074 "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=a+\\lambda b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $a.$ So, we begin with the vector $a,$ and then find the remaining vector needed to reach the point $C.$\n\n![](images/b0/b0b7160eef056eccd922fbd6264cb247b9f787bb431c51ff3345be39aa26b872.png)\n\nNote that in $△OAC,$ we have:\n\n- $AC=1.5|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $b$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=1.5b.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =a+1.5b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=a+\\lambda b=a+1.5b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $b,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =1.5\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045417 true] [17592186338757 1074 "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $b.$ So, we begin with the vector $b,$ and then find the remaining vector needed to reach the point $C.$\n\n![](images/9b/9b99ad62bfedf6945020d345ef0bbc3531c58af1088698a78daf482cd4c14fe1.png)\n\nNote that in $△OBC,$ we have:\n\n- $BC=4|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{BC}$ and $a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{BC}=4a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OB}+\\overset{\\to }{BC} \\\\  & =b+4a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+b=b+4a.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =4\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045417 true] [17592186338785 1074 "We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OA}=a=2i+j, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{OB}=b=i+3j-3k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates of $a+2b$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+2b & =(2i+j)+2(i+3j-3k) \\\\  & =4i+7j-6k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a+2b| & =\\sqrt{(4)^{2}+(7)^{2}+(-6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+49+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{101}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045417 true] [17592186338834 1074 "To find the complex conjugate, we flip the sign of the imaginary part.\n\nIn the given number $z=-2-8\\text{i},$ the imaginary part is $-8\\text{i}.$ Flipping the sign, we get $8\\text{i}.$\n\nTherefore, the complex conjugate is given by $\\overset{―}{z}=-2+8\\text{i}.$" 13194139533429 17592186045417 true] [17592186338835 1074 "To find the complex conjugate, we flip the sign of the imaginary part.\n\nIn the given number $z=11\\text{i},$ the imaginary part is $11\\text{i}$ (there is no real part, just an imaginary part). Flipping the sign, we get $-11\\text{i}.$\n\nTherefore, the complex conjugate is given by $\\overset{―}{z}=-11\\text{i}.$" 13194139533429 17592186045417 true] [17592186338836 1074 "The number $z=\\frac{3}{4}$ is a real number, and the complex conjugate of any real number is just the number itself.\n\nSo here, the complex conjugate is $\\overset{―}{z}=z=\\frac{3}{4}.$" 13194139533429 17592186045417 true] [17592186338842 1074 "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨4,-2,3⟩.$\n\nSince the line  passes through the points $A(-1,0,1)$ and $B(1,0,-1)$ we can find a vector parallel to the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨1,0,-1⟩-⟨-1,0,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,0,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, a normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨2,0,-2⟩.$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,0,-2⟩ & =⟨4,-2,3⟩\\cdot ⟨2,0,-2⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,0,-2⟩ & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045417 true] [17592186338874 1074 "Using the definition of the standard Hermitian inner product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & ={x}_{1}\\overset{―}{{y}_{1}}+{x}_{2}\\overset{―}{{y}_{2}}+{x}_{3}\\overset{―}{{y}_{3}}+{x}_{4}\\overset{―}{{y}_{4}} \\\\  & =i\\cdot (\\overset{―}{\\,i})+(-i)\\cdot \\overset{―}{2}+i\\cdot (\\overset{―}{-\\text{i}})+(-1)\\cdot (\\overset{―}{-1}) \\\\  & =i\\cdot (-i)-i\\cdot 2+i\\cdot \\text{i}+(-1)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =1-2i+(-1)+1 \\\\  & =1-2i.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045417 true] [17592186338879 1074 "First, we need to find two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other). Let's use the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM} & =⟨2,5,-3⟩-⟨-1,-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,6,-4⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =⟨0,-8,-1⟩-⟨-1,-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-7,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we consider the position vector $l=⟨-1,-1,1⟩$ of the point $l,$ then the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =l+s\\overset{\\to }{LM}+t\\overset{\\to }{LN} \\\\ r & =⟨-1,-1,1⟩+s⟨3,6,-4⟩+t⟨1,-7,-2⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045417 true] [17592186338881 1074 "First, we need to find two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other). Let's use the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{UV}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{UW},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{UV} & =⟨3,4,-3⟩-⟨2,-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,5,-3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{UW} & =⟨2,0,0⟩-⟨2,-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we consider the position vector $u=⟨2,-1,0⟩$ of the point $U,$ then the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =u+s\\overset{\\to }{UV}+t\\overset{\\to }{UW} \\\\ r & =⟨2,-1,0⟩+s⟨1,5,-3⟩+t⟨0,1,0⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045417 true] [17592186338894 1074 "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{WX}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{WY}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{WX} & =x-w \\\\  & =(-2i+j+2k)-(2i-3j+k) \\\\  & =-4i+4j+k \\\\  & =⟨-4,4,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{WY} & =y-w \\\\  & =(3i-j+3k)-(2i-3j+k) \\\\  & =i+2j+2k \\\\  & =⟨1,2,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{WX}\\times \\overset{\\to }{WY} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -4 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}-4 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(4\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 2)i-((-4)\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 1)j+((-4)\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 4)k \\\\  & =6i+9j-12k \\\\  & =⟨6,9,-12⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{WX}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{WY}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{WX}\\times \\overset{\\to }{WY}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+{9}^{2}+{12}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{261}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,WXY$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{261}}{2}.\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045417 true] [17592186338895 1074 "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{NP}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{NQ}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{NP} & =p-n \\\\  & =(2i+2j+3k)-(i+k) \\\\  & =i+2j+2k \\\\  & =⟨1,2,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{NQ} & =q-n \\\\  & =(2i+j+k)-(i+k) \\\\  & =i+j \\\\  & =⟨1,1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{NP}\\times \\overset{\\to }{NQ} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 0-1\\cdot 2)i-(1\\cdot 0-1\\cdot 2)j+(1\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 2)k \\\\  & =-2i+2j-k \\\\  & =⟨-2,2,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{NP}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{NQ}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{NP}\\times \\overset{\\to }{NQ}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,NPQ$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045417 true] [17592186338896 1074 "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{FG}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{FH}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{FG} & =g-f \\\\  & =(2i+2j+3k)-(i+j+2k) \\\\  & =i+j+k \\\\  & =⟨1,1,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{FH} & =h-f \\\\  & =(i+k)-(i+j+2k) \\\\  & =-j-k \\\\  & =⟨0,-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{FG}\\times \\overset{\\to }{FH} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(1\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot 1)i-(1\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot 1)j+(1\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot 1)k \\\\  & =j-k \\\\  & =⟨0,1,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{FG}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{FH}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{FG}\\times \\overset{\\to }{FH}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,FGH$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045417 true] [17592186338898 1074 "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{CD}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{CE}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{CD} & =d-c \\\\  & =(i-3j+3k)-(i-2j+3k) \\\\  & =-j \\\\  & =⟨0,-1,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{CE} & =e-c \\\\  & =(i+j+k)-(i-2j+3k) \\\\  & =3j-2k \\\\  & =⟨0,3,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{CD}\\times \\overset{\\to }{CE} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot (-2)-3\\cdot 0)i-(0\\cdot (-2)-0\\cdot 0)j+(0\\cdot 3-0\\cdot (-1))k \\\\  & =2i \\\\  & =⟨2,0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{CD}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{CE}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{CD}\\times \\overset{\\to }{CE}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,CDE$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=1.\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045417 true] [17592186338899 1074 "Note that the length of the altitude $MH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,LMN$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}MH\\cdot LN\n$$\n\nSo, to find $MH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM} & =m-l \\\\  & =(i+j+2k)-(3i+3j+2k) \\\\  & =-2i-2j \\\\  & =⟨-2,-2,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =n-l \\\\  & =(2i+2j+k)-(3i+3j+2k) \\\\  & =-i-j-k \\\\  & =⟨-1,-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot 0)i-((-2)\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot 0)j+((-2)\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot (-2))k \\\\  & =2i-2j \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{LM}\\times \\overset{\\to }{LN}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,LMN$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{2}=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $LN$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}LN & =|\\overset{\\to }{LN}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}MH\\cdot LN,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}MH\\cdot LN \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}MH\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2} & =MH\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\ MH & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ MH & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045417 true] [17592186338900 1074 "Note that the length of the altitude $VH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,TUV$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}VH\\cdot TU\n$$\n\nSo, to find $VH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{TU}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{TV}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{TU} & =u-t \\\\  & =j-(3i+2j+3k) \\\\  & =-3i-j-3k \\\\  & =⟨-3,-1,-3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{TV} & =v-t \\\\  & =(2i+j+2k)-(3i+2j+3k) \\\\  & =-i-j-k \\\\  & =⟨-1,-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{TU}\\times \\overset{\\to }{TV} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & -1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot (-3))i-((-3)\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot (-3))j+((-3)\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot (-1))k \\\\  & =-2i+2k \\\\  & =⟨-2,0,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{TU}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{TV}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{TU}\\times \\overset{\\to }{TV}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,TUV$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{2}=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $TU$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TU & =|\\overset{\\to }{TU}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}VH\\cdot TU,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}VH\\cdot TU \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}VH\\cdot \\sqrt{19} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2} & =VH\\cdot \\sqrt{19} \\\\ VH & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{19}} \\\\ VH & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{38}}{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045417 true] [17592186338901 1074 "Note that the length of the altitude $ZH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,WYZ$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}ZH\\cdot WY\n$$\n\nSo, to find $ZH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{WY}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{WZ}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{WY} & =y-w \\\\  & =(i+2j+k)-(2i+3j+2k) \\\\  & =-i-j-k \\\\  & =⟨-1,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{WZ} & =z-w \\\\  & =(i-j+k)-(2i+3j+2k) \\\\  & =-i-4j-k \\\\  & =⟨-1,-4,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{WY}\\times \\overset{\\to }{WZ} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -4 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot (-1)-(-4)\\cdot (-1))i-((-1)\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot (-1))j+((-1)\\cdot (-4)-(-1)\\cdot (-1))k \\\\  & =-3i+3k \\\\  & =⟨-3,0,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{WY}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{WZ}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{WY}\\times \\overset{\\to }{WZ}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,WYZ$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $WY$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}WY & =|\\overset{\\to }{WY}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}ZH\\cdot WY,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}ZH\\cdot WY \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}ZH\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 3\\sqrt{2} & =ZH\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\ ZH & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ ZH & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045417 true] [17592186338902 1074 "Note that the length of the altitude $GH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,GSM$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}GH\\cdot SM\n$$\n\nSo, to find $GH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{SG}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{SM}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{SG} & =g-s \\\\  & =(-2i+2k)-(i-j+2k) \\\\  & =-3i+j \\\\  & =⟨-3,1,0⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{SM} & =m-s \\\\  & =(i+j)-(i-j+2k) \\\\  & =2j-2k \\\\  & =⟨0,2,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{SG}\\times \\overset{\\to }{SM} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(1\\cdot (-2)-2\\cdot 0)i-((-3)\\cdot (-2)-0\\cdot 0)j+((-3)\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 1)k \\\\  & =-2i-6j-6k \\\\  & =⟨-2,-6,-6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{SG}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{SM}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{SG}\\times \\overset{\\to }{SM}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-6)^{2}+(-6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{76} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,GSM$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{2\\sqrt{19}}{2}=\\sqrt{19}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $SM$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}SM & =|\\overset{\\to }{SM}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}GH\\cdot SM,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}GH\\cdot SM \\\\ \\sqrt{19} & =\\frac{1}{2}GH\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{19} & =GH\\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\\\ GH & =\\frac{\\sqrt{19}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ GH & =\\frac{\\sqrt{38}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045417 true] [17592186338904 1074 "Note that the length of the altitude $DH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,DMA$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}DH\\cdot MA\n$$\n\nSo, to find $DH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{MD}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{MA}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{MD} & =d-m \\\\  & =(-i+2j+k)-(-i-k) \\\\  & =2j+2k \\\\  & =⟨0,2,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{MA} & =a-m \\\\  & =(i-2j+k)-(-i-k) \\\\  & =2i-2j+2k \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{MD}\\times \\overset{\\to }{MA} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(2\\cdot 2-(-2)\\cdot 2)i-(0\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 2)j+(0\\cdot (-2)-2\\cdot 2)k \\\\  & =8i+4j-4k \\\\  & =⟨8,4,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{MD}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{MA}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{MD}\\times \\overset{\\to }{MA}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{8}^{2}+{4}^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{96} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,DMA$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{4\\sqrt{6}}{2}=2\\sqrt{6}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $MA$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}MA & =|\\overset{\\to }{MA}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{12} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}DH\\cdot MA,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}DH\\cdot MA \\\\ 2\\sqrt{6} & =\\frac{1}{2}DH\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{6} & =DH\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\ DH & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ DH & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045417 true] [17592186339037 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nWhen we add a complex number and its conjugate, the result is always twice the real part of the complex number.\n\nFor the given complex number $z=3+5\\text{i},$ the real part is $Re(z)=3,$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+\\overset{―}{z} & =2\\text{Re}(z) \\\\  & =2(3) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nAlternatively, we can actually carry out the process of adding the complex number and its conjugate.\n\nTo obtain the conjugate of the complex number $z=3+5\\text{i},$ we flip the sign of the imaginary part and get $\\overset{―}{z}=3-5\\text{i}.$\n\nAdding, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+\\overset{―}{z} & =(3+5\\text{i})+(3-5\\text{i}) \\\\  & =(3+3)+\\text{i}(5-5) \\\\  & =6+\\text{i}0 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045417 true] [17592186339038 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nWhen we add a complex number and its conjugate, the result is always twice the real part of the complex number.\n\nFor the given complex number $z=4+7\\text{i},$ the real part is $Re(z)=4,$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+\\overset{―}{z} & =2\\text{Re}(z) \\\\  & =2(4) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nAlternatively, we can actually carry out the process of adding the complex number and its conjugate.\n\nTo obtain the conjugate of the complex number $z=4+7\\text{i},$ we flip the sign of the imaginary part and get $\\overset{―}{z}=4-7\\text{i}.$\n\nAdding, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+\\overset{―}{z} & =(4+7\\text{i})+(4-7\\text{i}) \\\\  & =(4+4)+\\text{i}(7-7) \\\\  & =8+\\text{i}0 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045417 true] [17592186339039 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nWhen we add a complex number and its conjugate, the result is always twice the real part of the complex number.\n\nFor the given complex number $z=-8+2\\text{i},$ the real part is $Re(z)=-8,$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+\\overset{―}{z} & =2\\text{Re}(z) \\\\  & =2(-8) \\\\  & =-16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nAlternatively, we can actually carry out the process of adding the complex number and its conjugate.\n\nTo obtain the conjugate of the complex number $z=-8+2\\text{i},$ we flip the sign of the imaginary part and get $\\overset{―}{z}=-8-2\\text{i}.$\n\nAdding, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+\\overset{―}{z} & =(-8+2\\text{i})+(-8-2\\text{i}) \\\\  & =(-8+(-8))+\\text{i}(2-2) \\\\  & =-16+\\text{i}0 \\\\  & =-16.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045417 true] [17592186339040 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nWhen we multiply a complex number and its conjugate, the result is always the sum of squares of the real part and the imaginary part.\n\nFor the given complex number $z=8+6\\text{i},$ the real part is $\\text{Re}(z)=8,$ and the imaginary part is $\\text{Im}(z)=6,$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z\\overset{―}{z} & =(\\text{Re}(z))^{2}+(\\text{Im}(z))^{2} \\\\  & ={8}^{2}+{6}^{2} \\\\  & =64+36 \\\\  & =100.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nAlternatively, we can actually carry out the process of multiplying the complex number and its conjugate.\n\nTo obtain the conjugate of the complex number $z=8+6\\text{i},$ we flip the sign of the imaginary part and get $\\overset{―}{z}=8-6\\text{i}.$\n\nMultiplying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z\\overset{―}{z} & =(8+6\\text{i})(8-6\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={8}^{2}+(8)(6)\\text{i}+(-6)(8)\\text{i}-{\\text{i}}^{2}(6)^{2} \\\\  & =64+48\\text{i}-48\\text{i}-(-1)(36) \\\\  & =64+36 \\\\  & =100.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045417 true] [17592186339085 1074 "**Method 1**\n\nWhen we multiply a complex number and its conjugate, the result is always the sum of squares of the real part and the imaginary part.\n\nFor the given complex number $z=-1-7\\text{i},$ the real part is $\\text{Re}(z)=-1,$ and the imaginary part is $\\text{Im}(z)=-7,$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z\\overset{―}{z} & =(\\text{Re}(z))^{2}+(\\text{Im}(z))^{2} \\\\  & =(-1)^{2}+(-7)^{2} \\\\  & =1+49 \\\\  & =50.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nAlternatively, we can actually carry out the process of multiplying the complex number and its conjugate.\n\nTo obtain the conjugate of the complex number $z=-1-7\\text{i},$ we flip the sign of the imaginary part and get $\\overset{―}{z}=-1+7\\text{i}.$\n\nMultiplying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z\\overset{―}{z} & =(-1-7\\text{i})(-1+7\\text{i}) \\\\  & =(-1)^{2}+(-1)(7)\\text{i}+(-7)(-1)\\text{i}-{\\text{i}}^{2}(7)^{2} \\\\  & =1-7\\text{i}+7\\text{i}-(-1)(49) \\\\  & =1+49 \\\\  & =50.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533429 17592186045417 true] [17592186341859 1074 "The diagonal $\\overset{―}{AC}$ divides the parallelogram into two congruent triangles. Therefore, the area of the parallelogram is twice the area of $△ACB$ (see the illustration below).\n\n![](images/7c/7cfcb5cdf77b4afbf25afd64e37bb33f0ab2c211e09659181bfc16db0ccd2db7.png)\n\n Computing the area of $△ACB,$ we find\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ACD} & =\\frac{1}{2}(18)(8)\\operatorname{sin}⁡{70}^{∘} \\\\  & =72\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{70}^{∘} \\\\  & ≈67.658,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places. Consequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{ABCD} & =2{A}_{ACB} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 67.658 \\\\  & =135.316.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to the nearest integer, the area of the parallelogram is $135.$" 13194139533430 17592186045417 true] [17592186341883 1074 "For a function $f(x,y),$ the double integral of $f$ over a rectangular region $R$ is the unique number $I$ (if it exists) such that\n\n$$\nL(f,P)\\le I\\le U(f,P)\n$$\n\nover all partitions $P$ of $R.$\n\nFirst, notice that ${\\overset{¯}{x}}_{i}=\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})$ will always lie between ${x}_{i-1}$ and ${x}_{i}$, and similarly ${\\overset{¯}{y}}_{j}=\\frac{1}{2}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})$ will always lie between ${y}_{j-1}$ and ${y}_{j}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n3{x}_{i-1}+{y}_{j-1}-1\\le 3\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})+\\frac{1}{2}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})-1\\le 3{x}_{i}+{y}_{j}-1\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to \n\n$$\n3{x}_{i-1}+{y}_{j-1}-1\\le \\frac{3}{2}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})+\\frac{1}{2}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})-1\\le 3{x}_{i}+{y}_{j}-1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying this by $Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}$ and applying the double summation gives:\n\n$$\nL(f,P)\\le {\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[\\frac{3}{2}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})+\\frac{1}{2}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})-1]Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\le U(f,P)\n$$\n\nSeparating the sums using the addition and constant multiple rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & \\le \\frac{3}{2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\  & \\,+\\frac{1}{2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\  & \\,-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\  & \\le U(f,P).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the hint, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} & =\\frac{3}{2}({4}^{2}-{1}^{2})(4-2)=45 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} & =\\frac{1}{2}({4}^{2}-{2}^{2})(4-1)=18 \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} & =(4-1)(4-2)=6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P)\\le 45+18-6\\le U(f,P)\\,⟹\\,L(f,P)\\le 57\\le U(f,P)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=57.\n$$" 13194139533430 17592186045417 true] [17592186341884 1074 "For a function $