d{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(4-3\\text{i}){x}_{1}+5{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}+(-4-3\\text{i}){x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(1+3\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5}(-4-3\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{5}(-4-3\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-5,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}4+3\\text{i} \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, to compute the second eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=1-3\\text{i}$, we simply compute the complex conjugate of ${\\overset{―}{v}}_{1},$ and get\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}4-3\\text{i} \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{(-5)(-5)}{(4+3\\text{i})(4-3\\text{i})}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535661 17592186045443 true] [17592186351247 1074 "The complement of $A,$ denoted $\\overset{―}{A},$ consists of every element in the universal set $U$ that is not in the set $A.$\n\nThe elements of $U$ that are not in $A$ are $2,$ $4,$ $6,$ $8,$ and $10.$\n\nTherefore, the complement of $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{A}={2,4,6,8,10}.\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045417 true] [17592186351248 1074 "The complement of $S,$ denoted $\\overset{―}{S},$ consists of every element in the universal set $U$ that is not in the set $S.$\n\nThe only elements of $U$ that are not in $S$ are $p,$ $r,$ and $u.$\n\nTherefore, the complement of $S$ is\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{S}={p,r,u}.\n$$" 13194139533433 17592186045417 true] [17592186351254 1074 