ks." 13194139535488 17592186045443 true] [17592186367102 1074 "Notice that we are given a non-scalar $1\\times 3$ matrix that has the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ of algebraic multiplicity $3.$ \n\nAs a result, there are two possibilities for the Jordan blocks corresponding to $\\lambda =1:$ \n\n- *Case I.* We may get the single $3\\times 3$ Jordan block ${J}_{3}(1).$ This happens when the generalized eigenvectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ corresponding to $\\lambda =1$ form the following chain:\n\n$$\n{v}_{3}\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{2}\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }0\n$$\n\n- *Case II.* We may get a composition of one $2\\times 2$ and one $1\\times 1$ Jordan blocks, say ${J}_{2}(1)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(1).$ This happens when the generalized eigenvectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ corresponding to $\\lambda =1$ form the following chains:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2}\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }0 &  \\\\ {v}_{3}\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo figure out which of the cases we obtain in this situation, let's find the number of linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to $\\lambda =1.$\n\nComputing $A-I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce this matrix to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\text{nullity}(A-I)=1,$ there will be only one eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1,$ say, ${v}_{1}.$ So, the generalized eigenvectors corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ form the following chain: \n\n$$\n{v}_{3}\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{2}\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have *case I*, and $A$ has the following Jordan canonical form:\n\n$$\n{J}_{3}(1)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \n\n**Note:** The Jordan canonical form is unique up to the order of the Jordan blocks." 13194139535488 17592186045443 true] [17592186367109 1074 "Since the Jordan block $J$ has a single $3\\times 3$ Jordan block corresponding to $\\lambda =-6,$ the columns of $P$ form the following chain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{3}\\overset{A+6I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{2}\\overset{A+6I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A+6I\\,}{\\to }0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that \n\n- the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvector ${v}_{1}$ of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-6,$\n\n- the second column of $P$ corresponds to the vector ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ such that $(A+6I){v}_{2}={v}_{1},$\n\n- the third column of $P$ corresponds to the vector ${v}_{3}$ such that $(A+6I){v}_{3}={v}_{2}.$\n\nNow, computing $A+6I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+6I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -7\\end{bmatrix}+6\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(A+6I){v}_{3}={v}_{2}$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} 