ix}}_{\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v})}+\\text{i}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v}) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v}) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536958 17592186576411 true] [17592186367999 1074 "We know that the line passes through the points $P(1,1,5)$ and $Q(1,-2,4).$ Now, we need to find a vector parallel to the given line. Let's consider the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ which is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨1,-2,4⟩-⟨1,1,5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-3,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the point $P$ and the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$, we have that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the given line is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-1}{0}=\\frac{y-1}{-3}=\\frac{z-5}{-1}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-1}{0}=\\frac{y-1}{-3}=\\frac{z-5}{-1}.\n$$\n\nBut the above equation is problematic because $\\frac{x-1}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, in our line, the $x$ component always maintains the constant value of $x=1.$ For example, our two points $P(1,1,5)$ and $Q(1,-2,4)$ both have $x$-coordinates of $x=1.$\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\frac{x-1}{0}$ part and instead write $x=1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{y-1}{-3}=\\frac{z-5}{-1},\\,x=1.\n$$" 13194139533451 17592186045417 true] [17592186368521 1074 "Recall that if $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ real matrix that has complex eigenvalues \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{4}$ \n\nwith corresponding eigenvectors \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{3}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{4},$ \n\nthen $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{3}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{3}) \\\\ 0 & 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{3}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{3})\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ \\text{Re}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Re}({v}_{3}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the first and second columns of $P$ correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and the third and forth columns of $P$ correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{3}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" 13194139535488 17592186045443 true] [17592186369707 1074 "The orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -3 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}18 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot