0,$ we conclude that $c$ does not belong to $A\\cap B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533455 17592186045417 true] [17592186376714 1074 "First, we recall the definition of an injection:\n\nA function $f:X\\to Y$ is an injection if every element $x\\in X$ is mapped to a distinct element in $Y:$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\ne {x}_{2}\\,⇒\\,f({x}_{1})\\ne f({x}_{2})\n$$\n\nIn this case, it would be easier to prove the contrapositive statement. \n\nThis implication is equivalent to its contrapositive:\n\n$$\nf({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2})\\,⇒\\,{x}_{1}={x}_{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we simply need to derive ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}$ by assuming that $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ where ${x}_{1}=({z}_{1},{w}_{1})$ and ${x}_{2}=({z}_{2},{w}_{2}):$\n\nSo, let $({z}_{1},{w}_{1}),({z}_{2},{w}_{2})\\in C\\times C,$ and suppose that $f({z}_{1},{w}_{1})=f({z}_{2},{w}_{2}).$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({z}_{1},{w}_{1}) & =f({z}_{2},{w}_{2}) \\\\ (\\overset{―}{{z}_{1}-{w}_{1}},{z}_{1}\\text{i}+{w}_{1}) & =(\\overset{―}{{z}_{2}-{w}_{2}},{z}_{2}\\text{i}+{w}_{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince two pairs are equal if and only if their respective components are equal, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{z}_{1}-{w}_{1}} &=\\overset{―}{{z}_{2}-{w}_{2}} \\\\ {z}_{1}\\text{i}+{w}_{1} &={z}_{2}\\text{i}+{w}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\right. & \\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1}-{w}_{1} &={z}_{2}-{w}_{2} \\\\ {z}_{1}\\text{i}+{w}_{1} &={z}_{2}\\text{i}+{w}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\right. \\\\  & \\,⇒\\,{z}_{1}+{z}_{1}\\text{i}={z}_{2}+{z}_{2}\\text{i} \\\\  & \\,⇒\\,{z}_{1}(1+\\text{i})={z}_{2}(1+\\text{i}) \\\\  & \\,⇒\\,{z}_{1}={z}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1}\\text{i}+{w}_{1} & ={z}_{1}\\text{i}+{w}_{2} \\\\ {w}_{1} & ={w}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we state the conclusion:\n\nHence, $({z}_{1},{w}_{1})=({z}_{2},{w}_{2}),$ as was to be shown. Therefore, $f$ is an injection." 13194139535661 17592186045443 true] [17592186376891 1074 "To prove a set equality using biconditionals, we must show an element belongs to one set if and only if it belongs to the other.\n\nIn this case, we're required to prove De Morgan's Law for disjunctions:\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{A\\cup B}=\\overset{―}{A}\\cap \\overset{―}{B}\n$$\n\nSince we're proving this statement using a sequence of biconditionals, we begin our proof as follows:\n\nFor any element $x,$ the sequence of logical equivalences unfolds as follows:\n\nFirst, we assume $x\\in \\overset{―}{A\\cup B}$ and apply the definition of the set complement:\n\nTo belong to $\\overset{―}{A\\cup B}$ is synonymous with not belonging to $A\\cup B:$\n\n$$\nx\\in \\overset{―}{A\\cup B}\\,⇔\\,x\\notin A\\cup B\n$$\n\nNext, we apply the definition of negation and the definition of union:\n\nNot being a member of the union $A\\cup B$ is equivalent to not satisfying the conditions $x\\in A$ or $x\\in B$ individually: \n\n$$\nx\\notin A\\cup B\\,⇔\\,¬(x\\in A∨x\\in B)\n$$\n\nNext, we apply De Morgan's law for disjunctions: $¬(P∨Q)≡¬P∧¬Q:$\n\nThe negation of the conditions individually is the same as the conjunction of their negations by De Morgan's Law for disjunction:\n\n$$\n¬(x\\in A∨x\\in B)\\,⇔\\,¬(x\\in A)∧¬(x\\in B)\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of negation gives:\n\nThe conjunction of the negations corresponds to not belonging to $A$ and not belonging to $B:$\n\n$$\n¬(x\\in A)∧¬(x\\in B)\\,⇔\\,(x\\notin A)∧(x\\notin B)\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the definition of the set complement and intersection gives:\n\nConsequently, the combined condition is satisfied if and only if $x$ belongs to the intersections of the complements, sets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x\\notin A)∧(x\\notin B)\\, & ⇔\\,(x\\in \\overset{―}{A})∧(x\\in \\overset{―}{B}) \\\\  & ⇔\\,x\\in \\overset{―}{A}\\cap \\overset{―}{B}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFollowing the sequence of equivalent statements, we've shown that an element belongs to one set if and only if it belongs to the other. Thus, our proof is complete, and we write down our conclusion.\n\nThus, it follows that for any element $x,$ membership in $\\overset{―}{A\\cup B}$ is equivalent to membership in $\\overset{―}{A}\\cap \\overset{―}{B},$ leading to the conclusion:\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{A\\cup B}=\\overset{―}{A}\\cap \\overset{―}{B}\n$$" 13194139533455 17592186045417 true] [17592186378004 1074 "We have the linear Diophantine equation\n\n$$\nax+by=c\n$$\n\nwhere $a=7,$ $b=9,$ and $c=11.$ We're given that the equation has solutions.\n\nNotice that our equation can't be solved for either of the two variables using integer coefficients. So, let's find the linear representation of $\\text{gcd}(7,9)$ using the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}9 & = & 7\\cdot 1 & + & 2 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 7 & = & 2\\cdot 3 & + & 1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the remainders (the rightmost terms) in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =9-7 \\\\ 1 & =7-2\\cdot 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =7-2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =7-3(9-7) \\\\  & =7\\cdot 4+(-3)\\cdot 9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that $d=\\text{gcd}(a,b)=1$ and\n\n$$\n7\\cdot 4+9\\cdot (-3)=1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying this equation by $11,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7\\cdot 4+9\\cdot (-3) & =1 \\\\ 7\\cdot 4\\cdot 11+9\\cdot (-3)\\cdot 11 & =11 \\\\ 7\\cdot 44+9\\cdot (-33) & =11,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that $(x,y)=(44,-33)$ is a solution to our Diophantine equation.\n\nNow, the general solution is\n\n$$\n(44+\\frac{b}{d}t,\\,-33-\\frac{a}{d}t)=(44+9t,\\,-33-7t),\n$$\n\nwhere $t=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…\\,.$\n\nThe smallest possible positive value of $x=44+9t$ is attained when $t=-4.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{∼}{x}=44+9\\cdot (-4)=8.\n$$" 13194139533455 17592186045417 true] [17592186378011 1074 "We have the linear Diophantine equation\n\n$$\nax+by=c\n$$\n\nwhere $a=7,$ $b=3,$ and $c=5.$ We're given that the equation has solutions.\n\nNotice that our equation can't be solved for either of the two variables using integer coefficients. So, let's find the linear representation of $\\text{gcd}(7,3)$ using the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}7 & = & 3\\cdot 2 & + & 1 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 3 & = & 1\\cdot 3 & + & 0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the remainders (the rightmost terms) in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =7-3\\cdot 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that $d=\\text{gcd}(a,b)=1$ and\n\n$$\n7+3\\cdot (-2)=1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying this equation by $5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7+3\\cdot (-2) & =1 \\\\ 7\\cdot 5+3\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 5 & =5 \\\\ 7\\cdot 5+3\\cdot (-10) & =5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that $(x,y)=(5,-10)$ is a solution to our Diophantine equation.\n\nNow, the general solution is\n\n$$\n(5+\\frac{b}{d}t,\\,-10-\\frac{a}{d}t)=(5+3t,\\,-10-7t),\n$$\n\nwhere $t=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…\\,.$\n\nThe smallest possible positive value of $y=-10-7t$ is attained when $t=-2.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{∼}{y}=-10-7\\cdot (-2)=4.\n$$" 13194139533455 17592186045417 true] [17592186378573 1074 "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(-j+3k)-(3i+k) \\\\  & =-3i-j+2k \\\\  & =⟨-3,-1,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(i-2j+2k)-(3i+k) \\\\  & =-2i-2j+k \\\\  & =⟨-2,-2,1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =d-a \\\\  & =(2i+2j+k)-(3i+k) \\\\  & =-i+2j \\\\  & =⟨-1,2,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }