92186045417 true] [17592186388181 1074 "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that the function $f(x,y)=6{x}^{4}{y}^{3}$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨1,1⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1)-f(0,0) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨1,1⟩-⟨0,0⟩) \\\\ 6-0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,1⟩ \\\\ 6 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨24{x}^{3}{y}^{3},\\,18{x}^{4}{y}^{2}⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨24{{c}_{1}}^{3}{{c}_{2}}^{3},\\,18{{c}_{1}}^{4}{{c}_{2}}^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6 & =∇f⟨c⟩\\cdot ⟨1,1⟩ \\\\ 6 & =⟨24{{c}_{1}}^{3}{{c}_{2}}^{3},\\,18{{c}_{1}}^{4}{{c}_{2}}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,1⟩ \\\\ 6 & =24{{c}_{1}}^{3}{{c}_{2}}^{3}+18{{c}_{1}}^{4}{{c}_{2}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{a}}{{y}_{b}-{y}_{a}} & =\\frac{x-{x}_{a}}{{x}_{b}-{x}_{a}} \\\\ \\frac{y-0}{1-0} & =\\frac{x-0}{1-0} \\\\ y & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & ={c}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the equation $24{{c}_{1}}^{3}{{c}_{2}}^{3}+18{{c}_{1}}^{4}{{c}_{2}}^{2}=6,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24{{c}_{1}}^{3}{{c}_{1}}^{3}+18{{c}_{1}}^{4}{{c}_{1}}^{2} & =6 \\\\ 42{{c}_{1}}^{6} & =6 \\\\ {{c}_{1}}^{6} & =\\frac{1}{7} \\\\ {c}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[6]{7}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find ${c}_{2}={c}_{1}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[6]{7}}.$ So, the point $C$ that satisfies the mean value theorem is $(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[6]{7}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[6]{7}}).$" 13194139533465 17592186045417 true] [17592186388389 1074 "Recall that if $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ real matrix that has complex eigenvalues \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{4}$ \n\nwith corresponding eigenvectors \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{3}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{4},$ \n\nthen $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{3}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{3}) \\\\ 0 & 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{3}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{3})\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ \\text{Re}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Re}({v}_{3}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the first and second columns of $P$ correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and the third and forth columns of $P$ correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{3}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 2 & 5 & -5 \\\\ -2 & -7 & -7 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -2 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -5 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" 13194139535490 17592186045443 true] [17592186388396 1074 "Recall that if $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ real matrix that has complex eigenvalues \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{4}$ \n\nwith corresponding eigenvectors \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{3}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{4},$ \n\nthen $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{3}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{3}) \\\\ 0 & 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{3}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{3})\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | & | \\\\ \\text{Re}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Re}({v}_{3}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the first and second columns of $P$ correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and the third and forth columns of $P$ correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{3}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -6 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 5 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & -7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" 13194139535490 17592186045443 true] [17592186388423 1074 "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{3},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{1}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{2},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 \\\\ -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {\\lambda }_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ \\text{Re}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{1}) & {v}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the first and second columns of $P$ will