\\ {v}_{1} & \\text{Re}({v}_{2}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -8 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the first column of $P$ will correspond to the real eigenvector ${v}_{1}$ and the second and third columns of $P$ will correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139535490 17592186045443 true] [17592186388744 1074 "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\ne C,\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda })\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=C.\n$$\n\nSo, our rotation-scaling matrix $C$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\overset{―}{\\lambda }=2-3\\text{i},$ \n the complex conjugate of $\\lambda .$ Hence, to construct the matrix $P,$ we use the eigenvector $\\overset{―}{v},$ the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nNext, we find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{v} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1-2\\text{i} \\\\ 2-\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v})}+\\text{i}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v}) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v}) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536958 17592186576411 true] [17592186388799 1074 "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}}^{x} & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}}^{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\hat{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\,\\,\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 14 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 28 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{84}\\begin{bmatrix}21 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 14\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 28 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536958 17592186576411 true] [17592186388834 1074 "We want to find a \"path\" from $Q$ to $S$ consisting only of the vectors $a,b,c,$ and $d.$ \n\nAccording to the triangle law of addition,\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{QS}=\\overset{\\to }{QP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR}+\\overset{\\to }{RS}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/31/317d78cec35bc3721ebbd64ac1ed53aa5b36c89101a170b93805d17c670f2673.png)\n\n  \n\nNow, from the original diagram, we have that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{QP}=a,\\,\\overset{\\to }{PR}=-d,\\,\\overset{\\to }{RT}=b.\n$$\n\nLet's add these to our diagram.\n\n![](images/ff/ff4b4292bb8a15551cb22da1f621684e9fdf24d35ae873b408dc612e5b3255e8.png)\n\n   \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{QS} & =\\overset{\\to }{QP}+\\overset{\\to }{PR}+\\overset{\\to }{RS} \\\\  & =a-d+b.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533466 17592186045417 true] [17592186388869 1074 "We want to find a \"path\" from $T$ to $P$ consisting only of the vectors $a,b,c,$ and $d.$ \n\nAccording to the triangle law of addition,\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{TP}=\\overset{\\to }{TR}+\\overset{\\to }{RQ}+\\overset{\\to }{QP}\n$$\n\n \n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/e0/e0e6351dfbef6dadf6a6fb179f1545622ddaeab24c2c06dee03c87821304695c.png)\n\n \n\nNow, from the original diagram, we have that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{TR}=-d,\\,\\overset{\\to }{RQ}=b,\\,\\overset{\\to }{QP}=a.\n$$\n\nLet's add these to our diagram. \n\n![](images/02/02899ac9c536b0020514780db617e29977a5dc4daf60f42d80ed671883327fe3.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{TP} & =\\overset{\\to }{TR}+\\overset{\\to }{RQ}+\\overset{\\to }{QP} \\\\  & =-d+b+a \\\\  & =a+b-d.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533466 17592186045417 true] [17592186389490 1074 "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $A=2\\cdot 2=4\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.$\nSince the plate has a uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{(\\frac{3}{2})}{4}=\\frac{3}{8}\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2},\n$$\n\nand its center of mass is its centroid:\n\n$$\n(\\overset{¯}{x},\\overset{¯}{y})=(\\frac{-1+1}{2},\\frac{0+2}{2})=(0,1)\n$$\n\nThen, the moment of inertia about the perpendicular line through the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{M} & ={∬}_{D}((x-\\overset{¯}{x})^{2}+(y-\\overset{¯}{y})^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3{x}^{2}+3(y-1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{[3{x}^{2}y+(y-1)^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}6{x}^{2}+2\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{[2{x}^{3}+2x]}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(2+2-(-2-2)) \\\\  & =1\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the parallel axis theorem, the moment of inertia about a line $\\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{m}$ from the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M \\\\  & =1+{(\\sqrt{6})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =1+9 \\\\  & =10\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533466 17592186045417 true] [17592186389497 1074 "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nFirst, we find