et{―}{z}=z=-8.$" 13194139533495 17592186045417 true] [17592186403871 1074 "First, we find the vector equation of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =⟨1,1,1⟩+t⟨1,-1,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSince $Q$ lies on the line, we know that there must be a real number $t$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OQ} & =⟨1,1,1⟩+t⟨1,-1,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(⟨1,1,1⟩+t⟨1,-1,5⟩)-⟨1,1,1⟩ \\\\  & =t\\,⟨1,-1,5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t,\\,-t,\\,5t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $PQ=3\\sqrt{3},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}PQ & =3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ |\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| & =3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ |⟨t,\\,-t,\\,5t⟩| & =3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{{t}^{2}+(-t)^{2}+(5t)^{2}} & =3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{27{t}^{2}} & =3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\pm 3\\sqrt{3}t & =3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ t & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the values $t=\\pm 1$ into the equation for the line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{O{Q}_{1}}=⟨1,1,1⟩+⟨1,-1,5⟩=⟨2,0,6⟩, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{O{Q}_{2}}=⟨1,1,1⟩-⟨1,-1,5⟩=⟨0,2,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points are ${Q}_{1}(2,0,6)$ and ${Q}_{2}(0,2,-4).$" 13194139533497 17592186045417 true] [17592186403878 1074 "![](images/7c/7cf17440c27765efb34b6a01c12a76ad4e980fc540307e2218227153661db5dd.png)\n\nIn $△OAC$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $OA=2.5|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{OA}$ and $a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{OA}=2.5a.$\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $AC=3|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $b$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}=3b.$\n\nAdditionally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\  & =2.5a+3b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+\\mu b=2.5a+3b.$ \n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients, we get:\n\n$\\,$ $\\lambda =2.5$ and $\\mu =3$"