mpute the moment about the $x$-axis: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\cdot xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2-y}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2-y}x\\,\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=2-y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}(2-y)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2{y}^{2}-2{y}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{4}+\\frac{1}{10}{y}^{5}]}_{y=0}^{y=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}-8+\\frac{16}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}=\\frac{(\\frac{8}{15})}{(\\frac{2}{3})}=\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$" 13194139534330 17592186045417 true] [17592186419232 1074 "The center of mass of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $m={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A$ is the mass of the lamina,\n\n- ${M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\,$ is the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- ${M}_{y}={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A$ is the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis.\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type II plane region:\n\n![](images/e0/e01f73436854c4d337f4fa1dfbb31645c99ccdccbd4e36ae5145305f8b7b4a1f.png)\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le -y,\\,-1\\le y\\le 0}\n$$\n\nand $\\lambda (x,y)=x-y.$ \n\nSince we require the $y$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $x$-axis: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\cdot (x-y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}xy-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{-y}xy-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{2}-x