 \\\\ -3x+y+z & =9 \\\\ z & =9+3x-y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $x=1$ and $y=1,$ we get \n\n$$\nz=9+3(1)-(1)=11.\n$$\n\nSo, the point $Q(1,1,11)$ lies on the plane. \n\nNow, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨1,1,11⟩-⟨-3,4,7⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,-3,4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the equation of a plane, we get a normal vector $n=⟨-3,1,1⟩.$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|4\\cdot (-3)+(-3)\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 1|}{\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-12-3+4|}{\\sqrt{9+1+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-11|}{\\sqrt{11}} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{\\sqrt{11}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534899 17592186045417 true] [17592186443266 1074 "Let's start by picking a point on the plane. To do that, we simply substitute some concrete values for $x$ and $y$ into the equation and solve for $z.$ Let's choose $x=2$ and $y=3$ (though it doesn't really matter what values we choose). Solving for $z,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y+2z & =-1 \\\\ 2(2)-(3)+2z & =-1 \\\\ 2z & =-2 \\\\ z & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the point $Q(2,3,-1)$ which lies on the plane.\n\nComputing $\\overset{\\to }{PQ},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨2,3,-1⟩-⟨-1,0,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,3,-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also need a normal vector to the plane. For any Cartesian equation of a plane $ax+by+cz=d,$ the normal vector is $n=⟨a,b,c⟩.$ So, for the plane $Π,$ we have $n=⟨2,-1,2⟩.$ \n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|3\\cdot 2+3\\cdot (-1)+(-3)\\cdot 2|}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|6-3-6|}{\\sqrt{4+1+4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\,-3\\,|}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534899 17592186045417 true] [17592186443267 1074 "Let's pick a point on the plane, for instance, $Q(3,0,1).$ Then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨3,0,1⟩-⟨1,1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,-1,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, from the equation of the plane, we get a normal vector $n=⟨1,-8,4⟩.$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|2\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot (-8)+2\\cdot 4|}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-8)^{2}+{4}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|2+8+8|}{\\sqrt{1+64+16}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|18|}{\\sqrt{81}} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534899 17592186045417 true] [17592186443268 1074 "First, we need to find a point on the plane. If $r=⟨x,y,z⟩,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,4,4⟩ & =8 \\\\ -2x+4y+4z & =8 \\\\ 4z & =8+2x-4y \\\\ z & =2+\\frac{x}{2}-y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $x=2$ and $y=1,$ we get \n\n$$\nz=2+\\frac{2}{2}-1=2.\n$$\n\nSo, the point $Q(2,1,2)$ lies on the plane. \n\nNow, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨2,1,2⟩-⟨-3,2,6⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5,-1,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the equation of a plane, we get a normal vector $n=⟨-2,4,4⟩.$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|5\\cdot (-2)+(-1)\\cdot 4+(-4)\\cdot 4|}{\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{4}^{2}+{4}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-10-4-16|}{\\sqrt{4+16+16}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-30|}{\\sqrt{36}} \\\\  & =\\frac{30}{6} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534899 17592186045417 true] [17592186443478 1074 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ Then, the sample variance ${S}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{S}^{2}=\\frac{1}{n-1}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}=\\frac{n}{n-1}[\\overset{―}{{X}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}].\n$$\n\nNote that:\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- the sample variance ${S}^{2}$ is an unbiased estimator of the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ and\n\n- ${\\overset{^}{\\sigma }}^{2}={s}^{2}$ denotes an unbiased estimate of ${\\sigma }^{2}$ that's computed from the sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}.$\n\nWe're given that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{51}{({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}=24.5$ and $n=51.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\overset{^}{\\sigma }}^{2} & ={s}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{51-1}\\cdot 24.5 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{50}\\cdot 24.5 \\\\  & =0.49.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, an unbiased estimate of the population variance is ${\\overset{^}{\\sigma }}^{2}=0.49.$ We take the square root to compute an unbiased estimate of the standard deviation. This gives\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\sigma }=\\sqrt{0.49}=0.7.\n$$" 13194139534902 17592186045417 true] [17592186443479 1074 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ Then, the sample variance ${S}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{S}^{2}=\\frac{1}{n-1}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{({X}_{i}-\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}=\\frac{n}{n-1}[\\overset{―}{{X}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{X})}^{2}].\n$$\n\nNote that:\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n