045417 true] [17592186447168 1074 "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nSince the population standard deviation is $\\sigma =\\sqrt{25}=5,$ we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe require that the standard error is less than $0.25.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{n}} & <0.25 \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\frac{5}{0.25} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >20 \\\\ n & >{20}^{2} \\\\ n & >400.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest sample size is $n=401.$" 13194139534993 17592186045417 true] [17592186447466 1074 "Two lines are perpendicular if they intersect and form a ${90}^{∘}$ angle.\n\nFirst, notice that we have two sets of perpendicular lines:\n\n- $\\overset{⟷}{PU}$ is perpendicular to $\\overset{⟷}{PQ}$\n\n- $\\overset{⟷}{RS}$ is perpendicular to $\\overset{⟷}{ST}$\n\n![](images/24/2492594432bf29b2753d646c53b66ffd6230c21db71c4dd12479c9811217dc91.png)\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the sides $\\overset{―}{UP}$ and $\\overset{―}{PQ}$ are perpendicular, and $\\overset{―}{RS}$ and $\\overset{―}{ST}$ are also perpendicular. We can write this as follows:\n\n$\\,$ $\\overset{―}{UP}⊥\\overset{―}{PQ}$ and $\\overset{―}{RS}⊥\\overset{―}{ST}$" 13194139534999 17592186045417 true] [17592186447469 1074 "Two lines are perpendicular if they intersect and form a ${90}^{∘}$ angle.\n\nFirst, notice that the lines $\\overset{⟷}{EA}$ and  $\\overset{⟷}{AB}$ are perpendicular.\n\n![](images/81/81e84f9f6f3f50f6a5ef3f39381ebce028ccb52fa09b2d7042c9206a7027f337.png)\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the sides $\\overset{―}{EA}$ and $\\overset{―}{AB}$ are perpendicular. We can write this as follows: \n