)-3(0)+6z & =16 \\\\ 6z & =12 \\\\ z & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the point $Q(-2,0,2)$ which lies on the plane.\n\nComputing $\\overset{\\to }{PQ},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨-2,0,2⟩-⟨5,3,7⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-7,-3,-5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also need a normal vector to the plane. For any Cartesian equation of a plane $ax+by+cz=d,$ the normal vector is $n=⟨a,b,c⟩.$ So, for the plane $Π,$ we have $n=⟨-2,-3,6⟩.$ \n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|(-7)\\cdot (-2)+(-3)\\cdot (-3)+(-5)\\cdot 6|}{\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-3)^{2}+(6)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|14+9-30|}{\\sqrt{4+9+36}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-7|}{\\sqrt{49}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{7} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535160 17592186045417 true] [17592186479879 1074 "Let $v=\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $w=\\overset{\\to }{AC}.$ Then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =b-a \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}w & =c-a \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $\\theta =m∠BAC$ be the angle between the vectors $v$ and $w.$ Then, we can find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{v\\cdot w}{‖v‖\\,‖w‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{{v}_{1}{w}_{1}+{v}_{2}{w}_{2}+{v}_{3}{w}_{3}+{v}_{4}{w}_{4}}{\\sqrt{{v}_{1}^{2}+{v}_{2}^{2}+{v}_{3}^{2}+{v}_{4}^{2}}\\,\\sqrt{{w}_{1}^{2}+{w}_{2}^{2}+{w}_{3}^{2}+{w}_{4}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0\\cdot (-4)+3\\cdot (-4)+0\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot 2}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\,\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+(-4)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-14}{\\sqrt{10}\\cdot \\sqrt{40}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{14}{20} \\\\  & =-\\frac{14÷2}{20÷2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535497 17592186045443 true] [17592186479886 1074 "First, let\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a=\\overset{\\to }{OA}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\overset{\\to }{OB}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAlso, let $\\theta =m∠AOB$ be the angle between the vectors $a$ and $b.$ Then, we can find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{‖a‖\\,‖b‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{1}{b}_{1}+{a}_{2}{b}_{2}+{a}_{3}{b}_{3}+{a}_{4}{b}_{4}}{\\sqrt{{a}_{1}^{2}+{a}_{2}^{2}+{a}_{3}^{2}+{a}_{4}^{2}}\\,\\sqrt{{b}_{1}^{2}+{b}_{2}^{2}+{b}_{3}^{2}+{b}_{4}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 0+(-1)\\cdot 3}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\,\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{\\sqrt{10}\\cdot \\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535497 17592186045443 true] [17592186482514 1074 "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}}^{x}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -26\\end{bmatrix}}^{b}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\hat{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\hat{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={(\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -26\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}11 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 18\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -26\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{198}\\begin{bmatrix}18 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 11\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -26\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{198}\\begin{bmatrix}18 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 11\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536958 17592186576411 true] [17592186482531 1074 "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{n}$ of rank $n$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{n}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{n-1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }…\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{n-1},{v}_{n-2},…,{v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ An *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nFirst, we compute $A-3I:$\n\n$$\nA-3I=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-3\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A-3I:$\n\n$$\n(A-3I)v=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A-3I:$\n\n$$\n(A-3I)w=\\begin{bmatrix}2