ight=\"20\" width=\"15\">$\\,2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$\\,⋮$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nWe could continue the division, but the same digit keeps repeating. Thus, the decimal continues with repeating $3$'s:\n\n$$\n7.833…\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{47}{6}=7.833…=7.8\\overset{―}{3}.\n$$" 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186489222 1074 "Let's examine each of our numbers in turn.\n\n- $1.353124…$ is not a rational number. The notation \"$…$\" tells us that digits after the decimal point extend forever. However, there are no repeating digits.\n\n- $1.5\\overset{―}{21}$ is a rational number. The notation $\\overset{―}{21}$ tells us that the digits $2$ and $1$ after the decimal point repeat indefinitely.\n\n$$\n1.5\\overset{―}{21}=1.52121212121…\n$$\n\nAny decimal with repeating digits can be represented as a ratio of two integers. In this case,\n\n$$\n1.5\\overset{―}{21}=\\frac{1\\,506}{990}.\n$$\n\n- $4.055$ is a rational number because it can be expressed as a ratio of two integers:\n\n$$\n4.055=\\frac{4\\,055}{1\\,000}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186489282 1074 "To write the fraction as a decimal, we divide the numerator by the denominator:\n\n$$\n35÷6\n$$\n\nWe proceed using long division as usual, continuing until a repeating pattern of digits appears after the decimal point:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"150\">\n<br/><br/><table align=\"left