\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$-$</td><td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$8$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$\\,0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$-\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$\\,8$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$\\,2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\">$\\,⋮$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"20\" width=\"15\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nWe could continue the division, but the same digit keeps repeating. Thus, the decimal continues with repeating $3$'s:\n\n$$\n5.833…\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{35}{6}=5.833…=5.8\\overset{―}{3}.\n$$" 13194139535272 17592186045417 true] [17592186491123 1074 "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the shirts in the population have sizes $8$ and $10,$ and these are distributed in the ratio $1:3.$ Therefore, to compute the population's mean and variance, we can consider a population consisting of $1+3=4$ shirts only with the following sizes:\n\n$$\n8,\\,10,\\,10,\\,10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(8+3\\cdot 10) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(8+30) \\\\  & =\\frac{38}{4} \\\\  & =9.5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}^{2}-{\\mu }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}({8}^{2}+3\\cdot {10}^{2})-{9.5}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(364)-90.25 \\\\  & =91-90.25 \\\\  & =0.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the population standard deviation is $\\sigma =\\sqrt{0.75},$ and we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\frac{0.75}{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe require that the standard error is less than $0.5.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{\\frac{0.75}{n}} & <0.5 \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\frac{\\sqrt{0.75}}{0.5} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\sqrt{\\frac{0.75}{{0.5}^{2}}} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\sqrt{\\frac{0.75}{0.25}} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\sqrt{3} \\\\ n & >3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest sample size is $n=4.$" 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186491146 1074 "Let's draw triangle $△ABH,$ which is a ${30}^{∘}$-${60}^{∘}$-${90}^{∘}$ triangle.\n\n![](images/7c/7cbfe29dd9087d07b6d2101e85b9154836704221320f4dd19346f49f3a858fa3.png)\n\n \n\nSince the longer leg, $\\overset{―}{AH},$ has a length of $40,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}x & =40 \\\\ x & =\\frac{40}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{40\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now calculate the length of the hypotenuse, $\\overset{―}{AB},$ as follows:\n\n$$\nAB=2x=2\\cdot \\frac{40\\sqrt{3}}{3}=\\frac{80\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $△ABC$ is an equilateral triangle, we get\n\n$$\nAC=AB=\\frac{80\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$" 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186491183 1074 "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the coins in the population have denominations $$1$ and $$0.50,$ and these are distributed in the ratio $1:4.$ Therefore, to compute the population's mean and variance, we can consider a population consisting of $1+4=5$ coins only with the following denominations:\n\n$$\n$1,\\,$0.5,\\,$0.5,\\,$0.5,\\,$0.5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(1+4\\cdot 0.5) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(1+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5} \\\\  & =$0.6,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}^{2}-{\\mu }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}({1}^{2}+4\\cdot {0.5}^{2})-{0.6}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(2)-0.36 \\\\  & =0.4-0.36 \\\\  & =0.04.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the population standard deviation is $\\sigma =\\sqrt{0.04}=0.2,$ and we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{0.2}{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe require that the standard error is less than $$0.02.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{0.2}{\\sqrt{n}} & <0.02 \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\frac{0.2}{0.02} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >10 \\\\ n & >{10}^{2} \\\\ n & >100.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest sample size is $n=101.$" 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186491194 1074 "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nSince the population variance is ${\\sigma }^{2}=4$ and the sample size is $n=50,$ we have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}] & =\\frac{4}{50} \\\\  & =0.08.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{0.08}≈0.28.\n$$" 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186491203 1074 "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nSince the population standard deviation is $\\sigma =\\sqrt{1}=1,$ we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe require that the standard error is less than $0.5.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}} & <0.5 \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >\\frac{1}{0.5} \\\\ \\sqrt{n} & >2 \\\\ n & >{2}^{2} \\\\ n & >4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest sample size is $n=5.$" 13194139535285 17592186045417 true] [17592186492050 1074 "The vector equation of the straight line passing through $H$ and parallel to $v$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=h+tv,\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $h$ is the position vector of $H.$ \n\nWe can find a direction vector by computing the displacement vector between the given points $H(1,-2,3)$ and $S(4,-2,1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{HS} \\\\  & =⟨4,-2,1⟩-⟨1,-2,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,0,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector equation of the line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535498 17592186045443 true] [17592186492072 1074 "First, we find the vector equation of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =⟨-4,\\,2,\\,-3⟩+t⟨1,\\,0,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nSince $X$ lies on the line, we know that there must be a real number $t$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OX} & =⟨-4,\\,2,\\,-3⟩+t⟨1,\\,0,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PX} & =\\overset{\\to }{OX}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =(⟨-4,\\,2,\\,-3⟩+t⟨1,\\,0,\\,2⟩)-⟨-4,\\,2,\\,-3⟩ \\\\  & =t⟨1,\\,0,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t,\\,0,\\,2t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $PX=\\sqrt{20},$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XP & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\ |\\overset{\\to }{PX}| & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\ |⟨t,\\,0,\\,2t⟩| & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\ \\sqrt{{t}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(2t)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\ \\sqrt{5{t}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\ \\pm \\sqrt{5}t & =2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ t & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the values $t=\\pm 2$ into the equation for the line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{O{X}_{1}}=⟨-4,\\,2,\\,-3⟩+2⟨1,\\,0,\\,2⟩=⟨-2,2,1⟩, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{O{X}_{2}}=⟨-4,\\,2,\\,-3⟩-2⟨1,\\,0,\\,2⟩=⟨-6,2,-7⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points are ${X}_{1}(-2,2,1)$ and ${X}_{2}(-6,2,-7).$" 13194139535291 17592186045417 true] [17592186492822 1074 "By the converse of the perpendicular bisector theorem, a point equidistant from the endpoints of a segment lies on the perpendicular bisector of the segment. \n\nHere, the point $R$ is equidistant from the endpoints of $\\overset{―}{PQ}.$ Therefore, $\\overset{⟷}{SR}$ is the perpendicular bisector of $\\overset{―}{PQ}.$ \n\nIn particular, we have that $SP=SQ.$ We can use this equation so solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}SP & =SQ \\\\ 2x+5 & =6x-3 \\\\ 8 & =4x \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186492825 1074 "First, let\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a=\\overset{\\to }{OA}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\overset{\\to }{OB}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, let $\\theta =m∠AOB$ be the angle between the vectors $a$ and $b.$ Then, we can find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{‖a‖\\,‖b‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{1}{b}_{1}+{a}_{2}{b}_{2}+{a}_{3}{b}_{3}+{a}_{4}{b}_{4}}{\\sqrt{{a}_{1}^{2}+{a}_{2}^{2}+{a}_{3}^{2}+{a}_{4}^{2}}\\,\\sqrt{{b}_{1}^{2}+{b}_{2}^{2}+{b}_{3}^{2}+{b}_{4}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot (-1)+0\\cdot 4+1\\cdot 0+0\\cdot (-1)}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}\\,\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{4}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{18}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{\\sqrt{36}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535498 17592186045443 true] [17592186492826 1074 "We have the following diagram.\n\n![](images/29/29610a9e6bc2d25522c151c1676d438a860503e640bf9f7beb178ab73b3b6be7.png)\n\nSince $S$ is the midpoint of $\\overset{―}{PQ}$, the two segments $\\overset{―}{PS}$ and $\\overset{―}{SQ}$ have the same length. So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}PS & =SQ \\\\ 2x+5 & =x+9 \\\\ 2x+5-x & =x+9-x \\\\ x+5 & =9 \\\\ x+5-5 & =9-5 \\\\ x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=4\\,\\text{in}.$" 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186492838 1074 "Since $M$ is the midpoint of $\\overset{―}{AB},$ the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is twice the length of the segment $\\overset{―}{AM}.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2AM & =AB \\\\ 2(3x-2) & =4x+6 \\\\ 6x-4 & =4x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we subtract $4x$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x-4-4x & =4x+6-4x \\\\ 2x-4 & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we add $4$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-4+4 & =6+4 \\\\ 2x & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide both sides by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x}{2} & =\\frac{10}{2} \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186492846 1074 "By the converse of the perpendicular bisector theorem, a point equidistant from the endpoints of a segment lies on the perpendicular bisector of the segment. \n\nHere, the points $P$ and $N$ are both equidistant from the endpoints of $\\overset{―}{KL}.$ Therefore, $P$ and $N$ both  lie on the perpendicular bisector of $\\overset{―}{KL}.$ \n\nSince only one line can be drawn through two given points, the line $\\overset{⟷}{PN}$ is the perpendicular bisector of $\\overset{―}{KL}.$ \n\nIn particular, we have that $KM=LM.$ We can use this equation to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}KM & =LM \\\\ 8x-6 & =7x+1 \\\\ x & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186492853 1074 "A bisector of $\\overset{―}{KL}$:\n\n- must split $\\overset{―}{KL}$ in half.\n\nAmong the given options, only diagrams II and IV show a bisector to the line segment $\\overset{―}{KL}.$ \n\nNotice that in diagrams I and III the intersection of the two segments does not split $\\overset{―}{KL}$ in half." 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186492875 1074 "To determine the amount of orange essence that Catherine must mix with $0.6$ liters of alcohol, we must multiply $0.6$ by $0.2.$\n\nWe proceed by multiplying the two numbers just as we would with whole numbers.\n\nNotice that the second number has a leading $0$, which we ignore during the multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,.6\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,.2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere is $1$ decimal place in $0.6$ and there is $1$ decimal place in $0.2.$\nTherefore, their product will have $1+1=2$ decimal places.\n\nSo, we take our value of $12$ and add a decimal point to make a number with $2$ decimal places.\n\n$$\n\\,\\overset{↓}{.}\\,\\,\\,\\underbrace{12}_{2\\text{ digits}}\n$$\n\nHence, $0.6\\times 0.2=0.12.$\n\nWe conclude that Catherine must use $0.12$ liters of the essence with $0.6$ liters of alcohol." 13194139536958 17592186576411 true] [17592186492876 1074 "A bisector of $\\overset{―}{CD}$:\n\n- must split $\\overset{―}{CD}$ in half.\n\nAmong the given options, only diagrams I, II, and III show a bisector to the line segment $\\overset{―}{CD}.$ \n\nNotice that in diagram IV the intersection of the two segments does not split $\\overset{―}{CD}$ in half." 13194139535300 17592186045417 true] [17592186492904 1074 "Since $M$ is the midpoint of $\\overset{―}{AB}$, the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ is twice the length of the segment $\\overset{―}{AM}.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2AM & =AB \\\\ 2(2x+1) & =5x-2 \\\\ 4x+2 & =5x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we subtract $4x$ from both sides of the equation:\n