-6{x}^{2} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{3(-2{x}^{3})}{x} \\\\ -6{x}^{2} & =-6{x}^{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y=-2{x}^{3}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535315 17592186045443 true] [17592186494079 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in an interval where the function is defined and the equation is valid.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)=-3{x}^{4},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(-3{x}^{4})}^{′} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 4{x}^{3} \\\\  & =-12{x}^{3} \\\\  & \\ne -7{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y=-3{x}^{4}$ and ${y}^{′}=-12{x}^{3}$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4y}{x} \\\\ -12{x}^{3} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4(-3{x}^{4})}{x} \\\\ -12{x}^{3} & =-12{x}^{3}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y=-3{x}^{4}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535315 17592186045443 true] [17592186494086 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in an interval where the function is defined and the equation is valid.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)=-4{x}^{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(-4{x}^{2})}^{′} \\\\  & =-4\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =-8x \\\\  & \\ne -6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y=-4{x}^{2}$ and ${y}^{′}=-8x$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2y}{x} \\\\ -8x & \\overset