 is $n=350,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.7.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=350(0.7)=245>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=350(1-0.7)=105>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=350$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.7,\\frac{0.7(1-0.7)}{350}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.7,0.0006).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the probability we wish to find. If fewer than $224$ apricots in the sample weigh more than $40$ g, then \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}<\\frac{224}{350}=0.64.\n$$\n\nHence, the probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.64).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.64) & =P(Z<\\frac{0.64-0.7}{\\sqrt{0.0006}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<-2.45) \\\\  & =Φ(-2.45).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535388 17592186045417 true] [17592186503697 1074 "![](images/70/7045c84435a9ed2934569aceabf9d01e01d4278de6b89329664b654dc53b5250.png)\n\nTwo angles are supplementary if their measures add up to ${180}^{∘},$ a straight angle.\n\nSince $\\overset{―}{CE}$ is a segment, we have that $∠COD$ and $∠DOE$ are supplementary. \n\n$$\nm∠COD+m∠DOE={180}^{∘}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the measure of $∠COD$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠COD & ={180}^{∘}-m∠DOE \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{144}^{∘} \\\\  & ={36}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503702 1074 "Two angles are supplementary if their measures add up to ${180}^{∘},$ a straight angle.\n\nSince $\\overset{―}{AD}$ is a segment, we have that $∠BOA$ and $∠BOD$ are supplementary. \n\n$$\nm∠BOA+m∠BOD={180}^{∘}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the measure of $∠BOD$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠BOD & ={180}^{∘}-m∠BOA \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{132}^{∘} \\\\  & ={48}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535437 17592186045417 true] [17592186503724 1074 "![](ima