}$ is a circular *segment*.\n\nTherefore, the correct mathematical statement is the following:\n\n\"*If $A$ and $B$ are two points on a circle and $O$ is the circle's center, then $\\overset{―}{AB},$ which passes through $O,$ is **a diameter** of the circle. Additionally, if we consider the region bounded by $\\overset{―}{AB}$ and the corresponding arc $\\overset{⌢}{AB},$ then we obtain a circular **segment**.*\"" 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186504675 1074 "We can see that $\\overset{―}{JK}$ is a line segment whose endpoints lie on the circle. So, $\\overset{―}{JK}$ is a chord. It is not a diameter since the line segment does not pass through the center of the circle." 13194139535445 17592186045417 true] [17592186505731 1074 "First, from the diagram, notice that $∠BCA$ and $∠BCD$ are supplementary. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠BCA+m∠BCD & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠BCA+y & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠BCA & ={180}^{∘}-y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $△ABC$ is an isosceles triangle where $\\overset{―}{AC}≅\\overset{―}{BC}$, the base angles must be equal:\n\n$$\nm∠B=m∠A={56}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since the interior angles of any triangle sum to ${180}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠BCA & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {56}^{∘}+{56}^{∘}+({180}^{∘}-y) & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ y & ={112}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535455 17592186045417 true] [17592186505736 1074 "First, let's interpret the data:\n\n- the population consists of all U.S. households,\n\n- some of the households surveyed own at least one pet, and some don't,\n\n- the size of the random sample is $n=1\\,200,$ and\n\n- the proportion of households in the sample that own at least one pet can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{p\\,}=\\frac{816}{1\\,200}=0.68\n$$\n\nNow, we have that\n\n- $n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=1\\,200\\cdot 0.68=816>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})=1\\,200\\cdot (1-0.68)=384>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation: \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\ove