17 true] [17592186512871 1074 "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=96,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.72.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=96(0.72)=69.12>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=96(1-0.72)=26.88>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=96$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.72,\\frac{0.72(1-0.72)}{96}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.72,0.0021).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(0.7<\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.8).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.7<\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.8) & =P(\\frac{0.7-0.72}{\\sqrt{0.0021}}<Z\\le \\frac{0.8-0.72}{\\sqrt{0.0021}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-0.44<Z\\le 1.75) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1.75)-P(Z\\le -0.44) \\\\  & =Φ(1.75)-Φ(-0.44)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(1.75) & =0.9599 \\\\ Φ(-0.44) & =0.3300.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.7<\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.8) & =Φ(1.75)-Φ(-0.44) \\\\  & =0.9599-0.3300 \\\\  & =0.6299.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535532 17592186045417 true] [17592186512885 1074 "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ the sum of squared deviations from the mean for the data ${y}_{i}$ can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{yy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y})^{2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i}{y}_{i}^{2}-\\frac{1}{n}{({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSu