r instance, $Q(5,-1,6).$ Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨5,-1,6⟩-⟨7,-2,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,1,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, from the equation of the plane, we get a normal vector $n=⟨-6,3,2⟩.$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|(-2)\\cdot (-6)+1\\cdot 3+3\\cdot 2|}{\\sqrt{(-6)^{2}+{3}^{2}+{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|12+3+6|}{\\sqrt{36+9+4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|21|}{\\sqrt{49}} \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{7} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186516812 1074 "The similarity ratio is the ratio of the corresponding sides.\n\nSince $△ABC∼△VTU,$ the side $\\overset{―}{BC}$ corresponds to the side $\\overset{―}{TU}$\n\nTherefore, the similarity ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =\\frac{BC}{TU} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186516828 1074 "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=80,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.7.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=80(0.7)=56>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=80(1-0.7)=24>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=80$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.7,\\frac{0.7(1-0.7)}{80}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.7,0.002\\,625).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(0.6\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.8).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.6\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.8) & =P(\\frac{0.6-0.7}{\\sqrt{0.002\\,625}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{0.8-0.7}{\\sqrt{0.002\\,625}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-1.95\\le Z\\le 1.95) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1.95)-P(Z\\le -1.95) \\\\  & =Φ(1.95)-Φ(-1.95)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186516846 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s$ along any closed curve $C\\in D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}\\in D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x+2y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-8x-4y))=4-4=0,\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\nSince $F$ is source-free, we know the flux of $F$ is independent of the path. In particular, as we have that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{2}$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B,$ and ${n}_{2}$ is the unit normal to ${C}_{2},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/36/363ebd04d2629ed2b1b29ccab326e22737cf42e4c026d62910a50a1dd88e80f2.png)\n\nNotice that both ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotations of their respective unit tangent vectors.\n\nTo calculate the flux of $F$ across ${C}_{2},$ we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply the formula,\n\n- the curve should be parametrized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector ${n}_{2}$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit normal tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nWe have the parametrization\n\n$$\nr(t)=(2-3t)\\,i+(1-2t)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =4x+2y=4(2-3t)+2(1-2t)=-16t+10, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =-8x-4y=-8(2-3t)-4(1-2t)=32t-20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-3t)=-3, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-2t)=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the flux integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-16t+10)\\cdot (-2)-(32t-20)\\cdot (-3)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(32t-20)+(96t-60)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}128t-80\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[64{t}^{2}-80t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =64-80 \\\\  & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s=-16.\n$$" 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186516848 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s$ along any closed curve $C\\in D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}\\in D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nFirst, notice th