 true] [17592186533975 1074 "We want $\\overset{^}{y}$ to be an *input value* $y$ where the weight $w(y)$ is as large as possible. \n\nThat is exactly what $\\operatorname{arg}⁡max$ returns.\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{y}=\\operatorname{arg}⁡{max}_{y\\in \\{-7,-5,-3,-1\\}}w(y).\n$$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186534096 1074 "We want $\\overset{^}{y}$ to be an *input value* $y$ where the profit $g(y)$ is as large as possible. \n\nThat is exactly what $\\operatorname{arg}⁡max$ returns.\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{y}=\\operatorname{arg}⁡{max}_{y\\in \\{3,7,11,15\\}}g(y).\n$$" 13194139535861 17592186045417 true] [17592186535030 1074 "From the figure, we note that the points $D$ and $E$ have coordinates $\\underbrace{(-4,0)}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{1})}$ and $\\underbrace{(0,3)}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{2})},$  respectively.\n\nTo find the length of $\\overset{―}{DE},$ we use the distance formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}DE & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(0-(-4))^{2}+(3-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(4)^{2}+(3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536958 17592186576411 true] [17592186535948 1074 "The center of a dilation lies on any line which passes through a point and its image.\n\nTherefore, we can find the center of our dilation as, for example, the point of intersection of the lines $\\overset{⟷}{B{B}^{′}}$ and $\\overset{⟷}{C{C}^{′}},$ as shown below.  \n\n![](images/d7/d7215165822a95ce9689e3666ff6ea9454048a936c372b07a6224d199b8d0ed7.png)\n\nFrom the diagram above, where we have that \n\n$$\n\\frac{{B}^{′}{C}^{′}}{BC}=\\frac{6}{2}=3,\n$$\n\nand the same proportion holds for the other corresponding sides too. As a result, the absolute value of the scale factor is \n\n$$\n|k|=3.\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, notice that our figures have different orientations, which means that the scale factor of the dilation is negative.\n\nTherefore, the scale factor is $k=-3.$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535993 1074 "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nFirst, we need to compute the means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (2+3+3+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =3 \\\\ \\overset{―}{y} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (28+20+24+16) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 88 \\\\  & =22\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(2\\,-\\,3)(28\\,-\\,22)+(3\\,-\\,3)(20\\,-\\,22)+(3\\,-\\,3)(24\\,-\\,22)+(4\\,-\\,3)(16\\,-\\,22)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(-1)(6)+(0)(-2)+(0)(2)+(1)(-6)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[-6+0+0+(-6)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (-12) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186535999 1074 "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(2-10)(-2-(-1))+(14-10)(-1-(-1)) \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,+(18-10)(-3-(-1))+(6-10)(2-(-1))] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(-8)(-1)+(4)(0)+(8)(-2)+(-4)(3)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[8+0-16-12] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (-20) \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535876 17592186045417 true] [17592186536825 1074 "Two sides are consecutive if they have a vertex in common. Therefore, $\\overset{―}{AF}$ and $\\overset{―}{EH}$ are consecutive sides to $\\overset{―}{FH}.$" 13194139535886 17592186045417 true] [17592186536828 1074 "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ ${S}_{xy}$ is the sum of products of the deviations of ${x}_{i}$ and ${y}_{i}$ from their respective means, that is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the sample means $\\overset{―}{x}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{5+8+9+5+6}{5}=6.6\n$$\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{8+9+10+7+9}{5}=8.6\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the differences between the ${x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{x}$, ${y}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}$, and their product.\n\n| **$\\,{x}_{i}\\,$** | $5$ | $8$ | $9$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **$\\,{y}_{i}\\,$** | $8$ | $9$ | $10$ | $7$ | $9$ |\n| **$\\,({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})\\,$** | $-1.6$ | $1.4$ | $2.4$ | $-1.6$ | $-0.6$ |\n| **$\\,({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y})\\,$** | $-0.6$ | $0.4$ | $1.4$ | $-1.6$ | $0.4$ |\n| **$\\,({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y})\\,$** | $0.96$ | $0.56$ | $3.36$ | $2.56$ | $-0.24$ |\n\nTherefore, ${S}_{xy}$ is\n\n$$\n{S}_{xy}=0.96+0.56+3.36+2.56+(-0.24)=7.2.\n$$" 13194139535886 17592186045417 true] [17592186536834 1074 "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. A square is a rectangle where all sides are congruent, hence $AB=BC=CD=AD.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The angles in a square are all right. Therefore, $m∠ABC={90}^{∘}=m∠ADC.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Opposite sides of a square are parallel, and $\\overset{―}{AD}$ is opposite $\\overset{―}{BC},$ therefore $\\overset{―}{AD}$ is parallel to $\\overset{―}{BC}.$\n\nIn conclusion, statements I, II, and III are true." 13194139535886 17592186045417 true] [17592186536838 1074 "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ the sum of squared deviations from the mean for the data ${x}_{i}$ can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}-\\frac{1}{n}{({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given statistics in the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & =12\\,050-\\frac{1}{27}(540)^{2} \\\\  & =12\\,050-10\\,800 \\\\  & =1\\,250.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535886 17592186045417 true] [17592186536892 1074 "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ ${S}_{xy}$ is the sum of products of the deviations of ${x}_{i}$ and ${y}_{i}$ from their respective means, that is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the sample means $\\overset{―}{x}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1.2+1.0+0.7+1.3+1.8+1.4+1.7}{7}=1.3\n$$\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{6.6+6.1+5.7+6.0+6.3+5.9+5.4}{7}=6\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the differences between the ${x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{x}$, ${y}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}$, and their product.\n\n| **$\\,{x}_{i}\\,$** | $1.2$ | $1.0$ | $0.7$ | $1.3$ | $1.8$ | $1.7$ | $1.4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **$\\,{y}_{i}\\,$** | $6.6$ | $6.1$ | $5.7$ | $6.0$ | $6.3$ | $5.9$ | $5.4$ |\n| **$\\,({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})\\,$** | $-0.1$ | $-0.3$ | $-0.6$ | $0$ | $0.5$ | $0.4$ | $0.1$ |\n| **$\\,({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y})\\,$** | $0.6$ | $0.1$ | $-0.3$ | $0$ | $0.3$ | $-0.1$ | $-0.6$ |\n| **$\\,({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y})\\,$** | $-0.06$ | $-0.03$ | $0.18$ | $0$ | $0.15$ | $-0.04$ | $-0.06$ |\n\nTherefore, ${S}_{xy}$ is\n\n$$\n{S}_{xy}=(-0.06)+(-0.03)+0.18+0+0.15+(-0.04)+(-0.06)=0.14.\n$$" 13194139535886 17592186045417 true] [17592186538932 1074 "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7x-4) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x+2) \\\\ 7x-4 & =5x+2 \\\\ 2x & =6 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=3$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7(3)-4) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5(3)+2) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(17) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(17)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=3$ is a valid solution." 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538942 1074 "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-2) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-x+4) \\\\ {x}^{2}-2 & =-x+4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+x-6 & =0 \\\\ (x+3)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-3,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation:\n\n- Substituting $x=-3$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-2) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-x+4) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡((-3)^{2}-2) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-(-3)+4) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(7) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=-3$ is a valid solution.\n\n- Substituting $x=2$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-2) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-x+4) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡((2)^{2}-2) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(-(2)+4) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=2$ is a valid solution.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=-3,2.$" 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538951 1074 "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x-1) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(6x+9) \\\\ 2x-1 & =6x+9 \\\\ 4x & =-10 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=-\\frac{5}{2}$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(2(-\\frac{5}{2})-1) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}⁡(6(-\\frac{5}{2})+9) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡(-6) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}⁡(-6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\operatorname{log}⁡(-6)$ is not a real number.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the equation has no real solutions." 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186538984 1074 "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{12}⁡({v}^{2}+35) & ={log}_{12}⁡(-12v-1) \\\\ {v}^{2}+35 & =-12v-1 \\\\ {v}^{2}+12v+36 & =0 \\\\ (v+6)^{2} & =0 \\\\ v & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation:\n\n- Substituting $v=-6$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{12}⁡({v}^{2}+35) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{12}⁡(-12v-1) \\\\ {log}_{12}⁡((-6)^{2}+35) & \\overset{?}{=}{log}_{12}⁡(-12(-6)-1) \\\\ {log}_{12}⁡(71) & ={log}_{12}⁡(71).\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $v=-6$ is a valid solution." 13194139535898 17592186045417 true] [17592186539000 1074 "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{8}⁡({x}^{2}+19x) & ={log}_{8}⁡(10x-8) \\\\ {x}^{2}+19x & =10x-8 \\\\ {x}^{2}+9x+8 & =0 \\\\ (x+1)(x+