.92,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Since $|\\rho |>0.7,$ the correlation is strong. Also, since $\\rho $ is positive, the correlation is positive.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186546993 1074 "Recall the definitions of ${S}_{xx},$ ${S}_{xy},$ and ${S}_{yy}$ for the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}:$\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2},\\,{S}_{xy}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\\,{S}_{yy}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y})^{2}\n$$\n\nThe linear correlation coefficient $\\rho $ of the data sets $x$ and $y$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\rho (x,y)=\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}}=\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{\\sqrt{{S}_{xx}\\cdot {S}_{yy}}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{(-39)}{\\sqrt{41}\\cdot \\sqrt{61}} \\\\  & ≈-\\frac{39}{6.403\\,124\\cdot 7.810\\,250} \\\\  & ≈-0.78\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186546998 1074 "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(4x) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(5x+9) \\\\ 4x & =5x+9 \\\\ x & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=-9$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(4x) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}⁡(5x+9) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡(4(-9)) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}⁡(5(-9)+9) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡(-36) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}⁡(-36)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\operatorname{log}⁡(-36)$ is not a real number.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the equation has no real solutions." 13194139535947 1759218604541