set{―}{y}) \\\\  & =(5-5)(-1-2)+(8-5)(2-2)+(2-5)(5-2) \\\\  & =(0)(-3)+(3)(0)+(-3)(3) \\\\  & =0+0-9 \\\\  & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSecond, we compute the sums in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =(5-5)^{2}+(8-5)^{2}+(2-5)^{2} \\\\  & =0+9+9 \\\\  & =18 \\\\ {S}_{yy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y})^{2} \\\\  & =(-1-2)^{2}+(2-2)^{2}+(5-2)^{2} \\\\  & =9+0+9 \\\\  & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{(-9)}{\\sqrt{18}\\cdot \\sqrt{18}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{-9}{3\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186547041 1074 "The linear correlation coefficient $\\rho $ of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\rho (x,y)=\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}}=\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{\\sqrt{{S}_{xx}\\cdot {S}_{yy}}}.\n$$\n\nIn addition, we have the following rule of thumb:\n\n- if $|\\rho |>0.7,$ the correlation is *strong*\n\n- if $0.3\\le |\\rho |\\le 0.7,$ the correlation is *weak*\n\n- if $|\\rho |<0.3,$ then the correlation is *negligible*\n\nLet's now examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. To calculate $\\rho ,$ we first compute the sum in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =(10-15)(3-4)+(15-15)(6-4) \\\\  & +(12-15)(5-4)+(23-15)(2-4) \\\\  & =(-5)(-1)+(0)(2)+(-3)(1)+(8)(-2) \\\\  & =5+0-3-16 \\\\  & =-14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSecond, we compute the sums in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =(10-15)^{2}+(15-15)^{2}+(12-15)^{2}+(23-15)^{2} \\\\  & =25+0+9+64 \\\\  & =98 \\\\ {S}_{yy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y})^{2} \\\\  & =(3-4)^{2}+(6-4)^{2}+(5-4)^{2}+(2-4)^{2} \\\\  & =1+4+1+4 \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{-14}{\\sqrt{98}\\cdot \\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & ≈-\\frac{14}{9.899\\,495\\cdot 3.162\\,278} \\\\  & ≈-0.45,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n- Statement II is false while statement III is true. Since $0.3\\le |\\rho |\\le 0.7,$ the correlation is weak. Also, since $\\rho $ is negative, the correlation is negative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186547065 1074 "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(3x-5) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(7-x) \\\\ 3x-5 & =7-x \\\\ 4x & =12 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=3$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(3(3)-5) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}⁡(7-3) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡(4) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(4)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=3$ is a valid solution." 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186547068 1074 "Recall the definitions of ${S}_{xx},$ ${S}_{xy},$ and ${S}_{yy}$ for the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}:$\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2},\\,{S}_{xy}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\\,{S}_{yy}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y})^{2}\n$$\n\nThe linear correlation coefficient $\\rho $ of the data sets $x$ and $y$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\rho (x,y)=\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}}=\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{\\sqrt{{S}_{xx}\\cdot {S}_{yy}}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{186}\\cdot \\sqrt{96}} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{2}{13.638\\,182\\cdot 9.797\\,959} \\\\  & ≈0.01\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186547078 1074 "The linear correlation coefficient $\\rho $ of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\rho (x,y)=\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}}=\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{\\sqrt{{S}_{xx}\\cdot {S}_{yy}}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the sum in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =(13-9)(0-2)+(4-9)(-3-2)+(10-9)(9-2) \\\\  & =(4)(-2)+(-5)(-5)+(1)(7) \\\\  & =-8+25+7 \\\\  & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSecond, we compute the sums in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =(13-9)^{2}+(4-9)^{2}+(10-9)^{2} \\\\  & =16+25+1 \\\\  & =42 \\\\ {S}_{yy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y})^{2} \\\\  & =(0-2)^{2}+(-3-2)^{2}+(9-2)^{2} \\\\  & =4+25+49 \\\\  & =78\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{24}{\\sqrt{42}\\cdot \\sqrt{78}} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{24}{6.480\\,741\\cdot 8.831\\,761} \\\\  & ≈0.42\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." 13194139535947 17592186045417 true] [17592186547981 1074 "To solve the equation $\\sqrt{-3x-8}=x+2,$ we raise both sides to the power of $2.$  This allows us to get rid of the radical, and gives us a quadratic equation that is easier to solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{-3x-8} & =x+2 \\\\ {(\\sqrt{-3x-8})}^{2} & =(x+2)^{2} \\\\ -3x-8 & ={x}^{2}+4x+4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+7x+12 & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+4x)+(3x+12) & =0 \\\\ x(x+4)+3(x+4) & =0 \\\\ (x+3)(x+4) & =0 \\\\ x & =-4,\\,-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember that in square root equations, we should always check the obtained value(s) to ensure that they are actually solutions of the original equation.\n\n- First, we check whether the obtained value $x=-4$ is a solution of the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{-3(-4)-8} & \\overset{?}{=}-4+2 \\\\ \\sqrt{4} & \\overset{?}{=}-2 \\\\ 2 & \\ne -2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $x=-4$ does *not* satisfy the original equation, so we discard it.\n\n- Second, we check whether the obtained value $x=-3$ is a solution of the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{-3(-3)-8} & \\overset{?}{=}-3+2 \\\\ \\sqrt{1} & \\overset{?}{=}-1 \\\\ 1 & \\ne -1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $x=-3$ does *not* satisfy the original equation, so we discard it.\n\nTherefore, there is no solution to $\\sqrt{-3x-8}=x+2x.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548068 1074 "To solve the equation $\\sqrt{5x-9}=x-1,$ we raise both sides to the power of $2.$  This allows us to get rid of the radical, and gives us a quadratic equation that is easier to solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5x-9} & =x-1 \\\\ {(\\sqrt{5x-9})}^{2} & =(x-1)^{2} \\\\ 5x-9 & ={x}^{2}-2x+1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-7x+10 & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2x)+(-5x+10) & =0 \\\\ x(x-2)-5(x-2) & =0 \\\\ (x-5)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =2,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember that in square root equations, we should always check the obtained value(s) to ensure that they are actually solutions of the original equation.\n\n- First, we check whether the obtained value $x=2$ is a solution of the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5(2)-9} & \\overset{?}{=}2-1 \\\\ \\sqrt{1} & \\overset{?}{=}1 \\\\ 1 & =1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIndeed, the value $x=2$ does satisfy the original equation.\n\n- Second, we check whether the obtained value $x=5$ is a solution of the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5(5)-9} & \\overset{?}{=}5-1 \\\\ \\sqrt{16} & \\overset{?}{=}4 \\\\ 4 & =4\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIndeed, the value $x=5$ does satisfy the original equation.\n\nTherefore, the solutions of $\\sqrt{5x-9}=x-1$  are $x=2,\\,5.$" 13194139535953 17592186045417 true] [17592186548195 1074 "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{′}(a,b)=∇f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector and the corresponding unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨4,1⟩-⟨2,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ u & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{‖\\overset{\\to }{AB}‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(2)^{2}+(1)^{2}}}⟨2,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the gradient $∇f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $A(2,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4 x^{2} y + 2 x e^{y}),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4 x^{2} y + 2 x e^{y})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8 x y + 2 e^{y},2 x \\left(2 x + e^{y}\\right)⟩ \\\\ ∇f(2,0) & =⟨8 (2) (0) + 2 e^{(0)},4 (2)^{2} + 2 (2) e^{(0)}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,20⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{′}(2,0) & =∇f(2,0)\\cdot u \\\\  & =⟨2,20⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{2 \\sqrt{5}}{5},\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{24 \\sqrt{5}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535954 17592186045443 true] [17592186549015 1074 "By the converse of the perpendicular bisector theorem, a point equidistant from the endpoints of a segment lies on the perpendicular bisector of the segment.\n\nHere, the points $Y$ and $Z$ are both equidistant from the endpoints of $\\overset{―}{VW}.$ Therefore, $Y$ and $Z$ both  lie on the perpendicular bisector of $\\overset{―}{VW}.$    \n\nSince only one line can be drawn through two given points, the line $\\overset{⟷}{YZ}$ is the perpendicular bisector of $\\overset{―}{VW}.$ \n\nIn particular, we have that $VX=WX.$ We can use this equation to solve for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}VX & =WX \\\\ 4x-2 & =2x+4 \\\\ 2x & =6 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186549029 1074 "By the converse of the perpendicular bisector theorem, a point equidistant from the endpoints of a segment lies on the perpendicular bisector of the segment. \n\nHere, the points $P$ and $N$ are both equidistant from the endpoints of $\\overset{―}{KL}.$ Therefore, $P$ and $N$ both  lie on the perpendicular bisector of $\\overset{―}{KL}.$ \n\nSince only one line can be drawn through two given points, the line $\\overset{⟷}{PN}$ is the perpendicular bisector of $\\overset{―}{KL}.$ \n\nIn particular, we have that $KM=LM.$ We can use this equation to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}KM & =LM \\\\ 2x+2 & =4x-6 \\\\ -2x & =-8 \\\\ x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186549051 1074 "By the converse of the perpendicular bisector theorem, a point equidistant from the endpoints of a segment lies on the perpendicular bisector of the segment. \n\nHere, the points $P$ and $N$ are both equidistant from the endpoints of $\\overset{―}{KL}.$ Therefore, $P$ and $N$ both  lie on the perpendicular bisector of $\\overset{―}{KL}.$ \n\nSince only one line can be drawn through two given points, the line $\\overset{⟷}{PN}$ is the perpendicular bisector of $\\overset{―}{KL}.$ \n\nIn particular, we have that $KM=LM.$ We can use this equation to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}KM & =LM \\\\ 9x & =6x+9 \\\\ 3x & =9 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535959 17592186045417 true] [17592186550012 1074 "First, let's find the missing angle $T.$ Since the interior angles of a triangle sum to ${180}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠R+m∠S+m∠T & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {40}^{∘}+{103}^{∘}+m∠T & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠T & ={180}^{∘}-{143}^{∘} \\\\ m∠T & ={37}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $∠T$ is the smallest angle, its opposite side $\\overset{―}{RS}$ is the shortest side of the triangle.\n\nSince $∠S$ is the largest angle, its opposite side $\\overset{―}{RT}$ must be the longest side of the triangle. \n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nRS<ST<RT\n$$\n\nand therefore, the required order is\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{RS},\\overset{―}{ST},\\overset{―}{RT}.\n$$" 13194139535966 17592186045417 true] [17592186551239 1074 "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]}^{X}\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}^{\\beta } & =\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}8.6 \\\\ 13.1 \\\\ 24.6 \\\\ 28.1 \\\\ 38.6\\end{aligned}]}^{y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}11 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}113 \\\\ 414\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.1 \\\\ 7.5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=0.1+7.5x.$\n\nFinally, to predict the distance the object will cover after $6$ seconds, we substitute $x=6$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.1+7.5(6) \\\\  & =45.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, according to the model, the object will cover a distance of $45.1$ inches in $6$ seconds.  \n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=6$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable." 13194139536958 17592186576411 true] [17592186551244 1074 "| $x$ | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $15$ | $19$ | $14$ | $-9$ | $-11$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the curve \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}^{3}