a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(b)-f(a) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (b-a) \\\\ 4-(-3) & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c),{f}_{z}(c)⟩\\cdot (⟨3,-2,-5⟩-⟨-1,-2,4⟩) \\\\ 7 & =⟨{f}_{x}(c),{f}_{y}(c),{f}_{z}(c)⟩\\cdot ⟨4,0,-9⟩ \\\\ 7 & =4{f}_{x}(c)-9{f}_{z}(c).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the complete sentence is as follows:\n\n*The mean value theorem states that there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that $4{f}_{x}(c)-9{f}_{z}(c)=7.$ *" 13194139536086 17592186045417 true] [17592186566235 1074 "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nFirst, we need to compute the means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (4+4+(-4)+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ \\overset{―}{y} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot ((-1)+(-2)+7+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(4-2)(-1-2)+(4-2)(-2-2)+(-4-2)(7-2)+(4-2)(4-2)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(2)(-3)+(2)(-4)+(-6)(5)+(2)(2)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[-6-8-30+4] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (-40) \\\\  & =-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186566273 1074 "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nFirst, we find the mass of the plate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =x{|}_{0}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}{|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the moment of inertia about the perpendicular line through the center of mass $(1,2)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{M} & ={∬}_{D}((x-\\overset{¯}{x})^{2}+(y-\\overset{¯}{y})^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}((x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}((x-1)^{2}+(y-2)^{2})y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(x-1)^{2}y+{y}^{3}-4{y}^{2}+4y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)^{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{4}-\\frac{4}{3}{y}^{3}+2{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{9}{2}(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{9}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3}{2}(x-1)^{3}+\\frac{9}{4}x]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{9}{2}-(-\\frac{3}{2}+0) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the parallel axis theorem, the moment of inertia about a line $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,\\text{m}$ from the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}+{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}+\\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =12\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536104 17592186045417 true] [17592186567039 1074 "Let's examine each of our numbers in turn.\n\n- $0.1222$ is a rational number because it can be expressed as a ratio of two integers:\n\n$$\n0.1222=\\frac{1\\,222}{10\\,000}\n$$\n\n- $1.3\\overset{―}{4}$ is a rational number. The notation $\\overset{―}{4}$ tells us that the digit $4$ after the decimal point repeats indefinitely.\n\n$$\n1.3\\overset{―}{4}=1.34444444444…\n$$\n\nAny decimal with repeating digits can be represented as a ratio of two integers. In this case,\n\n$$\n1.3\\overset{―}{4}=\\frac{121}{90}.\n$$\n\n- $1.981354…$ is not a rational number. The notation \"$…$\" tells us that digits after the decimal point extend forever. However, there are no repeating digits.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186567079 1074 "The height (or altitude) corresponding to $\\overset{―}{PR}$ is a line segment that \n\n- runs from the opposite vertex $Q$ to the line $\\overset{⟷}{PR},$ and\n\n- is perpendicular to $\\overset{⟷}{PR}.$\n\nTo draw the height, we proceed as follows:\n\n- Extend the line segment $\\overset{―}{PR}.$\n\n![](images/66/664c9507be2e02908b8a151d0c58222545a84a7d71648c28e61badfb23e1981b.png)\n\n- Draw the perpendicular from $Q$ to $\\overset{⟷}{PR}.$\n\n![](images/89/89bc576f142925d0ef639e3a2ade1da2900e24f920e2469fe159b6a8eda238c7.png)" 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186567097 1074 "For a function $f(x,y),$ the double integral of $f$ over a rectangular region $R$ is the unique number $I$ (if it exists) such that\n\n$$\nL(f,P)\\le I\\le U(f,P)\n$$\n\nover all partitions $P$ of $R.$\n\nFirst, notice that ${\\overset{¯}{x}}_{i}=\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})$ will always lie between ${x}_{i-1}$ and ${x}_{i}$, and similarly ${\\overset{¯}{y}}_{j}=\\frac{1}{2}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})$ will always lie between ${y}_{j-1}$ and ${y}_{j}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n4{x}_{i-1}-2{y}_{j}-2\\le 4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})-2\\le 4{x}_{i}-2{y}_{j-1}-2\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n4{x}_{i-1}-2{y}_{j}-2\\le 2({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})-({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})-2\\le 4{x}_{i}-2{y}_{j-1}-2.\n$$\n\nMultiplying this by $Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}$ and applying the double summation gives:\n\n$$\nL(f,P)\\le {\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[2({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})-({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})-2]Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\le U(f,P)\n$$\n\nSeparating the sums using the addition and constant multiple rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & \\le 2{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\  & \\,-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\  & \\,-2{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\  & \\le U(f,P).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the hint, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} & =2({3}^{2}-{1}^{2})(1-(-1))=32 \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} & =({1}^{2}-(-1)^{2})(3-1)=0 \\\\ 2{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} & =2(3-1)(1-(-1))=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P)\\le 32-0-8\\le U(f,P)\\,⟹\\,L(f,P)\\le 24\\le U(f,P)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=24.\n$$" 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186567105 1074 "For a function $f(x,y),$ the double integral of $f$ over a rectangular region $R$ is the unique number $I$ (if it exists) such that\n\n$$\nL(f,P)\\le I\\le U(f,P)\n$$\n\nover all partitions $P$ of $R.$\n\nFirst, notice that ${\\overset{¯}{x}}_{i}=\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})$ will always lie between ${x}_{i-1}$ and ${x}_{i}$, and similarly ${\\overset{¯}{y}}_{j}=\\frac{1}{2}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})$ will always lie between ${y}_{j-1}$ and ${y}_{j}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{x}_{i-1}-{y}_{j}\\le \\frac{1}{2}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})-\\frac{1}{2}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})\\le {x}_{i}-{y}_{j-1}\n$$\n\nMultiplying this by $Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}$ and applying the double summation gives:\n\n$$\nL(f,P)\\le {\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}[\\frac{1}{2}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})-\\frac{1}{2}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})]Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}\\le U(f,P)\n$$\n\nSeparating the sums using the addition and constant multiple rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & \\le \\frac{1}{2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}-\\frac{1}{2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\  & \\le U(f,P).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the hint, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} & =\\frac{1}{2}({6}^{2}-{0}^{2})(2-1)=18 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j-1}+{y}_{j})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} & =\\frac{1}{2}({2}^{2}-{1}^{2})(6-0)=9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P)\\le 18-9\\le U(f,P)\\,⟹\\,L(f,P)\\le 9\\le U(f,P)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that \n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A=9.\n$$" 13194139536106 17592186045417 true] [17592186570766 1074 "The element shown in the diagram is the part of the circle that is enclosed between the points $B$ and $D$ on the circle and passes through the point $C$ on the circle. So, this element represents the arc $\\overset{⌢}{DCB}.$" 13194139536395 17592186045417 true] [17592186571426 1074 "![](images/3e/3e03c8d3377aef27d7542ef2d734ebd64d00bbc1539e63222b7df9abc696bd44.png)\n\nAs we can see from the picture above, in $△BAD$ we have $∠BAD={90}^{∘}$, $AB=18$ and $AD=BC=9.$ So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{\\to }{BD}| & =BD \\\\  & =\\sqrt{A{B}^{2}+A{D}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{18}^{2}+{9}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{405} \\\\  & =9\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $|\\overset{\\to }{BD}|=9\\sqrt{5}.$" 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186571436 1074 "![](images/53/537669ec91bb6fd620d64816d4ccf0e0a5c403aa8ba6e03c8fb89c8d71785d24.png)\n\nAs we can see from the picture above, in $△BAD$ we have $m∠BAD={90}^{∘},$ $AB=10,$ and $AD=BC=6\\sqrt{2}.$ So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{\\to }{BD}| & =BD \\\\  & =\\sqrt{A{B}^{2}+A{D}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{10}^{2}+(6\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{100+72} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{172} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{43}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $|\\overset{\\to }{BD}|=2\\sqrt{43}.$" 13194139536397 17592186045417 true] [17592186572125 1074 "From the figure, we note that the points $A$ and $B$ have coordinates $\\underbrace{(-1,1)}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{1})}$ and $\\underbrace{(3,-2)}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{2})},$ respectively.\n\nTo find the length of $\\overset{―}{AB},$ we use the distance formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3-(-1))^{2}+(-2-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(4)^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536958 17592186576411 true] [17592186572132 1074 "To obtain the unit vector that is parallel to $\\overset{\\to }{ST},$ we divide $\\overset{\\to }{ST}$ by its length:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{ST}}{|\\overset{\\to }{ST}|}=\\frac{1}{6}\\overset{\\to }{ST}\n$$" 13194139536398 17592186045417 true] [17592186572149 1074 "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\ne C,\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda })\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=C.\n$$\n\nSo, our rotation-scaling matrix $C$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\overset{―}{\\lambda }=2+5\\text{i},$ \n the complex conjugate of $\\lambda .$ Hence, to construct the matrix $P,$ we use the eigenvector $\\overset{―}{v},$ the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nNext, we find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{v} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1-4\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v})}+\\text{i}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v}) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v}) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536958 17592186576411 true] [17592186572182 1074 "The region shown in the diagram is bounded by the arc $\\overset{⌢}{AB}$ of the circle and the radii $\\overset{―}{OA}$ and $\\overset{―}{OB}.$ So, this region represents the circular sector $OAB.$" 13194139536398 17592186045417 true] [17592186572191 1074 "According to the triangle law of addition, the resultant is the vector that goes from the tail of $\\overset{\\to }{CD}$ to the head of $\\overset{\\to }{DC}.$ Therefore, we obtain a new vector for which the tail and head is the same point, the tail of $\\overset{\\to }{CD}.$ \n\nThat vector is called the zero vector, and we write\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{CD}+\\overset{\\to }{DC}=0.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/8e/8e51db7c2e9eff46679a185b82a477d65842d43d1dd71289f3e6bab1a7a503de.png)" 13194139536398 17592186045417 true] [17592186572196 1074 "To obtain the unit vector that is parallel to $\\overset{\\to }{UV},$ we divide $\\overset{\\to }{UV}$ by its length:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{UV}}{|\\overset{\\to }{UV}|}=\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{3})}\\overset{\\to }{UV}=3\\overset{\\to }{UV}\n$$" 13194139536398 17592186045417 true] [17592186572664 1074 "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(3i+4j+k)-(2j-k) \\\\  & =3i+2j+2k \\\\  & =⟨3,2,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(-i+3j+2k)-(2j-k) \\\\  & =-i+j+3k \\\\  & =⟨-1,1,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =d-a \\\\  & =(2i+j)-(2j-k) \\\\  & =2i-j+k \\\\  & =⟨2,-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}-2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{vmatrix}+2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(1\\cdot 1-3\\cdot (-1))-2((-1)\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 2)+2((-1)\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =3(1+3)-2(-1-6)+2(1-2) \\\\  & =12-(-14)-2 \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelpiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|24|=24.\n$$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572666 1074 "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+2b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $2b.$ So, we first draw the vector $2b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/3c/3c31e9b0d926de6a3f8c9f35bd816349d6488c8b98864d8576fdbfc395bbd92d.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $2b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=3|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=3a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =2b+3a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a+2b=3a+2b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =3\n$$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572695 1074 "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\frac{4}{3}a+\\lambda b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $\\frac{4}{3}a.$ So, we first draw the vector $\\frac{4}{3}a$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/b8/b8db5015e22e55282e180b1d81f7a2e1ca544dfe0f468dc4dedb6283bac2da5c.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $\\frac{4}{3}a.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=\\frac{1}{4}|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $b$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=\\frac{1}{4}b.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}a+\\frac{1}{4}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\frac{4}{3}a+\\lambda b=\\frac{4}{3}a+\\frac{1}{4}b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $b,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$" 13194139536428 17592186045417 true] [17592186572704 1074 "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a+2b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $2b.$ So, we first draw the vector $2b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/7d/7d388cdd1db5cfe59b447c3fc27b597fc4e42397966935592f56c1c9f4a598e6.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $2b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned