ligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{AO}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =b-a \\\\  & =(3i+2j)-(i+j) \\\\  & =2i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573255 1074 "Since $△ABC$ is an isosceles triangle with base $\\overset{―}{AB}$, we have that $\\overset{―}{AC}≅\\overset{―}{CB}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =CB \\\\ 3x-9 & =2x-1 \\\\ x & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this value into the expression for the measure of $\\overset{―}{AC}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =3x-9 \\\\  & =3(8)-9 \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573269 1074 "For a point with coordinates $({a}_{x},{a}_{y}),$ the position vector is $⟨{a}_{x},{a}_{y}⟩.$\n\nThe point $W$ has coordinates $(1.5,-2.5),$ so the position vector is\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OW}=⟨1.5,-2.5⟩.\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573283 1074 "We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OA}=a=0i+3j, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{OB}=b=4i-j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ as the difference of position vectors, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{OB}-\\overset{\\to }{OA} \\\\  & =b-a \\\\  & =(4i-j)-(0i+3j) \\\\  & =4i-4j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+16} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573284 1074 "To compute $-\\frac{1}{3}\\overset{\\to }{c},$ we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3}\\overset{\\to }{c} & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ⟨12,-18⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(-\\frac{1}{3})\\cdot 12,(-\\frac{1}{3})\\cdot (-18)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573286 1074 "We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OA}=a=2i+0j, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{OB}=b=-3i-2j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, from $△OAB$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{AO}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =b-a \\\\  & =(-3i-2j)-(2i+0j) \\\\  & =-5i-2j.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573288 1074 "From the picture, we obtain $A(0,3)$ and $C(4,0).$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OA}=a=0i+3j, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{OC}=c=4i+0j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ as the difference of position vectors, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\overset{\\to }{OC}-\\overset{\\to }{OA} \\\\  & =c-a \\\\  & =(4i)-(3j) \\\\  & =4i-3j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{\\to }{AC}| & =\\sqrt{(4)^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573291 1074 "Our vector is shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/e1/e132f8e20ffb38c70ef435a560a584b25a6509486cc148636d873a9e0e94ea52.png)\n\nRemember that in general, if a point $R$ has coordinates $({r}_{x},{r}_{y}),$ then its position vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OR}={r}_{x}i+{r}_{y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nHere, the point $R$ has coordinates $(-4,5),$ so its position vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OR} & =(-4)\\cdot i+5\\cdot j \\\\  & =-4\\,i+5\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573293 1074 "To compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\overset{\\to }{p} & =3\\cdot ⟨-1,5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\cdot (-1),\\,3\\cdot 5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,15⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573297 1074 "We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OA}=a=-2i+0j, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{OB}=b=3i+3j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ as the difference of position vectors, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{OB}-\\overset{\\to }{OA} \\\\  & =b-a \\\\  & =(3i+3j)-(-2i+0j) \\\\  & =5i+3j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{34}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573299 1074 "For a point with coordinates $({a}_{x},{a}_{y}),$ the position vector is $⟨{a}_{x},{a}_{y}⟩.$\n\nThe point $U$ has coordinates $(3,2.5),$ so the position vector is\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OU}=⟨3,2.5⟩.\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573310 1074 "To compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\overset{\\to }{u}+\\overset{\\to }{v} & =2\\cdot ⟨4,-3⟩+⟨-1,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8,-6⟩+⟨-1,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8+(-1),-6+2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8-1,-6+2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨7,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573311 1074 "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,\\overset{\\to }{U}| & =\\sqrt{{u}_{x}^{2}+{u}_{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(7)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{49+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{50} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25\\cdot 2} \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573315 1074 "To compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{m}-\\overset{\\to }{n} & =⟨-6,2⟩-⟨5,-8⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-6-5,\\,2-(-8)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-11,10⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573316 1074 "Since $B$ is the midpoint of $\\overset{―}{AD}$, we have: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BD & =\\frac{1}{2}AD \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 29 \\\\  & =14.5\\,\\text{cm}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, by the segment addition postulate, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BD & =BC+CD \\\\ 14.5 & =BC+6.5 \\\\ 14.5-6.5 & =BC \\\\ BC & =8\\,\\text{cm}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573321 1074 "We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OA}=a=-i+2j, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{OB}=b=-i+3j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, from $△OAB$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{AO}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =-\\overset{\\to }{OA}+\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & =b-a \\\\  & =(-i+3j)-(-i+2j) \\\\  & =0i+j \\\\  & =j.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573322 1074 "Our vector is shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/ec/ecc6f3682d17eb4878e43a24ada2d6989d73f34d44ce2e3adcf9e6dc201761d2.png)\n\nRemember that in general, if a point $R$ has coordinates $({r}_{x},{r}_{y}),$ then its position vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{OR}={r}_{x}i+{r}_{y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nHere, the point $R$ has coordinates $(2,4),$ so its position vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OR} & =2\\,i+4\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536444 17592186045417 true] [17592186573878 1074 "De Morgan's law for unions states that\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{S\\cup T}≡\\overset{―}{S}\\cap \\overset{―}{T}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{S\\cup T} & =\\overset{―}{S}\\cap \\overset{―}{T} \\\\  & =\\{\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{7},\\sqrt{11}\\}\\cap \\{\\sqrt{3},\\sqrt{5}\\} \\\\  & =∅.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186573905 1074 "De Morgan's law for intersections states that\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{R\\cap S}≡\\overset{―}{R}\\cup \\overset{―}{S}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{R\\cap S} & =\\overset{―}{R}\\cup \\overset{―}{S} \\\\  & =\\{{x}_{3},{x}_{5},{x}_{7},{x}_{11}\\}\\cup \\{{x}_{4},{x}_{8},{x}_{13}\\} \\\\  & =\\{{x}_{3},{x}_{4},{x}_{5},{x}_{7},{x}_{8},{x}_{11},{x}_{13}\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536456 17592186045417 true] [17592186575542 1074 "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that the function $f(x,y)=y-{x}^{3}$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨-2,-8⟩$ and $b=⟨1,0⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0)-f(-2,-8) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨1,0⟩-⟨-2,-8⟩) \\\\ (0-{1}^{3})-(-8-(-2)^{3}) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨3,8⟩ \\\\ -1 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨3,8⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨-3{x}^{2},1⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨-3{c}_{1}^{2},1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & =∇f⟨c⟩\\cdot ⟨3,8⟩ \\\\ -1 & =⟨-3{c}_{1}^{2},1⟩\\cdot ⟨3,8⟩ \\\\ -1 & =-9{c}_{1}^{2}+8 \\\\ 9{c}_{1}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {c}_{1}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {c}_{1} & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{a}}{{y}_{b}-{y}_{a}} & =\\frac{x-{x}_{a}}{{x}_{b}-{x}_{a}} \\\\ \\frac{y-(-8)}{0-(-8)} & =\\frac{x-(-2)}{1-(-2)} \\\\ \\frac{y+8}{8} & =\\frac{x+2}{3} \\\\ y+8 & =\\frac{8(x+2)}{3} \\\\ y & =\\frac{8(x-1)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & =\\frac{8({c}_{1}-1)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we proceed to find the coordinates of the points that satisfy the mean value theorem.\n\n- For ${c}_{1}=-1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{c}_{2}=\\frac{8(-1-1)}{3}=-\\frac{16}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the first point are $(-1,-\\frac{16}{3}).$\n\n- For ${c}_{1}=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{c}_{2}=\\frac{8(1-1)}{3}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the second point are $(1,0).$\n\nFinally we can conclude that the coordinates of the points that satisfy the mean value theorem are $(-1,-\\frac{16}{3})$ and $(1,0).$" 13194139536629 17592186045417 true] [17592186575543 1074 "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overset{―}{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sigma ,$ we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=5.4,$ $z=0.5,$ and $\\sigma =2.4$ into the formula and solving for $\\overset{―}{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 5.4 & =\\overset{―}{x}+0.5\\cdot 2.4 \\\\ 5.4 & =\\overset{―}{x}+1.2 \\\\ \\overset{―}{x} & =4.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536629 17592186045417 true] [17592186575544 1074 "First, we need to find the mean number of vegetarian customers for each day:\n\n$$\n\\overset{¯}{x}=\\frac{145}{7}=20.71\n$$\n\nto $2$ decimal places.\n\nThe $z$-score of an observation $x$ is defined by\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{x-\\overset{―}{x}}{\\sigma }.\n$$\n\nThe $z$-score represents how many standard deviations an observation is above or below the mean of the data set. Rewriting this in terms of $\\sigma ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\sigma =\\frac{x-\\overset{―}{x}}{z}.\n$$\n\nSo, the standard deviation $\\sigma$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sigma  & =\\frac{x-\\overset{¯}{x}}{z} \\\\  & =\\frac{15-20.71}{-1.5} \\\\  & =3.81\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nto $2$ decimal places." 13194139536629 17592186045417 true] [17592186575545 1074 "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨-1,1⟩$ and $b=⟨2,-2⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,-2)-f(-1,1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨2,-2⟩-⟨-1,1⟩) \\\\ ((-2)^{2}+{2}^{2